// ScaleCorpus — the generated ladder the scale suite measures.
//
// ONE PROGRAM PER RUNG, and each rung DOUBLES EVERY structural knob at once. Six rungs is a 32x span in
// six compiles. The alternative — a matrix, one axis per knob — would be one ladder per knob and six
// compiles each to learn the same thing, because the question is never "which knob is
// the compiler slow in" (the per-phase table answers that already) but "does the whole thing stay
// linear as a program grows", and a program grows in every direction at once. (Some shapes ride
// along that are COVERAGE, not knobs — they are fixed-size and do not double; see `shiftCoverageSource`
// and `scalarCoverageSource`.)
//
// ⚠ THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO SAY "twenty-five", AND SO DID THE HEADING OVER THE KNOB LIST. THE FILE HAD
// TWENTY-SIX when that was last read, and has twenty-seven now. A COUNT IS A DERIVED FACT, and one
// written into prose is a fact nothing can keep true — so the count is gone rather than corrected. The
// live spelling is `grep -c 'knobAt(' ` over `generateRung`, and it cannot drift because it IS the
// enumeration.
//
// ⚠ THE CORPUS NOW DECLARES ITSELF. Every run prints `printCorpusManifest` — the constructs it
// generates, the dimensions it scales, and, load-bearing, the ones it structurally CANNOT see (it
// measures allocations, bytes and the compiling thread's CPU time, never WALL time; it compiles a
// subset of Maxon, not the whole language; and no column of it can see a cost the generated programs
// never TRIGGER). The corpus was blind in four measured dimensions at once and every blind spot was
// silent; an instrument that cannot name what it cannot see keeps producing confident zeros.
//
// ARCHITECTURE.md claims compilation is linear in program size, and quotes measurements to back it —
// 1.96 -> ~1.0 for a 3,200-`if` function, 40x faster, a 19,200-block function that used to
// SIGSEGV. Every one of those numbers was measured on throwaway generated programs that were never
// committed. This file is the corpus that makes those claims falsifiable, and the two headline
// shapes are deliberately reproduced at the top rung: rung 5's `longFunction` is 3,200 `if`s, and
// its `deepBlocks` is ~19,200 blocks.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// THE TRAP, AND WHY EVERY VALUE HERE IS SEEDED FROM SOMETHING OPAQUE
// ===========================================================================================
//
// `foldConstOperands` is a real pass in this pipeline. A naively generated benchmark — the obvious
// one, a pile of arithmetic on literals — FOLDS AWAY TO NOTHING. The suite would then measure an
// empty compile at every rung, fit a perfect straight line through it, and report that the
// compiler is beautifully linear. It is the "check the instrument before the subject" failure, and
// it produces the most convincing possible wrong answer.
//
// So every value chain in this corpus starts from something the folder provably cannot see
// through, and there are exactly two such things in shv2:
//
//   1. A LOOP-CARRIED `var`. Its value at the top of the loop is a phi of its initializer and
//      whatever the body last wrote, so it is not a constant — and shv2 has no SCCP to prove
//      otherwise. `scaleSeed` is one, and it is where every rung's data originates.
//   2. A CALL RESULT. shv2 has no inliner and no interprocedural constant propagation, so a
//      function's return value is opaque at the call site no matter how trivial the callee. Every
//      generated function therefore takes its seed as a PARAMETER and derives everything from it.
//
// The runner does not take this on trust. `assertCorpusCompiled` (below) re-verifies it on every
// single ladder, and a corpus that folded flat is a BROKEN RUN — the one thing this instrument
// exits non-zero for, because it means nothing was measured at all.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// A RUNG DIRECTORY HOLDS EXACTLY THE GENERATED SOURCES, AND NOTHING ELSE
// ===========================================================================================
//
// This is not tidiness. It is the reproducibility the whole instrument rests on, and it was BROKEN.
//
// `build <dir>` LISTS that directory, and every entry it finds costs the compiler a `FilePath` and a
// `String` — measured, exactly, at +13 allocations and +459 bytes per extra file, at every rung. The
// runner used to write each rung's EXECUTABLE and its METRICS TSV into the rung's own source
// directory, and `.scale-tmp/` is never cleaned. So the first run on a fresh checkout compiled a
// ten-file directory and every run after it compiled a twelve-file one, and the two disagreed by 26
// allocations and 945 bytes per rung.
//
// The numbers were therefore "bit-for-bit reproducible" only if you never deleted your temp
// directory — which is to say they were not reproducible at all, and the committed goldens that
// gated on them would have failed on any machine that had never run the suite before. (Found by
// exactly the delta report this instrument now prints, on a run that changed no compiler code.)
//
// The outputs now live in the corpus ROOT, outside every rung (see ScaleTestRunner), and
// `pruneRungDir` guarantees the invariant holds even for a `.scale-tmp` left behind by an older
// build. ⚠ It DELETES: `<root>/rung<N>/` is owned by this generator, including under
// `--emit-corpus=<dir>`.
//
// ===========================================================================================
// NOT COMMITTED, ON PURPOSE
// ===========================================================================================
//
// The rungs are generated into `.scale-tmp/` at run time (gitignored, exactly as `.spec-tmp/` is).
// Committing them would add megabytes of generated Maxon to the tree for no benefit: generation is
// deterministic, so the corpus is a pure function of this file, and this file IS the corpus.
// `--emit-corpus=<dir>` dumps a ladder anywhere you want to look at it.
//
// THE SUBSET: shv2 implements `int` AND `float` arithmetic (including integer `/` and `mod`),
// functions (<=6 params), `if`, `while`, `break`, `return`, body-local `let`/`var`, TOP-LEVEL
// `let`/`var`, `type` declarations with field reads AND writes, structs across a call, `enum` + `match`,
// `String`, `Array`, the `__ManagedMemory` BUFFER SURFACE over one, unconstrained generics, interfaces
// with concrete dispatch, `print`, `or`/`xor`/`not`, `as` casts, bitwise + shifts (`and`/`shl`/`shr`),
// ranged typealiases, and NAMESPACES — a file's directory, the `module` visibility tier over it, and
// `dir.Name` / `dir.fn()` references at every segment count.
// No `for`, no closures, no async, no witness-table dispatch. Everything below
// stays inside it — and `printCorpusManifest` states exactly this boundary on every run, so a construct
// outside it (and the codegen change to it that would be invisible) is NAMED rather than silently absent.
//
// ⚠ THE SUBSET AND THE CORPUS ARE TWO DIFFERENT SETS, AND THE GAP BETWEEN THEM IS WHERE BUGS LIVE.
// This is the file's most durable defect, and it has now recurred SEVEN times:
//   • `float` was in the subset for a full rung before one line of this file generated any. See
//     `FloatOpsBase` for what that cost (a panicking emitter, shipped).
//   • integer `/` and `mod` were in it since P1.0d.3 — the entire `idiv` fixed-register path — and
//     every `/` the corpus emitted was a FLOAT `/`. See `IntDivideBase`.
//   • top-level `let` and `var` were in it for TWO rungs, 36 spec tests' worth. See `GlobalConstsBase`.
//   • `type` declarations were in it from P1.0r, the rung that gave shv2 a HEAP. See `StructTypesBase`.
//   • ⭐ AND THEN TEN RUNGS AT ONCE. `match`, `enum`, `String`, `Array`, generics and interfaces —
//     everything from P1.1b to P1.7a — were in the subset and the corpus generated NOT ONE of them.
//     Measured on the ladder this file shipped with: `match` 0, `enum` 0, `String` 0, `Array with` 0,
//     `type ... uses` 0, `interface` 0, `implements` 0, `print(` 0, ` or ` 0, ` xor ` 0, struct field
//     WRITE 0 — at rung 0 AND at rung 5, in 109,697 lines. So the one artifact that answers *"what has
//     this compiler's cost actually done, change by change"* was measuring the compiler roughly as it
//     stood at P1.1a, and rung after rung of PLAN.md recorded `scale allocsΔ0 … the corpus is
//     generics-free / Set-free / conformance-free` — which is not evidence of anything. Every optimizer
//     instead built a throwaway ladder of its own and discarded it. See `EnumTypesBase` onwards.
//   • ⭐ AND THE SIXTH IS THE ONE THE FIFTH'S OWN FIX WALKED PAST. `Array` moved to CONSTRUCTS GENERATED
//     with two knobs behind it, and the `__ManagedMemory` BUFFER SURFACE over that same record — a second
//     member roster on one type, eight `markBufferSurface` call sites in seven parser functions — was in
//     neither list. Measured on the ladder as it stood: `.managed` 0, `ByteArray` 0, `toByteArray` 0,
//     `addressableBytes` 0, `__ManagedMemory` 0, at rung 0 and rung 5 alike, so BATCH2 slice 6 shipped the
//     value-position mechanism against a ladder that read Δ0 and could not have read anything else. See
//     `BufferSurfaceSitesBase`. ⚠ **A FAMILY WHOSE NEIGHBOUR IS COVERED IS THE HARDEST KIND TO SEE** — the
//     same shape as `RangedReturnSitesBase`, where the cast door was priced and the `return` door was not,
//     and the manifest line above it read as covering both.
//   • ⭐ AND THE SEVENTH IS THE ONE NO KNOB COULD HAVE CLOSED, BECAUSE IT WAS THE DIRECTORY LAYOUT
//     ITSELF. Every rung's files went into ONE FLAT DIRECTORY, so `deriveModuleDirectory` answered `""`
//     for every source file in every rung and the ENTIRE namespace mechanism — N1a's `module` visibility
//     tier, N1b's qualified-alias mirror, its namespace-qualified function set, its dotted-chain
//     recognizer and its multi-segment walk — ran zero times at rung 0 and zero times at rung 5. This one
//     was NAMED BY ITS OWN IMPLEMENTER before it shipped ("the ladder is structurally blind to the USER
//     half of this rung"), which is why it is the first that cost nothing to find. See
//     `NamespacedModulesBase`.
//   • ⭐ AND THE EIGHTH IS THE ONE THAT LOOKED COVERED, WHICH IS THE HARDEST KIND. There WAS a String
//     knob — the fifth recurrence's own fix — and it generated a literal, an interpolation, `.append`,
//     `.byteLength()` and `==`. `dispatchStringMethod` answers about twenty-five members; the other
//     twenty generated NOTHING at any rung: no predicate, no case conversion, no `replace`/`split`, no
//     trim, no `StringIndex` producer or consumer, no materializer, no `isEmpty`/`clone`. Every one has
//     live spec cases, and every one is being RETIRED onto `stdlib/String.maxon`'s bodies one wave at a
//     time — so every wave would have read Δ0 in all three columns off a knob whose name says "String".
//     A knob is not coverage of the TYPE it is named for; it is coverage of the members it spells. See
//     `StringSearchOpsBase`, and note that the very first ladder run after the four knobs landed found a
//     cost nobody had priced: `CharacterSet.whitespacesAndNewlines()` is materialized INLINE at every
//     trim site, 194 ops a call, enough to take the register allocator out of memory at rung 4.
//
// The manifest named the first two as blind spots the whole time and they were read as footnotes. It
// did not name the third and fourth AT ALL. And for the fifth it did worse than either: it named the
// families under NOT GENERATED while also making two CLAIMS THAT HAD STOPPED BEING TRUE — that struct
// field writes were "P1.1a wave 2 and NOT IMPLEMENTED" and a struct crossing a call "P1.4, deferred on
// the borrow-vs-consume ruling". Both had shipped; `specs-shv2/challenge-struct-field-assign.md` and
// `struct-managed-field.md` had live tests for them. A manifest that names a gap with the WRONG reason
// is worse than one that omits it, because the reason is what a reader uses to decide whether to close
// it. See `printCorpusManifest`, which is the load-bearing half of this file rather than its
// documentation.
//
// For the SIXTH the manifest was silent in all three headings at once — `__ManagedMemory` appeared under
// neither CONSTRUCTS GENERATED nor NOT GENERATED nor the cost dimensions — which is the failure the rule
// two paragraphs up already forbids ("every construct in the subset appears under EXACTLY ONE heading")
// and which nothing checks. The list is hand-maintained; that is the price, and stating it is the only
// mitigation this file has.

// Which rung of the ladder, and how many of them. A rung DOUBLES the one below it, so these are
// exponents, not sizes: `--rungs=20` would not be a big run, it would be a program a hundred
// thousand times rung 0 that never finishes. Hence a hard, refused-not-clamped upper bound.
export typealias RungIndex = int(0 to 15)
export typealias RungCount = int(1 to 16)

export let MinRungCount = 1 as RungCount
export let MaxRungCount = 8 as RungCount
export let DefaultRungCount = 6 as RungCount

// Total bytes of generated source in one rung — the denominator of the degeneracy check.
export typealias SourceByteCount = int(0 to u32.max)

// The outcome of parsing `--rungs=<n>`. A union rather than a throw, because the caller is inside
// the argument loop and must neither diverge nor invent a value: `invalid` is recorded as a bad
// option and the driver then refuses to run at all.
export union RungCountArg
	valid(count RungCount)
	invalid
end 'RungCountArg'

// Parse `--rungs=<n>`.
//
// A rung count outside the ladder's range is REFUSED, never clamped. Each rung DOUBLES the program,
// so `--rungs=20` is not "a thorough run", it is a program a hundred thousand times rung 0 that will
// never finish — and a caller who asked for it and silently got 8 would believe they had measured
// something they had not.
//
// The digits themselves are `parseCount`'s job (ScaleTestRunner) — this only decides what a valid
// RANGE is. Two hand-rolled decimal loops in one suite would be one loop too many.
export function parseRungCount(raw String) returns RungCountArg
	let value = try parseCount(raw) otherwise 'notANumber'
		return RungCountArg.invalid
	end 'notANumber'

	if value < MinRungCount or value > MaxRungCount 'outOfRange'
		return RungCountArg.invalid
	end 'outOfRange'
	return RungCountArg.valid(value as RungCount)
end 'parseRungCount'

// The number of `if`s, files, functions... in one knob at one rung. Bounded well above the top
// rung's largest knob (rung 7's deepBlocks is 200 << 7 = 25,600).
typealias KnobSize = int(1 to 1048576)

// A POSITION within a knob, not a COUNT of one — 0-based, where `KnobSize` starts at 1. The upper
// bound is `KnobSize`'s max minus one because the generators walk `0 upto size`, and `upto` excludes
// its bound.
//
// ⚠ These are two different domains and sharing one alias was a live defect: `chainLinkName` took a
// `KnobSize` while every one of its callers passes a link INDEX, which starts at 0 —
// `chainLinkName(0)` for the chain's base and `chainLinkName(i - 1)` for each link's callee. The
// bootstrap did not enforce a ranged parameter's bounds at a call, so a value one below the declared
// minimum was passed on three lines and nothing said so; the moment the check was added the build
// stopped. A range that describes the wrong quantity reads as correct until something measures it.
typealias KnobIndex = int(0 to 1048575)

// ===========================================================================================
// The knobs, and their rung-0 sizes
// ===========================================================================================
//
// Each is `base << rung`. The bases are tuned so that rung 0 clears fixed process + stdlib
// overhead (a rung that compiles in 3 ms measures the OS, not the compiler) while the top rung stays
// tractable. They are the ONE place to retune if the ladder gets too slow or too fast.

// The realistic codebase shape: many small functions. Each is a few ops — a couple of arithmetic
// ops and a branch — which is what most real functions are.
let FunctionsBase = 16 as KnobSize

// Real source FILES, not one big one: this is what exercises `load`, `merge`, and the query spine,
// none of which a single-file corpus would touch at all. The `manyFunctions` above are distributed
// across them, so functions-per-file stays constant as both double.
let FilesBase = 4 as KnobSize

// ONE function, N sequential `if`s. The 3,200-`if` benchmark ARCHITECTURE.md quotes: at rung 5
// this is exactly 100 << 5 = 3,200.
let LongIfsBase = 100 as KnobSize

// ONE function, a huge BLOCK count. Each `if`/`else` contributes three blocks (then, else,
// continuation), so this is ~3x its knob: at rung 5, 200 << 5 = 6,400 if/elses = ~19,200 blocks —
// the shape that used to SIGSEGV, and the reason "no CFG traversal may recurse" is a rule. It is
// separate from `longFunction` because that one grows OPS in a mostly-straight line and this one
// grows the CFG.
let DeepBlocksBase = 200 as KnobSize

// N chained calls, each caller holding values live ACROSS its call — so this is the cross-call
// spill bracket knob, not merely a call-depth knob.
let CallChainBase = 8 as KnobSize

// ONE function, N pressured loops. Its base is the smallest of the six because its cost used to
// overtake everything else as the ladder climbed — TWICE, from two different sources, both now fixed.
//
// THIS KNOB IS LINEAR NOW, and the history is worth keeping because the comment here was WRONG in two
// successive ways, each naming a real bug that was then fixed elsewhere:
//
//   1. It read "KNOWN SUPERLINEAR: the splitter's driver recomputes liveness from scratch after every
//      split… regalloc:liveness grows x3.69 per doubling." True until `49b160ce2` made that driver
//      INCREMENTAL. `regalloc:liveness` reads x1.99 now.
//   2. Then the real bend was the PARSER: `parseWhileStatement` minted one header phi per mutable var
//      IN SCOPE, so a function whose `var`s accumulate paid Σk ≈ L²/2 phis — minted, carried through
//      four passes inflating every dense column sized by the block-arg id space, and then deleted by
//      `pruneDeadBlockArgs`. Fixed by `11b9bbcba`: a loop now carries a phi only for the vars its
//      extent ASSIGNS. `pruneDeadBlockArgs` reads x1.99, `elimTrivialBlockArgs` x1.97, and
//      `phase:parse` x1.99 — the tail is gone at the source, not subtracted from the reading.
//
// THE KNOB IS RIGHT TO STAY AS IT IS. A function with many loops and many mutable vars in scope is
// ordinary code, and this knob held the front end honest until the front end earned it: it is what
// made the O(V×B) SSA-construction cost visible, and it is what will catch the next thing that inflates
// the block-arg id space. What it must NOT carry is a claim about which pass is slow — twice that claim
// outlived the bug it named. If this knob bends again, MEASURE which phase, do not read this comment.
let PressuredLoopsBase = 2 as KnobSize

// ⭐⭐ ONE function, N straight-line groups, each one the shape that makes the **COLORER** — not the
// splitter — run out of registers, so that `SplitDriver.repairAtExhaustion` runs on this ladder at all.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE THE LADDER READ Δ0 FOR THE WHOLE OF THE A5g CURE, AND THAT Δ0 WAS THE
// INSTRUMENT'S BLIND SPOT RATHER THAN THE COST.** No other knob reaches the colorer's exhaustion path at
// any rung, so every column — allocations, bytes AND cpu — would have reported "nothing moved" about a
// mechanism that never ran. That is the failure this file's `floatSpill` knob was caught in at four
// (a count too small to force a single spill), stated once more in a new file rather than re-learned.
//
// THE SHAPE, and every part of it is load-bearing — `specs-shv2/register-pressure.md`'s
// `confined-reuse-defs-*` cases are the same program written by hand:
//
//  • `confinedUnconstrainedInts()` values that cross NO call, derived to be **x64's** whole GPR pool.
//    That count is a THRESHOLD, not a slope, and it is the decisive one: MEASURED, x64 reproduces at 14
//    and not at 12. They fill the machine, which is what forces the confined values below into the
//    callee-saved half.
//
//    ⚠⚠ **THE DERIVATION IS x64's, AND THIS KNOB IS THEREFORE BLIND ON AN arm64 HOST — SAID PLAINLY
//    RATHER THAN LEFT FOR A READER TO INFER FROM A Δ0.** The corpus is a SOURCE GENERATOR with no target
//    in hand: `x64AllocatablePool` / `calleeSavedMask` are the only pools reachable from here, which is
//    the same limitation `floatsLivePerSpillLoop` has and states. arm64's own threshold is RECORDED, not
//    generated — 26 reproduces and 24 does not, against its 26-register pool — so on an arm64 host this
//    knob emits 14/5, sits below the threshold, and fires ZERO repairs. Closing that needs the host
//    target threaded into the generator, which is more than this knob; until then, a Δ0 in the repair
//    columns on an arm64 ladder is THIS, not a cost.
//  • `confinedCrossCallDefs()` products of them, each live ACROSS the second call and so confined to
//    that same callee-saved half — which the values above have already taken. Hall's exact verdict at
//    that point is FEASIBLE; the greedy simply cannot get there, and the repair splits one.
//  • the SUM, which is what keeps every unconstrained value live right up to the call.
//
// Groups use FRESH NAMES, so their live ranges do not overlap and peak pressure stays at ONE group's
// worth however many groups there are — `pressuredLoops`' rule. So repairs are Θ(groups) against a
// function of size Θ(groups), which is exactly the reading this ladder is for: ×2 per doubling is the
// BATCHED repair loop, ×4 would be a per-repair re-colour.
let ConfinedDefsBase = 2 as KnobSize

// ONE function, ONE chain of loops NESTED to depth N — distinct from `pressuredLoops`, which grows
// loop COUNT at depth 1. Depth is what the loop-phi token pre-scan climbs: `parseWhileStatement`
// re-derives each loop's extent from the tokens (`blockStatementEndIndex`) and reads its carried set off
// them (`assignedBindingsIn`), and a loop NESTED inside another has its tokens re-scanned once per
// enclosing loop. So D nested loops make the scan O(depth x tokens) — quadratic in TIME. ⚠ **STILL TRUE
// AFTER 2026-07-28**, which removed a DIFFERENT quadratic from the same function (the O(vars in scope)
// filter) and left the token walk exactly as it was; `gennest.sh` is the ladder that isolates this one.
//
// ⚠ IT WILL READ ~LINEAR IN THE ALLOCATION COLUMN ANYWAY, AND THAT IS A LIMIT OF THAT COLUMN RATHER
// THAN OF THIS KNOB. The scan allocates one small position list per loop and mints one phi per
// carried var per loop — both LINEAR in depth — so allocations and bytes cannot express the scan's
// cost however quadratic in TIME it is. THE CPU COLUMN IS WHERE IT SHOULD NOW SHOW: per-phase CPU
// ticks measure the compiling thread's own time, so a `phase:parse` that bends past x2 per doubling
// on this ladder while its allocations hold at x2 is this scan, made visible. It stays the worked
// example in `printCorpusManifest` — of what ONE column can see and another structurally cannot,
// rather than of what the instrument cannot see at all. The knob is also still what will catch the
// day the scan starts ALLOCATING per token.
//
// Its base is the smallest of any knob: the loops all carry the SINGLE accumulator `acc`, so peak
// register pressure is one value at ANY depth (an E5001-safe deep nest — a per-level counter would be
// live across every enclosing loop and trip E5001 at depth ~14). Base 2 reaches depth 64 at rung 5 —
// realistic in shape (real code nests loops to depth 3), super-realistic in magnitude, exactly as
// `longFunction`'s 3,200 ifs are.
let LoopNestBase = 2 as KnobSize

// ONE function, N float ARITHMETIC/COMPARE groups — the float twin of `longFunction`.
//
// ⚠ THE CORPUS GENERATED NO FLOAT AT ALL UNTIL 2026-07-15, AND THAT IS THE FIFTH CONSECUTIVE RUNG
// THIS LADDER WAS BLIND TO WHAT LANDED. P1.0d.4 built an entire SSE backend — a second register
// FILE, a second pool, a conversion band, a NaN-correct compare lowering — and every float number
// this instrument printed was byte-identical BY CONSTRUCTION, because nothing in the ladder emitted
// one float instruction. The manifest even SAID so, in plain text, under NOT GENERATED. It was read
// five times as a footnote about the reader's own measurement instead of a defect in the tool, and
// the corpus finds only bugs someone already thought to generate.
//
// WHAT IT COST: `float-phi-*` in specs-shv2/float-type.md. A float `var` mutated in a branch or a
// loop — `var f = 0.0` — PANICKED the x64 emitter, because `Parser.mintPhi` hardcoded every phi's
// `argType` to `i64` and P1.0d.4 turned that field into the phi's REGISTER FILE. The commonest float
// idiom there is, and both the suite and this ladder were blind to it. This knob generates float
// phis by construction, so that class of bug cannot hide here again.
let FloatOpsBase = 64 as KnobSize

// ONE function, N loops, each holding `floatsLivePerSpillLoop()` floats live ACROSS a call — the most
// expensive thing a float does on this target, and a shape no GPR knob can stand in for.
//
// ⚠ WHAT THIS KNOB MEASURES CHANGED AT WAVE 2. Under Wave 1 every XMM was caller-saved, so a float
// live across a call had an allowed set of EXACTLY ∅ and MUST go to memory — a store/reload bracket
// at every call, which is what made `regalloc:splitting` superlinear in the number of cross-call
// floats. Wave 2 gave the XMM file a callee-saved half (xmm6-15, `calleeSavedMask`), so cross-call
// floats now live in registers exactly as `callChain`'s GPRs do, and this knob spills only the
// OVERFLOW past that half — which is why `floatsLivePerSpillLoop()` stopped being the literal 4 and
// became the half's width plus headroom, DERIVED from `calleeSavedMask` so the next move of that
// line retunes it instead of blinding it.
// It is forced spilling rather than E5001: the amended Rule 2 permits a spill whose placement is
// forced by a call bracket.
let FloatSpillLoopsBase = 2 as KnobSize

// The bounded set of float accumulators `scaleFloats` mutates — four, against an XMM file of
// `RegisterFileSize - FirstXmmRegisterNumber`. Small on purpose: the straight-line
// arithmetic must never spill, so this knob measures float ARITHMETIC and cannot quietly become a
// second spill knob. That shape is `scaleFloatSpill`'s job, and holding the two apart is the same
// separation `WorkingLocalsPerFunction` keeps against `pressuredLoops`.
let WorkingFloatsPerFunction = 4 as KnobSize

// THE FLOAT-SPILL KNOB'S WIDTH — spelled below as a HEADROOM constant plus a DERIVED half-width,
// which is why this block covers two declarations. Each spill loop holds this many floats live
// across its call: enough to OVERFLOW the callee-saved XMM half, because that half is now where a
// cross-call float lives.
//
// ⚠⚠ IT NEEDS A HEADROOM CALCULATION AGAIN, AND WAVE 2 IS WHY. It was 4, under a comment saying it
// "needs no headroom calculation because the XMM file's answer for a value live across a call is ∅
// at any pool size" — true while every XMM was caller-saved, and FALSE the moment xmm6–15 became
// callee-saved: four floats then fit comfortably and the knob generated a loop that spills NOTHING,
// measuring the force-spill path by not exercising it. That is the failure this corpus was rebuilt
// to end (see the `pressuredLoops` note): an instrument that cannot express the feature reports Δ0
// and the Δ0 reads as evidence.
//
// So it is now the float twin of `PressuredValuesPerLoop` in the way that matters: the width of the
// callee-saved XMM half is what the set live across the call must EXCEED for a bracket to be forced.
//
// ⭐⭐ AND THAT WIDTH IS **DERIVED FROM THE ALLOCATOR'S OWN MASKS**, NOT WRITTEN DOWN HERE AS A TEN.
// Raising the count to 12 fixes today's number and leaves the trap described above fully ARMED for
// the next change to `calleeSavedMask` — and a knob that has gone blind that way once will go blind
// that way again. Reading the width out of the mask instead makes that change RETUNE THE KNOB WITH
// IT, which is the difference between a number that is right today and one that stays right.
//
// ⚠ THE SET LIVE ACROSS THE CALL IS THIS KNOB PLUS ONE — `total` is a float accumulator live across
// every loop's call too — so the first forced bracket appears when this knob reaches the half's full
// width, not one past it. MEASURED, rather than reasoned, on a 64-loop ladder of exactly this shape
// (`regalloc:splitting` allocations, `--metrics`, this compiler): 4 → 784, 6 → 784, 8 → 2,064,
// 9 → 2,714, 10 → 31,364, 11 → 57,644, 12 → 87,418. 784 is the per-function floor, so the knob was
// INERT at 4 and at 6, barely engaged at 8–9, and forcing brackets from 10 up — which is the half's
// width, exactly as the derivation says.
//
// ⚠ THE HEADROOM IS **NOT** "the same slack the GPR knob keeps over its five", which this comment
// claimed until it was checked: `PressuredValuesPerLoop` is 8 against 5, a slack of THREE. The two
// knobs have different pools AND different accumulator counts, so their knees are in different
// places and neither slack derives from the other. Re-derive this one from the sweep above, never by
// copying the GPR figure.
let FloatSpillHeadroom = 2 as KnobSize

// How many floats each spill loop holds live across its call: the callee-saved XMM half's width plus
// `FloatSpillHeadroom`. A FUNCTION rather than a `let` only because a global initializer may not call
// one (E2045) — the value is a constant of the target, decided here and read at every use.
function floatsLivePerSpillLoop() returns KnobSize
	let calleeSavedXmms = regMaskPopcount(calleeSavedMask() and classRegisterMask(RegClass.xmm, file: x64RegisterFile()))
	return (calleeSavedXmms as KnobSize) + FloatSpillHeadroom
end 'floatsLivePerSpillLoop'

// THE `confinedDefs` KNOB'S TWO WIDTHS, both DERIVED from the allocator's own x64 masks for the reason
// `floatsLivePerSpillLoop` records paying for: a number that is right today and written down here goes
// stale silently, and a knob that has gone blind that way once will go blind that way again. What the
// derivation does NOT do is follow the HOST — see `ConfinedDefsBase`, which states the consequence.
//
// The unconstrained count is the WHOLE GPR pool — the threshold at which the machine is full at the
// moment the confined values are born.
function confinedUnconstrainedInts() returns KnobSize
	return classPoolSizeOf(x64AllocatablePool(), class: RegClass.gpr, file: x64RegisterFile()) as KnobSize
end 'confinedUnconstrainedInts'

// The call-crossing count is the width of the CALLEE-SAVED GPR half — the confined set is exactly as
// wide as the only home it is allowed, which is the competition the repair exists to settle. It is
// necessarily smaller than `confinedUnconstrainedInts()` (the pool contains its own callee-saved half),
// which the generator relies on: each product reads one of the unconstrained values.
function confinedCrossCallDefs() returns KnobSize
	return regMaskPopcount(calleeSavedMask() and classRegisterMask(RegClass.gpr, file: x64RegisterFile())) as KnobSize
end 'confinedCrossCallDefs'

// ONE file, N top-level `let` constants — the constant ARENA, the initializer DFS and its memo.
//
// ⚠ TWO WHOLE RUNGS WERE INVISIBLE HERE, AND THE MANIFEST DID NOT MERELY MISS THEM — IT REPORTED THEM
// AS COVERED. P1.0d.5a landed top-level `let` (the suite went 281 -> 295) and P1.0d.5b landed
// top-level `var` (295 -> 317). This corpus generated NEITHER, at any rung. The float case at least
// printed "no float" in plain text under NOT GENERATED for five rungs, and was read as a footnote
// about the reader's own measurement. This one was worse: the manifest listed `let / var` under
// CONSTRUCTS GENERATED — meaning the BODY-LOCAL kind, the only kind the corpus had — and named the
// top-level kind under NEITHER heading. A gap nobody is told about reads as coverage; a gap filed
// under the wrong heading reads as coverage and cannot be caught by reading the manifest at all.
//
// ⚠ BASE >= 2. The two seed constants are literals and every other derives from the two below it (see
// `globalConstsSource`), so a base below two would emit more constants than the knob claims to.
let GlobalConstsBase = 32 as KnobSize

// Keeps the constant DAG's values bounded — see `globalConstsSource`. The DAG is Fibonacci-shaped, so
// without a modulus its values would leave i64 many times over by the top rung's thousandth term. Any
// modulus whose successors stay well inside i64 would do; a thousand keeps the emitted literals short.
let GlobalConstModulus = 1000

// ONE file, N top-level `var`s — `.data` slots, `GlobalDataTable.layOut`, and a
// `globalAddr`/`loadIndirect`/`storeIndirect` triple per access.
//
// ⚠ NOT `globalConsts` WITH A DIFFERENT KEYWORD. The two share a syntax and NOTHING else, which is
// exactly the trap that let both go unmeasured behind one manifest line. A `let` is folded into its
// uses and has NO BACKEND AT ALL; a `var` is storage that survives to the linker, lays out a section,
// and loads through an impure op. A change to the data-section layout moves this knob and cannot move
// that one. Two knobs that a future change must be free to move independently are not one knob — the
// rule `floatSpill` states against `pressuredLoops`.
let GlobalVarsBase = 32 as KnobSize

// The value each global `var` is seeded with, cycled so the `.data` image is not one repeated byte
// pattern. A top-level `var`'s initializer MUST be a constant expression (E2045 otherwise), so this is
// a literal and CANNOT be seeded from `scaleSeed()` — and it does not need to be. See
// `globalVarsSource` for why a global is fold-resistant for free.
let GlobalVarSeedModulus = 7

// ONE file, N `type` declarations — the PER-TYPE cost: the type table and the layout descriptor.
//
// ⚠ P1.0r GAVE SHV2 A HEAP AND THIS LADDER GENERATED NO `type` AT ALL. The tell was printed on every
// run and read six times as a flat line: `phase:runtimeAugment` went 54 -> 58 across the WHOLE
// ladder. That was never a runtime that scaled beautifully — it was a runtime that was never
// INSTALLED, because nothing in the corpus allocated.
//
// ⚠ AND THAT PHASE STILL WILL NOT MOVE WITH THIS KNOB — it is fixed-cost BY CONSTRUCTION, so do not
// read it as this knob's report card. `phase:runtimeAugment` times `augmentWithRuntime` only, which is
// `prependEntryStub`: ONE hand-built entry stub. The heap runtime proper (`installMmRuntime`, five
// fixed functions) is called at Compiler.maxon:180, OUTSIDE every `PhaseProbe`, so it is billed to no
// phase at all. What DOES prove the runtime is installed is the leak gate: a struct in a block exits
// 101, which is unreachable unless `__mm_leak_check` was linked in. This knob's real cost lands in
// `lowerMaxonToStd`, `isel`, `regalloc` and `encode`, where the work actually is.
let StructTypesBase = 8 as KnobSize

// ONE file, M `create` sites over a BOUNDED set of types — the PER-SITE cost: an `__mm_alloc` call, a
// field store per field, and the release the value's death forces.
//
// ⚠ NOT `structTypes` WITH A BIGGER NUMBER. One knob grows how many types the compiler must LAY OUT;
// this one grows how many times a type is BUILT. P1.1a wave 2 (field writes) moves this one and not
// that one; a change to the layout descriptor moves that one and not this one. Same rule as
// `globalVars` above, and as `floatSpill` against `pressuredLoops`.
let StructAllocsBase = 32 as KnobSize

// The bounded set of types `scaleStructAllocs` rotates over. CONSTANT on purpose: were the alloc knob
// to grow its own types it would BE `structTypes`, the two would move together for ever, and the
// separation defended above would exist only in the comments.
let AllocStructTypeCount = 4 as KnobSize

// ONE file, N `/` and `mod` groups — `StdOp.div`, and the x64 `idiv` path whose RAX/RDX pinning is the
// only HARD fixed-physical-register constraint in the language.
//
// ⚠ P1.0d.3 BUILT THAT WHOLE PATH — `cqo`, and the implicit-def masks that keep the divisor and every
// value live across the `idiv` out of RAX and RDX — and the corpus measured none of it. It was not
// even an obvious zero: the corpus was FULL of `/`, and every one of them was a FLOAT `/`, which is a
// different Std op entirely (`binOp(div)` — no fixed registers, no trap, a different register file).
// Counting the operator would have said "covered"; only counting its TYPE says otherwise.
let IntDivideBase = 32 as KnobSize

// ⭐ **THE CHECKED-DIVIDE knob (A1) — the OTHER divide shape the language now has.** A divisor the
// compiler cannot prove non-zero makes `/` and `mod` THROWING, and the shape that lowers to is
// genuinely different from the bare one: three extra ops per site (`cmp`/`setcc`/`or`) plus the `try`
// fork's THREE BLOCKS, which every block-walking pass downstream then pays for.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS ITS OWN KNOB RATHER THAN A SECOND HALF OF `IntDivideBase`, and that is the corpus's own
// rule** — the one `FloatOpsBase`/`FloatSpillLoopsBase` are separated by: "over a FIXED set of types,
// so the two move independently". Folding it into the divide knob would make one row the sum of two
// exponents, and a bend in it unattributable. Half the divide base, because each group here costs
// three blocks where a bare one costs none, and the ladder DOUBLES both.
let CheckedDivideBase = 16 as KnobSize

// Masks the opaque seed into a divisor whose value is in 1..8: `(acc and DivisorMask) + 1`.
//
// BOTH HALVES ARE LOAD-BEARING. A CONSTANT divisor folds to a shift and measures no `idiv` at all, so
// it must derive from the seed. And it must not reach ZERO — which since BATCH10 is not a matter of
// taste: the rungs are RUN, so a `#DE` here would crash the run rather than pass unnoticed. It was
// worth excluding even when nothing observed it, and now something does. A
// corpus is a claim about the shape of real code, and real code does not divide by zero. Being
// non-negative also keeps it clear of `i64.min / -1`, the `idiv` path's other trap.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE MASK IS NO LONGER SUFFICIENT BY ITSELF (A1).** `(acc and 7) + 1` is in 1..8 to a READER, and
// to the compiler it is an ordinary runtime value — so after division became fallible, every group in
// this knob was **E3057** and the corpus stopped generating. The value being non-zero was never the
// question; the compiler being able to SEE it non-zero is, and the language's answer to that is a
// declared range. Hence `ScaleDivisorAlias` below: the mask keeps the number in the range, the CAST is
// what tells the compiler, and together they keep this knob measuring the BARE `idiv` it exists for.
let DivisorMask = 7

// The divisor both divide knobs actually get, once the accumulator is pinned: `(seed and DivisorMask) + 1`,
// which is the very expression the two emitters write. Stated here so the quotient and the remainder they
// are witnessed against come from ONE reading of that mask rather than one apiece.
//
// Its type states what the mask guarantees — `+ 1` over a masked value cannot be zero — which is also
// what lets it be a divisor in this file at all (E3057).
//
// ⚠ A RANGE BOUND MUST BE A LITERAL (E2010), so the upper bound cannot be written `DivisorMask + 1` the
// way the EMITTED alias one screen down is. The cast in `corpusDivisor` is what keeps the two honest: a
// mask that stopped admitting this range would panic on it rather than silently widen.
typealias CorpusDivisorValue = int(1 to 8)

function corpusDivisor() returns CorpusDivisorValue
	return ((corpusSeedValue() and DivisorMask) + 1) as CorpusDivisorValue
end 'corpusDivisor'

// The declared range the masked divisor is cast into — the language's escape hatch from a checked
// divide (`specs-shv2/division.md`'s `ranged-divisor-is-a-bare-idiv`). `1 to 8` is exactly what
// `DivisorMask` admits, so the guard the cast records can never fire; and an upper bound of 8 rather
// than `i64.max` is deliberate — it keeps the range HONEST about what the mask produces, at the cost of
// one extra `cmp` per group that the ladder measures along with everything else.
let ScaleDivisorAlias = "ScaleDivisor"

// ⭐ **THE RANGED-`return` KNOB (A2a): ONE function, N `return` sites guarded against a NARROWED alias.**
//
// ⚠ **THE CORPUS REACHED EVERY GUARD DOOR BUT THIS ONE**, and the precision matters, because the two
// nearest write-ups of it were BOTH WRONG in the direction that reads as less coverage than there is.
// `Testing/ladders/genrangesites.sh` said "the rung0 and rung5 binaries each contain exactly ONE
// range-check panic blob" and the ladders README repeated it; MEASURED on the corpus as it stood the day
// this knob was written, the two binaries held **33 and 1,025** — 32 and 1,024 of them `ScaleDivisor`,
// the A1 divide knob's `((acc and DivisorMask) + 1) as ScaleDivisor`, which is an ALIAS-NAMED cast site
// that DOUBLES with `IntDivideBase`, plus the one fixed `CoverageNarrowAlias` cast. So the cast door,
// `guardSiteAt` and the guard CHAIN have been priced here since A1. (Both write-ups are corrected; the
// count is now stated there as the COMMAND that re-derives it: `grep -c "Range check failed"`.)
//
// What was absent is the OTHER door. Not one generated function DECLARED a narrowed RETURN type — the
// aliases that reach a `return` (`ScaleXHoldValue`, `ScaleMeasure<N>`, `ScaleElement` and the rest) are
// all `int(i64.min to i64.max)`, which `rangeIsFull` discards before a site can be recorded, and the
// per-instance `GenericInstanceElemPrefix` aliases are only ever generic TYPE ARGUMENTS, erased before
// any door sees them. So `InsertRangeChecks.processReturnSites` — and `retBlockOf` and
// `splitBlockInPlace` behind it — ran exactly ZERO times at every rung of a 32x ladder, which is the
// `FloatSpillHeadroom` failure again in its hardest form: not a family nobody thought of, but one whose
// NEIGHBOUR was covered, so the manifest line above it read as covering both.
//
// ⚠ **AND THE COST THAT HID THERE WAS REAL.** A1h found `findRetBlock` walking every `func.blockRefs`
// entry once per site, over a set each guard it emits GROWS by three: MEASURED on the hand ladder
// (`Testing/ladders/genretsites.sh`, n = 50/100/200/400) at **x2.21 x2.53 x3.20 CPU per doubling** — a
// RISING ratio — while allocations and bytes read a dead-flat x1.99, because a scan allocates nothing.
// The cure (resolve the site's own block through the existing `guardChainTail` index) reads x1.96 x1.94
// x2.03. Both numbers live in a script nothing re-runs, so nothing re-checks that the cure holds; this
// knob is what puts that axis under the standing instrument.
//
// ⚠ **ONE FUNCTION RATHER THAN N, BECAUSE THE CONCENTRATION *IS* THE AXIS.** The term A1h found is
// sites x blocks-in-this-function, so it is invisible to a shape that spreads one site per function
// however many sites there are. `genretsites.sh`'s `spread` mode is the CONTROL that separates the
// product term from the per-site cost, and it stays there: a control answers a question that is only
// asked once a bend has appeared, and paying for it at every rung of every run would buy a second
// column for a question nobody has yet.
//
// The base is HALF the checked-divide knob, by that knob's own rule. A checked divide costs three
// blocks per group; a guarded ranged `return` costs FIVE — the `if`'s then-block and continuation, plus
// the guard's `__rc_ok`, `__rc_panic` and the one `__rc_chk` a two-bound cascade threads between them —
// and the ladder doubles all of them.
let RangedReturnSitesBase = 8 as KnobSize

// How many SCOPE-FILLER locals a long generated function carries — the V DIMENSION, and it DOUBLES.
//
// ⚠ THIS DOUBLES BECAUSE THE CORPUS WAS BLIND, AND IT WAS BLIND IN THE ONE DIMENSION IT NAMED ITSELF
// FOR. The parser builds SSA on the fly at a cost O(vars-in-scope) per branch — and until each was
// fixed, that cost was in BYTES: every `ParseExpression` snapshotted the variable registry (fixed with
// the ternary work), and every `if` snapshotted the whole mutable-var set (fixed by 8e10ce333). Both
// hid because `longFunction` and `deepBlocks` carried a FIXED handful of locals, so V never grew and no
// O(V·anything) cost could surface. **A fixed V is a V dimension the ladder cannot climb.** The comment
// that used to sit here defended the constant as "realistic, and that is the whole point" — and a review
// DISPROVED exactly that reasoning: real IS the shape, but the MAGNITUDE must be scaled past real, or
// the instrument sees nothing. `longFunction` already does this with 3,200 `if`s; this does it with locals.
//
// So V doubles, and the base is picked so the TOP rung reaches ~4,096 locals — into the thousands,
// which is where the deleted `ParseExpression` snapshot's O(V) cost actually bit (~4,000). Real
// functions top out near ~30 locals (measured across maxon-shv2 + stdlib: p99 = 16, max = 44), so
// this is deliberately super-realistic in magnitude and realistic in shape.
//
// WHAT THE LADDER NOW SEES (measured on current `main`, both V and the ifs doubling) — and this knob is
// now a REGRESSION GUARD on two fixes it first exposed:
//   • `phase:parse` ALLOCATIONS grow x2.00 — LINEAR. The `ParseExpression` HashSet snapshot that went
//     O(V) per call is GONE (deleted with the ternary short-circuit); its being linear confirms that.
//   • `phase:parse` BYTES grow ~x2.0 — LINEAR too, since 8e10ce333. This knob EXPOSED an O(V·ifs) BYTE
//     quadratic here (parse bytes read x3.9): each `if` snapshotted the whole mutable-var set — O(V) per
//     branch × O(ifs) = O(V·ifs) — invisible until V doubled. FIXED the way loops were (11b9bbcba): an
//     `if` now carries only the vars its token extent ASSIGNS, so the snapshot is O(assigned) and parse
//     bytes read x2.03.
//   • ⭐ `phase:parse` CPU grew x2.02 x2.21 x2.52 x2.90 x3.33 — the SAME O(V·ifs) shape one column
//     over, and the last place it was hiding. `assignedBindingsIn` filtered the WHOLE `mutableVars`
//     list through the assigned-name set, so it ran a hash probe per binding in scope per construct:
//     39,400,068 of them at rung 5, x3.99 per doubling, ALLOCATING NOTHING and therefore invisible to
//     the two columns above by construction. FIXED 2026-07-28 by inverting the lookup (a name -> position
//     index on the parser); the ratios now read x1.89 x1.92 x2.00 x2.06 x1.98. This knob is the guard
//     on all three: a future O(V)-per-branch cost shows up here in whichever column can see it.
let LocalsPerFunctionBase = 128 as KnobSize

// The BOUNDED set of accumulators the generated branches actually MUTATE — the "assigned" set, held
// apart from the "in-scope" set above so the two dimensions stay distinct.
//
// It is a small constant on purpose: values live across the branches must fit the 14-GPR pool, so the
// knob measures the PARSER (O(vars in scope)) and never accidentally becomes a second `pressuredLoops`
// by spilling. The V scope-fillers each fold into one of these the instant they are declared (see
// `fillerLocalsDecl`), so a filler's live range is length ~1 and the machine only ever holds these
// six — while all V+6 remain IN SCOPE for every branch's snapshot. That gap between "in scope" (V+6)
// and "live" (6) is the whole trick that lets V climb into the thousands without tripping E5001.
let WorkingLocalsPerFunction = 6 as KnobSize

// One tracked working accumulator, by slot. The two halves of the tracker are a pair — a read that
// cannot answer for a slot nobody declared, and a write that cannot address one — so both refuse loudly
// rather than inventing a value the witness would then be stated against.
function trackedAt(tracked TrackedValueArray, slot KnobIndex) returns TrackedValue
	return try tracked.get(slot) otherwise panic("ScaleCorpus: working-local slot {slot} was read before it was declared — the tracker and the emitted prelude have gone out of step")
end 'trackedAt'

function trackedSet(tracked TrackedValueArray, slot KnobIndex, value TrackedValue)
	try tracked.set(slot, value: value) otherwise panic("ScaleCorpus: working-local slot {slot} was written before it was declared — the tracker and the emitted prelude have gone out of step")
end 'trackedSet'

// Declare the bounded working accumulators — the set the generated branches MUTATE — each seeded from
// the parameter `a` so the folder cannot see through them and fold the function away.
//
// ⭐ IT TRACKS WHAT IT WRITES, IN THE LOOP THAT WRITES IT. `tracked` leaves holding the value each
// declared `w{n}` actually has, and every later emitter that touches a `w` updates it the same way, so
// the closed form the driver is finally witnessed against is derived by ONE pass over ONE model. The
// alternative — a separate function that re-derives the same values afterwards — is the second model
// this file is organised against, and it would be free to disagree.
function workingLocalsDecl(tracked TrackedValueArray) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	for w in 0 upto WorkingLocalsPerFunction 'decl'
		out.append("\tvar w{w} = a + {w + 1}\n")
		tracked.push(corpusSeedValue() + w + 1)
	end 'decl'
	return out.build()
end 'workingLocalsDecl'

// Declare `localCount` SCOPE-FILLER locals — the V dimension. Each is folded into a working
// accumulator the instant it is declared, which is load-bearing in two independent ways:
//
//   IN SCOPE, NOT LIVE. Once declared, a filler stays in the parser's mutable-var set until the
//     function ends, so every later branch snapshots it — that is the O(V) parser cost this knob
//     exists to grow. But its VALUE is dead after the fold one line down, so the backend holds it for
//     a single instruction and never spills it. Declaring all V at the top and reading them at the END
//     instead (the old `sum`) makes all V LIVE at once and trips E5001 at V > ~13 — which is why that
//     shape could never have carried a doubling V at all.
//   NOT DEAD. The fold USES the filler, so no dead-code pass may drop it and quietly take the V
//     dimension away again.
function fillerLocalsDecl(localCount KnobSize, tracked TrackedValueArray) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	for v in 0 upto localCount 'fill'
		let slot = v mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		// ⚠ **DECLARED EMPTY AND ASSIGNED, RATHER THAN INITIALIZED — because a `var` that is never
		// reassigned is now E3077**, and this filler MUST stay a `var` or the whole V dimension above
		// evaporates (a `let` leaves the parser's mutable-var set, which is the O(V) cost this knob exists
		// to grow). The assignment is the cheapest spelling that keeps it mutable AND legal; the VALUE is
		// unchanged, so `tracked` below still predicts the same witness. It costs one extra statement per
		// filler, which shifts this corpus's absolute counts — rows logged before 2026-08-10 are against
		// the one-statement shape and are not comparable term for term.
		out.append("\tvar l{v} = 0\n")
		out.append("\tl{v} = a + {v + 1}\n")
		out.append("\tw{slot} = w{slot} + l{v}\n")
		trackedSet(tracked, slot: slot, value: trackedAt(tracked, slot: slot) + corpusSeedValue() + v + 1)
	end 'fill'
	return out.build()
end 'fillerLocalsDecl'

// The full local prelude a scaling function opens with: the bounded working set the branches mutate,
// then `localCount` scope-fillers. Shared by `longFunction` and `deepBlocks` so the two measure the
// SAME V dimension against different CFG shapes rather than each spelling the prelude out.
function scopedLocalsDecl(localCount KnobSize, tracked TrackedValueArray) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append(workingLocalsDecl(tracked))
	out.append(fillerLocalsDecl(localCount, tracked: tracked))
	return out.build()
end 'scopedLocalsDecl'

// The shared epilogue: sum the bounded working accumulators and WITNESS the total. Only these six are
// read here, so peak pressure at the end stays at six — the fillers are long dead and cost nothing.
// It emits the driver's whole tail, so `longFunction` and `deepBlocks` share the epilogue and the
// witness rather than each spelling either out.
//
// The expectation is read straight off `tracked`, which the prelude and the branch loop above have been
// updating as they emitted — so it is the sum of the values the generated `w`s actually hold, and it
// costs nothing to derive.
// ⭐ EACH ACCUMULATOR IS PROBED SEPARATELY, AND A SUM IS NEVER USED TO CHECK ITS OWN ADDENDS. Six values
// summed into one comparison is six chances for two errors to cancel — the same hole the monotone counter
// closes between SITES, one level down. The tracker already holds each `w` individually, so probing them
// apart costs `WorkingLocalsPerFunction` instructions, a CONSTANT, against a knob whose branch count
// doubles. The `sum` is still built and still checked: it is the epilogue's measured shape.
function workingLocalsSumAndWitness(driverName String, tracked TrackedValueArray) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\tvar sum = a\n")
	var expected = corpusSeedValue()
	for w in 0 upto WorkingLocalsPerFunction 'sum'
		out.append("\tsum = sum + w{w}\n")
		out.append(witnessProbe("\t", valueExpr: "w{w}", expected: trackedAt(tracked, slot: w)))
		expected = expected + trackedAt(tracked, slot: w)
	end 'sum'
	out.append(driverWitnessTail(driverName, valueExpr: "sum", expected: expected))
	return out.build()
end 'workingLocalsSumAndWitness'

// A nesting-indent depth: bounded well above `LoopNestBase << MaxRungCount` plus the couple of levels
// the generator adds, so `d + 2` for the deepest loop stays in range.
typealias TabCount = int(0 to 2097152)

// `count` tab characters — the cosmetic indentation for the nested-loop knob. Maxon blocks are
// delimited by `end 'label'`, not by indentation, so this is purely for a human reading a dumped
// corpus; the parser's loop-phi scan counts `while`/`end` TOKENS and never looks at it.
function tabs(count TabCount) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	var n = count
	while n > 0 'each'
		out.append("\t")
		n = n - 1
	end 'each'
	return out.build()
end 'tabs'

// How many accumulators each pressured loop holds live ACROSS a call.
//
// EIGHT IS A CHOSEN NUMBER, not a round one. The allocatable pool is 14 GPRs, of which only 5 are
// callee-saved — so a value live across a call has just those 5 to live in, and the excess MUST go
// to memory. Eight accumulators plus the loop counter plus the running total is ten values live
// across the call: five forced spill brackets per loop, which is exactly the splitter work this
// knob exists to provoke.
//
// And it stays clear of E5001. Peak pressure here is eleven (the ten above plus the call's result),
// against a full pool of fourteen — so the loop FITS the machine, and the spills are the ABI's
// doing, not a register shortage. Raising this to fourteen-plus would make the corpus fail to
// compile with a register-pressure diagnostic and VOID the whole ladder. See
// `specs-shv2/register-pressure.md`, which spells out that a forced spill across a call is not
// E5001 and must never become it.
let PressuredValuesPerLoop = 8 as KnobSize

// Iterations of each generated loop. Small: the trip count is irrelevant to the COMPILE-cost
// measurement — only the code shape is — and the rungs are now RUN as well, so it is also what each
// loop's witnessed contribution is summed over. Big enough not to look degenerate.
let GeneratedLoopTrips = 4

// ===========================================================================================
// THE RUNGS ARE RUN, AND EVERY DRIVER WITNESSES ITSELF (BATCH10 A2n)
// ===========================================================================================
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SITE EXPRESSION CHANGED SHAPE, FROM *FOLD-INTO-`acc`* TO *COMPARE-AGAINST-A-CONSTRUCTED
// RELATION*, AND IT IS THE WHOLE OF THIS SECTION.** Every knob used to end a site by folding the
// construct's value into the threaded accumulator — `acc = acc + s.x + s.y`, `acc = acc + gv{v}`,
// `acc = acc + uv.probe() + uv.scaleBy(3)`. Every one of those is MULTIPLICATIVE in `acc` (each reads
// `acc` two or three times and adds the readings back), so `acc` did not grow, it COMPOUNDED: measured
// on this ladder's own rung 0, `7^N` for the struct knobs, `2^N` for the globals knob, `2^(N/6)` for
// `longFunction`'s coupled `w{q} = w{q} + w{r}`.
//
// **What that cost, all four MEASURED on the rung-0 binary before this change:**
//   • `acc` left i64 and came back NEGATIVE by the seventh driver (`scaleShifts`, at -8.98e18).
//   • ⭐ **NINE DRIVERS WERE RUNTIME NO-OPS, SEVEN OF THEM FOR THIS FILE'S OWN THESIS.**
//     `ownedSiteBlock`'s guard is `if acc > 0`; once `acc` wrapped negative EVERY heap-creating site in
//     the corpus was SKIPPED — the array knobs, both generic knobs, both interface knobs, the buffer
//     surface knob. They were still COMPILED, so no compile-cost trend is affected, but not one of them
//     ever EXECUTED. A guard that cannot fail, inside the ladder, for as long as it has had heap.
//   • `scaleNested` ran ZERO iterations at rung 0 (`acc` was already past `NestedLoopLimit`) and did not
//     TERMINATE at rung 1 (`acc` arrived at -4.87e18, so `while acc < 1000000` had 4.87e18/D trips left).
//     That is the hang this rung was filed for, and the loop-nest bound was innocent.
//   • `scaleFloats` returned `i64.min` — `trunc()` of a double outside i64.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND A SITE'S DATA COMES FROM THE PARAMETER `a`, NEVER FROM THE RUNNING `acc` — WHICH IS NOT A
// TIDINESS POINT, IT IS WHAT MAKES THE WITNESS DISCRIMINATE.** MEASURED, while sabotaging this rung's own
// mechanism to prove it fails: with the divide knob reading `acc`, breaking its emitted mask left every
// rung STILL EXITING 7. The reason is that a corrupted `acc` feeds the NEXT site, so the driver is a map
// `acc -> f(acc)` — and that map had a two-cycle through the seed, so an even site count (32 at rung 0)
// walked out to exactly the value it walked in with. A check that a systematic sabotage can cancel is a
// check that cannot fail, which is this batch's whole subject.
//
// Reading `a` breaks the feedback: every site's expectation is exact regardless of what happened at the
// previous one, so N sabotaged sites contribute N times one non-zero delta rather than folding back into
// a cycle. `a` is exactly as opaque as `acc` was — it is the PARAMETER, which this file's header names as
// one of the only two things `foldConstOperands` cannot see through, and every generated function already
// derived its first value from it. The cost is that `a` is live to the end of a driver where `acc` used
// to be; the count of long-lived values per driver is unchanged.
//
// ⇒ **A site now SUBTRACTS the value the generator constructed it to produce.** `s.x` is `a` because
// this generator wrote `create(acc, …)`; `ab{i}.count()` is 2 because it wrote two `push`es;
// `sv{i}.byteLength()` is the literal's length plus `st{i}.byteLength()` because it wrote the `append`.
// Subtracting that leaves `acc` at the value it arrived with, so `acc` is PINNED at the seed through
// every driver and every rung. Nothing is simulated: this is `Testing/ladders/genretsites.sh`'s idiom
// one level down — `rrPick(7)` returns 7 because the generator WROTE site 7 — and where a value takes a
// loop to know, it is TRACKED IN THE SAME EMISSION LOOP THAT WRITES THE SITE, which is one model and
// not two.
//
// It costs one `sub` per fold site, and buys: the rungs terminate, every heap site executes, and each
// driver's answer is CHECKED rather than merely produced.
//
// ⚠ **THE LADDER'S ABSOLUTES MOVED WITH THIS COMMIT AND ROWS EITHER SIDE OF IT ARE NOT COMPARABLE** —
// the same discontinuity `NamespacedModulesBase` records for its own arrival, and it is paid once.
// Growth RATIOS are unaffected: the added `sub` is one op per site, so it doubles with its knob.

// The generator's own arithmetic register — i64, exactly as the emitted programs' `int` is, and
// MEASURED to wrap identically under both compilers (`x + x` 200 times reads 0, `i64.max + 1` reads
// `i64.min`, on a bootstrap-built and an shv2-built binary alike). That equality is what makes tracking
// a value while emitting it sound even where the tracked value overflows, which `longFunction`'s
// accumulators do by rung 1.
typealias TrackedValue = int(i64.min to i64.max)
typealias TrackedValueArray = Array with TrackedValue

// What `main` returns when every driver in the rung witnessed itself. SEVEN, the convention the hand
// ladders already use (`Testing/ladders/README.md`) — a value nothing else in the process returns, so a
// rung that crashed, leaked or exited on a diagnostic cannot be mistaken for one that passed.
export let CorpusOkExit = 7

// ...and when one did not. Distinct from `CorpusOkExit` and from 0, so silence is never success.
let CorpusWitnessFailedExit = 1

// What a failed witness adds to the accumulator. Large and odd, so that neither two failures nor a
// failure plus a legitimate contribution can cancel back to the seed: every knob's real per-site
// contribution is under a million (`NestedLoopLimit` is the largest), and 10^5 failing sites would still
// be 14 digits short of wrapping i64.
let CorpusWitnessFailedOffset = 1000000007

// The seed loop's value, which is `main`'s expectation and every driver's input.
//
// It is a CLOSED FORM rather than a second copy of the loop: `seedSource` emits a loop that adds
// `SeedStep * i` for `i` in `[0, GeneratedLoopTrips)`, and the Gauss sum of that is stated here, over
// the same two constants the emitter reads. There is one recurrence and one statement of its value.
let SeedInitial = 1
let SeedStep = 3

function corpusSeedValue() returns TrackedValue
	return SeedInitial + SeedStep * ((GeneratedLoopTrips * (GeneratedLoopTrips - 1)) / 2)
end 'corpusSeedValue'

// `a`, `a + 5`, `a - 5` — the closed form a witness is stated as.
//
// ⚠ IT IS WRITTEN IN TERMS OF `a` AND NEVER AS A BARE CONSTANT, and that is not cosmetic: a witness
// whose BOTH sides are constants is one `foldConstOperands` may settle at compile time, and the check
// would then be free and worthless. Anchoring the expectation to the opaque parameter keeps the compare
// a runtime one whatever the left-hand side turns out to be.
function witnessExpr(expected TrackedValue) returns String
	let offset = expected - corpusSeedValue()
	if offset == 0 'exact'
		return "a"
	end 'exact'

	if offset < 0 'below'
		if offset == i64.min 'unnegatable'
			panic("ScaleCorpus.witnessExpr: an expectation {expected} below the seed by exactly i64.min has no negation to spell as a literal — the tracked value is almost certainly wrong")
		end 'unnegatable'
		return "a - {0 - offset}"
	end 'below'
	return "a + {offset}"
end 'witnessExpr'

// ⭐⭐ **THE MONOTONE HALF OF EVERY WITNESS, AND THE REASON IT EXISTS IS MEASURED RATHER THAN IMAGINED.**
// Subtracting an expectation from a running total is exact and REVERSIBLE — a later site can put it back
// — and one did: the FIRST sabotage written to prove this rung fails, a broken divisor mask, left every
// rung still exiting 7, because the driver's map had a two-cycle through the seed and an even site count
// walked out where it walked in. Pinning the site data to `a` stopped a corrupted total from steering
// later sites, but two sites failing by equal and opposite deltas would still have summed to nothing.
//
// So every expectation is ALSO folded into a counter that can only ever gain bits. `or` is the operator
// and not a stand-in for one: it has no carry, so no later value can borrow a bit back out, and
// `wit == 0` holds if and only if EVERY difference ever folded in was zero. Cancellation stops being
// unlikely and becomes unrepresentable.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND THE CANCELLING CASE WAS BUILT AND RUN, BECAUSE "I COULD NOT CONSTRUCT ONE" IS A STATEMENT
// ABOUT HOW HARD SOMEBODY LOOKED.** `structCreateInBlock` writes TWO folds against one `create`, so
// shifting the emitted `y` up by one and the emitted field REWRITE down by one makes the first fold read
// one too high and the second read one too low — equal, opposite, and at every site. MEASURED on the
// rung-0 binary with a print in front of the check: **`acc=19`, `wit=1`** — the additive total came out
// EXACTLY at the seed, so the check this rung shipped with an hour earlier would have passed it, and only
// the counter refused. That is the third sabotage, and it is the reason the counter exists rather than
// an argument that it might.
//
// ⛔ IT COSTS ONE INSTRUCTION AND NO BRANCH. The probe rides the straight line the fold already sits on,
// so no knob gains a block, an edge or a phi — which is not a nicety: `deepBlocks` exists to price CFG
// growth, and a corpus that grew its own CFG to check itself would be measuring the checker.
function witnessProbe(indent String, valueExpr String, expected TrackedValue) returns String
	return "{indent}wit = wit or (({valueExpr}) - ({witnessExpr(expected)}))\n"
end 'witnessProbe'

// The probe every fold site emits: the running total against the seed it is pinned at. One line, written
// once, because it follows ~40 fold sites and an inverted sign at any of them would arm the very hole
// this closes.
function accProbe(indent String) returns String
	return witnessProbe(indent, valueExpr: "acc", expected: corpusSeedValue())
end 'accProbe'

// The two accumulators every witnessed driver opens with: the ADDITIVE total, which each site neutralizes
// back to the seed, and the MONOTONE counter, which each site's difference is ORed into. Two independent
// failures — one that a later site could in principle undo, and one that nothing can.
function witnessedDriverHeader(name String) returns String
	return "{driverHeader(name)}\tvar acc = a\n\tvar wit = 0\n"
end 'witnessedDriverHeader'

// The tail EVERY driver ends with: hand back the argument UNCHANGED when the body computed what this
// generator built it to compute, and a value that cannot be the argument when it did not.
//
// Returning `a` rather than the body's value is what pins the accumulator across `main` — and pinning it
// is what makes every rung terminate and every `ownedSiteBlock` guard true. The failure path is an
// OFFSET rather than a throw because a rung is a program, not a test: `main` compares the threaded total
// once, at the end, and a single shifted driver moves it out of the only value that exits 7.
//
// The driver's own final value is folded into the counter as one more expectation rather than compared
// separately, so there is ONE thing to test at the end and it is the one that cannot cancel.
//
// ⭐ **`main` NEEDS NO COUNTER OF ITS OWN, AND THAT IS A CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN AN OPINION.** This tail
// returns exactly `a` or `a + CorpusWitnessFailedOffset` and nothing else, so the value `main` threads
// through N drivers is exactly `seed + k * CorpusWitnessFailedOffset` for `k` failures. That equals the
// seed only at `k = 0` until `k` reaches 2^64 / CorpusWitnessFailedOffset ≈ 1.8e10 drivers, against a top
// rung that has about a thousand. "The total is the seed" and "no driver failed" are the same statement
// there; a second accumulator in `main` would double the largest generated file and discriminate nothing.
function driverWitnessTail(driverName String, valueExpr String, expected TrackedValue) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append(witnessProbe("\t", valueExpr: valueExpr, expected: expected))
	out.append("\tif wit == 0 'witness'\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn a\n")
	out.append("\tend 'witness'\n")
	out.append("\treturn a + {CorpusWitnessFailedOffset}\n")
	out.append("end '{driverName}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'driverWitnessTail'

// The tail of a driver whose accumulator this generator pinned at the seed site by site — the common
// case, and the one the section header is about.
function accWitnessTail(driverName String) returns String
	return driverWitnessTail(driverName, valueExpr: "acc", expected: corpusSeedValue())
end 'accWitnessTail'

// ` - 5` / ` + 5` / `` — what a fold site appends to neutralize the contribution the generator knows it
// just wrote. Written once because thirty-odd sites append it and an inverted sign would pin `acc` to
// the wrong value everywhere at once.
function neutralizeText(contribution TrackedValue) returns String
	if contribution == 0 'nothing'
		return ""
	end 'nothing'

	if contribution < 0 'negative'
		return " + {0 - contribution}"
	end 'negative'
	return " - {contribution}"
end 'neutralizeText'

// ===========================================================================================
// The ten knobs for what the language gained between P1.1b and P1.7a
// ===========================================================================================
//
// ⚠ THIS IS THE FIFTH — AND LARGEST — RECURRENCE OF THIS FILE'S FOUNDING DEFECT, AND IT WAS TEN RUNGS
// DEEP. See the header for the measurement: `match`, `enum`, `String`, `Array`, generics, interfaces,
// `print`, `or`/`xor`, and struct field WRITES were all in the subset and the corpus generated ZERO of
// each, at every rung. The knobs below close it.
//
// EVERY ONE OF THEM IS CROSS-TARGET-SAFE, AND THAT IS A HARD CONSTRAINT, NOT A PREFERENCE. `compileRung`
// spawns `build` with NO `--target`, so the ladder always compiles for the HOST — and `witnessCall` on
// arm64/wasm is a `panic(...)`, not a clean diagnostic, so a rung that generated one would fail to
// compile and take the WHOLE RUN down through `brokenRun`. So: unconstrained generics only (no `where`
// clause anywhere ⇒ no witness table ⇒ no `funcAbs64InRdata` relocation), interfaces reached by CONCRETE
// dispatch only (no `where T is I`), and no `Set`. Those three, plus opaque-`T` managed arrays, are the
// x64-only families this ladder must not emit; `printCorpusManifest` names them under NOT GENERATED with
// exactly that reason.

// ONE file, N `enum` declarations, each matched exhaustively once — the PER-TYPE cost: the enum
// registry, its case table, and the tag's scalar backing.
//
// ⚠ NOT `matchSites` WITH A DIFFERENT NUMBER, and the separation is the same one `structTypes` keeps
// against `structAllocs`. This knob grows how many enums the compiler must DECLARE and lay out; that one
// grows how many `match` SITES dispatch over a fixed set of them. A change to enum declaration or tag
// layout moves this column and not that one; a change to the dispatch STRATEGY moves that one and not
// this one. Two knobs that a future change must be free to move independently are not one knob.
let EnumTypesBase = 4 as KnobSize

// How many cases each generated enum declares.
//
// ⚠ FOUR, AND THE NUMBER IS LOAD-BEARING — it is `Parser.MinIntervalsForDispatch`. At or above four
// disjoint intervals a `match` lowers to a balanced binary search or, if the covered span is dense
// enough, a JUMP TABLE; below four it lowers to a linear compare chain. Three cases here would make the
// dense statement-form site and the range-folded expression-form site pick the SAME strategy, and the
// jump table — an `.rdata` table plus an indirect jump — would be generated nowhere in the corpus.
let EnumCasesPerType = 4 as KnobSize

// ONE file, M `match` SITES over a FIXED pair of enums — the PER-SITE cost: the normalized dispatch
// interval list, the strategy chosen from it, and the blocks and phis it mints.
//
// ⚠ IT ROTATES OVER ALL THREE DISPATCH STRATEGIES, because a corpus that generated only one would
// measure a third of the lowering — the mistake `IntDivideBase` records for `idiv` (the corpus was full
// of `/` and every one was a FLOAT `/`). See `matchSitesSource` for which shape provokes which.
let MatchSitesBase = 16 as KnobSize

// The three site SHAPES `matchSitesSource` rotates over: statement-form dense (jump table),
// expression-form range-folded (linear chain), expression-form sparse (binary search).
let MatchDispatchShapeCount = 3 as KnobSize

// The raw-value stride between the SPARSE enum's cases — what forces a binary search rather than a
// table. `Parser.MaxTableSlotsPerInterval` is 32, so four cases spanning `3 * stride + 1` slots must
// exceed 128 covered slots to be refused a table: a stride of 100 spans 301 slots at 1.3% density,
// nowhere near the 40% floor. A stride of 1 would be the dense case and this shape would collapse into
// the one above it.
let MatchSparseCaseStride = 100

// ONE file, M `String` operation groups — the per-SITE cost of the heap String: a literal's `.rdata`
// blob and record, an INTERPOLATION of the opaque accumulator (an int→String conversion, which
// allocates), an `.append` (which detaches onto a private buffer), `==` through `__str_eq`, and the
// `__str_decref` each owned String's death forces.
//
// `byteLength()` is the way OUT of the String domain, exactly as `trunc` is for floats: it is what makes
// the group's result flow back into `acc` and therefore load-bearing, so no future DCE pass may take the
// String dimension away again.
let StringOpsBase = 8 as KnobSize

// ===========================================================================================
// THE FOUR KNOBS FOR THE REST OF `String`'S MEMBER SURFACE (P1.8 slices B, B2, C, D, E)
// ===========================================================================================
//
// ⚠ **THIS FILE'S FOUNDING DEFECT, FOR THE EIGHTH TIME, AND IT IS ABOUT TO BE PAID FOR ONE WAVE AT A
// TIME.** `stringOps` above generates a literal, an interpolation, `.append`, `.byteLength()` and `==`.
// It generates NOT ONE of the twenty-odd other members `dispatchStringMethod` answers — no predicate, no
// case conversion, no `replace`/`split`, no trim, no `StringIndex` producer or consumer, no materializer,
// no `isEmpty`/`clone`. Every one of them is in the subset (each has live, enabled cases in
// `specs-shv2/string-methods-ascii.md`, `string-index.md`, `string-trim.md` and `string-views.md`), and
// every one of them is about to be RETIRED from the compiler's synthesized surface onto
// `stdlib/String.maxon`'s bodies. Without these knobs every retirement wave would read a flat delta of
// ZERO in allocations, in bytes AND in CPU alike — the reading the header's seven other entries all
// describe, arriving in a programme whose whole point is that the cost be readable.
//
// ⭐ **FOUR KNOBS RATHER THAN ONE, AND THE SPLIT IS `dispatchStringMethod`'S OWN.** Its arms are grouped
// and labelled by the slice that built them, and the grouping is not cosmetic: each is a different piece
// of the compiler, and the retirement programme moves them ONE FAMILY AT A TIME. After a family is
// retired its per-site cost changes character completely — a synthesized runtime graph becomes an
// ordinary Maxon call into a body that must be parsed, typed and lowered — so a reader will need to move
// these counts independently of each other. That is exactly the test `EnumTypesBase` states for when two
// knobs are not one knob.

// Slice C — the seven BYTE/ASCII members, plus `replaceFirst` (slice E, which is `replace`'s emitter with
// one flag). No Unicode character database, no grapheme segmentation, no `StringIndex`: `startsWith`,
// `endsWith`, `contains`, `toLower`, `toUpper`, `replace`, `replaceFirst`, `split`.
let StringSearchOpsBase = 8 as KnobSize

// Slice B2 — the `StringIndex` family: the two ends (`startIndex`/`endIndex`), the two THROWING searches
// (`findFirst`/`findLast`), the third throwing method (`indexAfter`), both `slice` overloads and the
// index's own two accessors (`charIndex`/`bytePos`).
//
// ⚠ ITS PER-SITE COST IS BLOCKS, NOT MERELY OPS — every throwing member sits under a `try`, which forks —
// so it is the String family that moves the CFG columns, exactly as `CheckedDivideBase` is for divide.
//
// HALF ITS TWO ASCII SIBLINGS' BASE, for that reason and MEASURED: at an EQUAL base this one file compiled
// in 4.78s at the top rung against 2.23s for the search family and 0.94s for the materializers — four `try`
// forks per group buy BLOCKS where those two buy ops. (Each figure is a standalone `build` of that one rung-5
// file net of a 147 ms compiler startup, measured on this tree; the ladder compiles them together.)
let StringIndexOpsBase = 4 as KnobSize

// Slice D — the three trims, over BOTH argument forms: the default `CharacterSet` (built by the compiler
// when the call presents none) and an explicit one. They are one runtime graph and two flags, so what
// grows here is how many places that graph is REACHED from.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A QUARTER OF ITS THREE SIBLINGS' BASE, AND THE NUMBER IS MEASURED RATHER THAN CHOSEN.** A trim
// against `CharacterSet.whitespacesAndNewlines()` emits **194 x64 ops into the calling block** — the set is
// materialized INLINE at the call site, once per site. Counted on this tree with `--emit-ir`, in one `if`
// block each:
//
//     s.trim()                                    194 ops   <- the DEFAULT form
//     s.trim(CharacterSet.whitespacesAndNewlines())  194 ops   <- IDENTICAL: it is the SET, not the default
//     s.trim(CharacterSet.decimalDigits())         50 ops
//     s.trim(CharacterSet.letters())               50 ops
//     s.toUpper()                                  12 ops
//
// ⇒ ~144 of those ops are the whitespace set's own construction, and they are per-SITE. That is 16x a
// `toUpper` and it lands in ONE basic block, which is what the number has to be read against: the register
// allocator's pressure index gives each block a run of `roundUpToPowerOfTwo((ops + 1) * SlotGap + 1)` SLOTS
// (`SplitLiveRanges.placeRuns`), so a 194-op block costs 1,024 slots and a block holding three such trims
// costs 4,096. At `8 << 4` groups with all three trims in one block, `slotCount` reached ~4.2M and
// `PressureDeltaColumn.reserve` exhausted committed memory: **rung 4 did not compile at all** — a BROKEN
// RUN, which is the one thing this instrument exits non-zero for.
//
// ⇒ TWO responses, and they are different in kind. The corpus's own half is `trimSiteBlock`: ONE SITE PER
// BLOCK, so no block in this family carries more than one whitespace set and the padding a power-of-two run
// wastes falls from 1.77x to 1.31x. The base is the other half — this family simply buys 16x the ops per
// site that its neighbours do, so an equal base would make one knob the ladder's whole cost.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS A BUDGET, NOT A BLIND SPOT, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THIS FILE'S OWN.** All three members and
// both argument forms are generated at EVERY rung and the count still DOUBLES with the rung, so nothing
// here reads a flat zero. What the low base buys is that a rung compiles at all. At ONE, the top rung still
// holds 32 groups — 96 whitespace-set sites and 96 explicit ones — and this file alone still costs 2.53s
// there, the most of the four, against 0.94s for the materializer knob at EIGHT times the base. (Standalone
// rung-5 `build` times net of a 147 ms startup, measured on this tree.)
let StringTrimOpsBase = 1 as KnobSize

// Slices B and E — the MATERIALIZERS and the two leftovers: `count()` (the UAX #29 grapheme walk),
// `bytes()`, `toByteArray()`, `codepoints()`, `utf16()`, `isEmpty()` and `clone()`.
//
// ⚠ THIS IS THE ONLY KNOB IN THE WHOLE CORPUS WHOSE PAYLOAD IS NOT ALL-ASCII, and it has to be: the four
// counts a materializer answers are EQUAL on ASCII text, so an ASCII corpus cannot tell `count()` from
// `codepoints().count()` from `bytes().count()` and a materializer answering with its neighbour's walk
// would measure and witness identically. See `ViewSegmentText`.
let StringViewOpsBase = 8 as KnobSize

// ONE file, M `Character` operation groups — the per-SITE cost of the OTHER byte record.
//
// ⚠ **IT EXISTS BECAUSE A WHOLE LANGUAGE MODEL LANDED INTO A CORPUS THAT COULD NOT SPELL IT.** BATCH23
// made every character literal a `Character` (it had been an `int` whenever it fitted in one byte), gave
// `Character` an ORDERING through `__char_cmp`, and taught an integer position to convert a literal to
// its codepoint by REWRITING the emitted op in place. This generator emitted **not one character
// literal** at any rung — `grep -c "'" rung5/*.maxon` over a dumped corpus found none — so all three
// read a flat delta of zero in allocations, in bytes AND in CPU ticks. That is the blind spot this file's
// header calls its founding defect, arriving for the sixth time; the reading is only worth what the
// corpus can express.
//
// ⚠ **NOT `stringOps` WITH A NARROWER LITERAL.** The two types share the 56-byte record and every rule
// but one, and that one is the point: a `Character` is `Comparable` and a `String` is not, so `c < d`
// lowers to a `__char_cmp` call plus an `Ordering` verdict where `s < t` is a diagnostic. A change to
// `buildByteRecordCompare`, to the character `match` compare chain or to `retypeCharLiteralAsCodepoint`
// moves this column and leaves the String one flat.
let CharOpsBase = 8 as KnobSize

// ONE file, N types each owning a `String` field, each built once and dropped at scope exit — the
// PER-TYPE cost of the SYNTHESIZED destructor cascade (`__destruct_<T>`, which frees the field).
//
// ⚠ NOT `stringOps` WITH A STRUCT AROUND IT. That knob grows how many times String CODE runs; this one
// grows how many distinct `__destruct_<T>` bodies the compiler must SYNTHESIZE and root. A change to
// `installStructDestructors` moves this column and not that one.
let ManagedStructTypesBase = 4 as KnobSize

// ONE file, M TRIVIAL-element `Array` operation groups — `create`/`reserve`/`push`/`get`/`count`, plus
// the O(1) COW trio `slice`/`clone`/`append`, over a ranged-int element alias.
//
// The COW trio is not decoration: `.slice` and `.clone` refcount the BUFFER rather than the record, and
// `.append` is what forces a detach. A corpus with only `push`/`get` measures the growth path and none
// of the sharing path.
let ArrayOpsBase = 8 as KnobSize

// ONE file, M CONCRETE-MANAGED-element `Array` operation groups — `Array with String` and
// `Array with <struct owning a String>`: the element-destructor threading, and the RETAIN a `get` of a
// managed element owes.
//
// ⚠ NOT `arrayOps` WITH A DIFFERENT ELEMENT TYPE, for the reason the two struct knobs are not one: the
// managed path stores an element DROP function in the array record and calls it per slot, and a change
// to that threading moves this column while leaving the trivial one flat. (OPAQUE-`T` managed elements —
// the descriptor-gated `funcAbs64InRdata` path — are x64-only and deliberately absent; see this
// section's header.)
let ManagedArrayOpsBase = 4 as KnobSize

// ONE file, N generic BASE declarations, alternating `uses T` and `uses A, B`, each instantiated once —
// the PER-BASE cost: the type-parameter registry and the ONE shared method body per base that
// dictionary-passing compiles (there is no monomorphization, so a base costs one body however many
// instances it has).
//
// ⚠ NOT `genericInstances` WITH A DIFFERENT NUMBER. That knob holds the base FIXED and grows the
// INSTANTIATIONS, which is where the layout descriptors are. This one grows the bases, which is where
// the shared bodies are. A change to generic-body compilation moves this column; a change to
// `ensureLayoutDescriptor` moves that one.
let GenericBasesBase = 4 as KnobSize

// The two ARITY shapes `genericBasesSource` alternates over: `uses T` and `uses A, B`.
//
// Named for `MatchDispatchShapeCount`'s reason, and it used to be a bare `2` written at BOTH of that
// knob's loops. The count and the branches that realize it are one fact stated twice, so the `else` at
// each of those loops panics rather than falling through — raise this without adding a branch and the
// ladder stops loudly instead of silently generating fewer arities than the manifest claims.
let GenericBaseArityShapeCount = 2 as KnobSize

// ONE file, M `typealias … = Base with Arg` INSTANTIATIONS over a FIXED base — the PER-INSTANCE cost:
// the layout descriptor, the per-instance typealias, and (for a managed argument) that instance's own
// `__destruct_<instance>`.
//
// ⚠ EVERY INSTANCE NEEDS ITS OWN TYPE ARGUMENT, AND THAT IS WHY THE ARGUMENTS ARE GENERATED TOO. Two
// aliases of `Box with Integer` are two names for ONE instance, so a knob that spelled M aliases over
// one argument would grow M names and one descriptor — a dimension that reads as covered and is flat by
// construction, which is precisely this file's founding defect in miniature. So instance `i` gets its
// own argument: a ranged int alias whose RANGE is unique to `i` (a generic's ranged element type is part
// of its type, `e4146cf8e`), or a struct unique to `i` that owns a `String`.
let GenericInstancesBase = 8 as KnobSize

// The two type-argument shapes `genericInstancesSource` rotates over: a TRIVIAL ranged int (borrowed
// into the box) and a MANAGED struct owning a String (moved in, and dropped through the instance's own
// cascade). Both must scale, because they are different ownership paths through the same base.
let GenericInstanceArgShapeCount = 2 as KnobSize

// ⭐⭐ **M `try` SITES OVER A SUBSTITUTED *MANAGED* `T` RETURN — the shape BATCH25 built, and the shape
// this corpus could not spell on the day it shipped.**
//
// A shared generic body cannot classify its `T` return, so it hands one back as a BORROW and the CALLER
// takes the `+1` (`coOwnSubstitutedCallResult`) — necessarily after the call. Under a `try` that `+1`
// may not sit in the block the fork splits, where the error edge would run it against a result register
// the throw never wrote; BATCH25 LIFTS the promotion's ops off that block and re-attaches them at the
// head of the ok edge. **Every op of that mechanism is per-SITE**, which is what this knob doubles.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A SITE KNOB, AND THE TYPES ARE FIXED — for the Array knobs' reason.** The per-INSTANCE cost
// (a layout descriptor, a per-instance `__destruct_<instance>`) is `genericInstances`' dimension and
// this knob must not re-measure it: two knobs whose columns move together explain nothing about which
// change moved them. So this file declares exactly ONE base, ONE aggregate holder and TWO instances —
// `Box with <holder>` and `Box with String` — and only the SITE count climbs.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH MANAGED SPELLINGS ARE GENERATED, BECAUSE THE CURE IS KEYED ON AN OP RANGE PRECISELY BECAUSE
// THEY DIFFER.** An aggregate promotes with a single `__mm_retain` call; a `String` promotes by COPYING
// (`promoteToOwnedString`), whose trailing op is an interpolation rather than a call. A knob that
// generated only the aggregate would leave the arm that motivated the range unmeasured.
let TryCoOwnSitesBase = 8 as KnobSize

// The two managed spellings `tryCoOwnSource` rotates over — see the note above. Named for
// `GenericInstanceArgShapeCount`'s reason: the count and the branches that realize it are one fact, so
// the USE loop's `else` panics rather than silently generating fewer spellings than the manifest claims.
let TryCoOwnSpellingCount = 2 as KnobSize

// N interfaces and their conforming types — the PER-CONFORMANCE cost: `ConformanceCheck`'s declared-
// conformance index, the method-requirement match, and one compiled method body per requirement.
//
// ⚠ THIS KNOB GROWS FILES, DELIBERATELY, AND IT IS THE ONLY ONE BESIDES `manyFiles` THAT DOES.
// `Parser.requireInterfaceForParse` scans the CURRENT FILE's interfaces once per `where`-constraint
// site, so interfaces-per-file is a measured cost multiplier — and the standing instruction from the
// triage that filed it is not to land many protocol interfaces in one artifact. Holding
// `InterfacesPerFile` CONSTANT and letting the file count double is what keeps that scan bounded while
// the conformance count still climbs. (This corpus emits no `where` clause, so the scan has zero sites
// today; the shape is kept honest for the rung that adds one.)
let InterfaceTypesBase = 4 as KnobSize

// How many interfaces (and their conforming types) go in ONE generated file — see `InterfaceTypesBase`.
let InterfacesPerFile = 4 as KnobSize

// ONE file, M direct `instance.method()` DISPATCH SITES over a FIXED set of conforming types — the
// per-SITE cost of CONCRETE interface dispatch, which is a direct call to a known body and NOT a
// witness-table lookup (that is `where T is I`, x64-only, and absent — see this section's header).
//
// ⚠ NOT `interfaceTypes` WITH A DIFFERENT NUMBER: a change to conformance CHECKING moves that column,
// a change to how an interface method CALL lowers moves this one.
let IfaceDispatchBase = 8 as KnobSize

// The bounded set of conforming types `ifaceDispatchSource` rotates its call sites over. CONSTANT for
// the reason `AllocStructTypeCount` is: were the dispatch knob to grow its own conformances it would BE
// `interfaceTypes`, and the separation defended above would exist only in the comments.
let IfaceDispatchTypeCount = 4 as KnobSize

// ===========================================================================================
// The `__ManagedMemory` BUFFER SURFACE knob (BATCH9 G7)
// ===========================================================================================
//
// ⚠ **THE SIXTH RECURRENCE OF THIS FILE'S FOUNDING DEFECT, AND THE MANIFEST DID NOT EVEN NAME IT.**
// MEASURED on the ladder this knob was written against: `.managed`, `ByteArray`, `toByteArray`,
// `addressableBytes` and `__ManagedMemory` appeared **zero** times in the generated corpus, at rung 0 and
// at rung 5 alike. So `Parser.bufferSurfaceOf` and `Parser.markBufferSurface` — the whole surface
// mechanism, eight call sites in seven functions — ran EXACTLY ZERO TIMES on every rung, and the Δ0 the
// BATCH2 slice-6 rung recorded measured the instrument's blind spot rather than the cost.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE READ SIDE IS SHORT-CIRCUITED TO NOTHING IN A FILE THAT HAS NO MARKS, WHICH IS WHY "the corpus
// already dispatches array methods" WAS NOT COVERAGE.** `Parser.valueIsBufferSurface` is asked once per
// array-method dispatch, and on the shared zero-capacity set it "returns before it hashes anything" (its
// own note) — so all 134 array-method call sites the old ladder held at rung 0, and all 4,288 at rung 5,
// took the free path every time. `bufferSurfaceValues` is PER-PARSER, i.e. per FILE, so the product a
// linear-column regression would cost is (marks in this file) x (array dispatches in this file). Both
// live in this knob's own file, and both double.
//
// EIGHT, WHICH IS THIS BAND'S PER-SITE BASE — `StringOpsBase`, `ArrayOpsBase`, `IfaceDispatchBase` and
// `GenericInstancesBase` all sit there, and this is a per-SITE knob by the same test: it grows the number
// of places a buffer is PRODUCED over a FIXED set of three aliases and one holder type, so a change to how
// `__ManagedMemory` is LAID OUT moves nothing here and a change to how a buffer value is MINTED moves
// everything. Not 4 (the per-TYPE families' base, `ManagedStructTypesBase` / `ManagedArrayOpsBase`),
// because nothing in this knob is per-type.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT IS NOT `FloatSpillLoopsBase`'S TRAP IN A NEW COAT, BECAUSE THE MECHANISM HAS NO THRESHOLD** —
// one `.managed` in value position reaches `bufferSurfaceOf`, so there is no count below which the door
// stops opening. What a too-low base WOULD cost is the growth term: the mark set is copy-on-write off a
// shared empty one (`Parser.privateValueMarkSet`), so a knob of ONE site would price the COPY and never the
// SET, and the quadratic a value-keyed column would introduce could not out-run the constant term at any
// rung. Eight groups puts ~81 marks against 162 array-method dispatches in this file at rung 0, and ~2,592
// against 5,122 at rung 5 — a product that rises 1,024-fold across the ladder while the linear term rises
// 32-fold.
let BufferSurfaceSitesBase = 8 as KnobSize

// The buffer each group builds: `create(BufferSurfaceCapacity, elementSize: 1)`, a length published inside
// that capacity, and a slice inside that length. Named because each is a bare literal in TWO programs at
// once — this one and the one it generates — and because the three are ORDERED: a `setLength` past the
// capacity or a `slice` past the length is a shape real code does not write. That ordering is now CHECKED
// rather than merely asserted — `bufferStaticAndSliceBlock` reads both lengths back and witnesses them —
// where before, with nothing running the program, nothing would ever have caught it.
let BufferSurfaceCapacity = 8
let BufferSurfacePublishedLength = 4
let BufferSurfaceSliceEnd = 2

// The element stride `__ManagedMemory.create` is given. ONE, because `Parser.requireManagedMemoryElementSize`
// picks the result's `Array` instance from this constant and a byte stride is the instance `__ManagedMemory`
// canonically denotes — the same one `String.toByteArray()` hands back, which is what lets the two producers
// in this knob's file share a type.
let BufferSurfaceElementSize = 1

// Masks the opaque accumulator into the byte alias's range. `DivisorMask`'s argument, one alias over: the
// pushed value must derive from the seed or it folds, and it must fit `int(0 to u8.max)` or the cast's
// runtime guard would be a real failure in a program nobody runs to observe it.
let BufferSurfaceByteMask = 255

// ===========================================================================================
// The NAMESPACE knob (N1b) — SUBDIRECTORIES, and references written `dir.fn()` / `dir.Name`
// ===========================================================================================
//
// ⚠ **THE SEVENTH RECURRENCE OF THIS FILE'S FOUNDING DEFECT, AND THIS TIME THE RUNG'S OWN IMPLEMENTER
// NAMED IT BEFORE SHIPPING.** N1a taught the compiler that a file's DIRECTORY is its namespace and N1b
// taught it to resolve `dir.Name` / `dir.fn()` against it — and this generator wrote every rung's files
// into ONE FLAT DIRECTORY. So `deriveModuleDirectory` answered `""` for every source file in every rung,
// `Parser.namespace` was empty everywhere, and the whole user half of both rungs — the qualified-alias
// MIRROR, the namespace-qualified FUNCTION set, the dotted-chain recognizer, the multi-segment walk —
// ran EXACTLY ZERO TIMES on every rung of the ladder. N1b's own A/B measured −22,639 allocations at
// rung 5 and every one of them came from `splitQualifiedName` and the stdlib's mirrors; not one came
// from a user declaration or a user reference, because the corpus could not spell either.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THIS KNOB IS A WIDENING OF WHAT THE INSTRUMENT CAN EXPRESS, WHICH IS THE OPPOSITE OF TUNING IT.**
// Nothing here makes a number look better; it makes a dimension VISIBLE that read a structural zero
// before. ⚠ It therefore changes the ladder's ABSOLUTES — every rung compiles more files and more
// declarations than it did — so `docs/optimization-log.md` rows minted before this knob are NOT
// comparable to rows minted after it. That is the price of curing a blind spot and it is paid once.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS A DIRECTORY KNOB, WHICH IS WHY IT GROWS FILES — AND IT IS THE THIRD THAT DOES** (after
// `manyFiles` and `interfaceTypes`). A namespace is not a syntactic decoration this generator could
// emit into an existing file: it IS the directory a file sits in, so the only way to double the number
// of namespaces is to double the number of directories. Two files per module, both of them load-bearing
// and neither of them padding — see `writeNamespacedModules`.
//
// FOUR, the per-TYPE band's base (`EnumTypesBase`, `ManagedStructTypesBase`, `GenericBasesBase`), and
// for the same test: a module is a DECLARING unit, not a call site. It grows how many namespaces the
// index must hold mirrors for; the qualified REFERENCE count rides along with it (one consumer group
// per module) rather than getting a second knob, because a reference cannot exist without a namespace
// to name and the two would then double in lockstep anyway — which is one knob wearing two names.
let NamespacedModulesBase = 4 as KnobSize

// The subdirectory a module's SECOND file sits in, giving it the MULTI-SEGMENT namespace `ns{m}.inner`.
//
// ⚠ **MULTI-SEGMENT IS NOT A LONGER STRING, IT IS A DIFFERENT PARSER ARM.** A one-dot callee
// (`ns0.helper0()`) is claimed by `Parser.parseQualifiedCall`, which reaches the namespace registry with
// a name it already built for its `Type.method` mangling; a two-dot one (`ns0.inner.deep0()`) is claimed
// by `parseDottedPrimary`'s own arm, which is the one that WALKS the chain and ASSEMBLES the name. A
// corpus with only single-segment namespaces would exercise the probe and never the walk.
let NsInnerDirName = "inner"

// The directory name prefix. A module is `ns{m}/`, so its namespace is `ns{m}` and its inner file's is
// `ns{m}.inner` — the spelling every generated reference below is written with.
let NsModuleDirPrefix = "ns"

// What joins a NAMESPACE's segments in the generated SOURCE — `ns3` + `inner` ⇒ `ns3.inner`.
//
// ⚠ It is not a second copy of the compiler's `Project.NamespaceSeparator`, and the distinction matters
// because this file's whole job is to WRITE MAXON. Every knob here spells `typealias`, `export`,
// `module`, `and` and `as` as literal source text; a namespace's `.` is the same kind of literal, on the
// user's side of the boundary rather than the compiler's. Named only because it is written at two sites
// (the reference group and the module's own header comment) and the two must agree.
let NsQualifierSeparator = "."

// The mask that drives every qualified-alias cast in this knob, and the alias's own upper bound. One
// constant for both, so the cast can never be provably out of range: `(acc and 255)` is an opaque value
// the folder cannot see through (so the guard is emitted, and this is a real ranged-cast site) that
// nonetheless cannot exceed the bound (so a rung that someone one day RUNS does not panic).
let NsAliasMask = 255

// ⭐ **THE ALIAS NAME EVERY MODULE DECLARES, DELIBERATELY THE SAME IN ALL OF THEM.** M modules declaring
// one name is what puts M entries in `RangedAliasRegistry.multiplyDeclared`, and it is the exact
// situation a qualified spelling EXISTS for: the bare `NsScore` is M-way contested and resolves by
// `nameable`'s last-wins, while `ns7.NsScore` names one declaration and only one. A corpus that gave
// each module a unique alias name would register the mirrors and never make them matter.
//
// ⚠ ALL M DECLARATIONS SHARE ONE RANGE, and that is a correctness constraint rather than laziness: the
// bare reference inside a module's own inner file resolves through the contested door, so a per-module
// range would make what that cast admits depend on merge order. The CONTEST is the dimension; the range
// is not.
let NsSharedAliasName = "NsScore"

// The inner file's alias — a SECOND M-way contested name, reached only through a two-segment qualifier
// (`ns7.inner.NsDeep`). Distinct from `NsSharedAliasName` so the one-segment and two-segment mirror
// lookups are asked about different keys and cannot be answered by each other's map entry.
let NsDeepAliasName = "NsDeep"

// The generated names, each written ONCE — this file's own standing discipline (see `LongIfDriver`), and
// it binds harder here than anywhere else in the corpus: every one of these is spelled at a DECLARATION
// site in one generated file and at a REFERENCE site in another, and the reference is written with a
// QUALIFIER that must name the same function. A rename that reached only one of the two would produce a
// rung that does not compile — which `scale-test` reports as a BROKEN RUN, not as a number.
let NsHelperPrefix = "nsHelper"
let NsHiddenPrefix = "nsHidden"
let NsSharedPrefix = "nsShared"
let NsDeepFuncPrefix = "nsDeep"
let NsPrivateAliasPrefix = "NsLocal"

let NamespacesDriver = "scaleNamespaces"

// ===========================================================================================
// Rung layout on disk
// ===========================================================================================

// What separates a rung-relative path's segments in `written`, and what marks a recorded entry as a
// DIRECTORY rather than a file (a trailing one). `pruneRungDir` is the only reader; see there for why the
// generated set has to name directories at all.
//
// ⚠ It is `/` and never the platform separator: these strings are built by this generator and compared
// against strings this generator built, never against a `FilePath`'s rendering — `FilePath.filename()`
// hands back one segment on either host, and the prefix is assembled here.
let RungRelativePathSeparator = "/"

// Shared with ScalePerType, which locates the rungs this generator wrote, and with the runner, which
// names each rung's metrics + output file after it — in the corpus ROOT, never in the rung.
export let RungDirPrefix = "rung"
export let MaxonExtension = ".maxon"

// One generated rung: where it lives and how big it is. `sourceBytes` is the corpus self-check's
// denominator — the question "did the compiler's output grow with its input" needs both.
export type ScaleRung
	export let index as RungIndex
	export let dir as FilePath
	export let sourceBytes as SourceByteCount

	export static function create(index RungIndex, dir FilePath, sourceBytes SourceByteCount) returns ScaleRung
		return Self{index: index, dir: dir, sourceBytes: sourceBytes}
	end 'create'
end 'ScaleRung'

export typealias ScaleRungArray = Array with ScaleRung

// CEILING DIVISION — how many buckets of `perBucket` items it takes to hold `total`, with the last
// bucket taking the remainder.
//
// Written once because the off-by-one is easy to get wrong and because the two knobs that spread
// themselves over MANY FILES (`writeManyFunctions`, `writeInterfaceTypes`) must agree on what
// "distribute N over files" means. Each pairs it with a `>= total` break inside its own loop, so a
// bucket that would run past the end emits nothing rather than a name nothing declares.
function bucketCount(total KnobSize, perBucket KnobSize) returns KnobSize
	return (total + perBucket - 1) / perBucket
end 'bucketCount'

// A knob's size at `rung`: `base << rung`. THE one place the doubling lives, so no knob can drift
// out of step with the others and quietly change what "one rung" means.
function knobAt(base KnobSize, rung RungIndex) returns KnobSize
	var size = base
	for _ in 0 upto rung 'double'
		size = size * 2
	end 'double'
	return size
end 'knobAt'

// Generate the whole ladder under `root`, returning one ScaleRung per rung.
export function generateCorpus(root FilePath, rungs RungCount) returns ScaleRungArray throws ScaleError
	if not Directory.create(root) 'noRoot'
		throw ScaleError.reason("could not create the corpus root {root.toString()}")
	end 'noRoot'

	var ladder = ScaleRungArray.create()
	for rung in 0 upto rungs 'eachRung'
		ladder.push(try generateRung(root, rung: rung as RungIndex))
	end 'eachRung'
	return ladder
end 'generateCorpus'

// Generate one rung: a DIRECTORY of `.maxon` files (shv2's `build <dir>` walks them recursively),
// so the `manyFiles` knob is expressed as real files on disk rather than simulated.
function generateRung(root FilePath, rung RungIndex) returns ScaleRung throws ScaleError
	let dirName = try FilePath.from("{RungDirPrefix}{rung}") otherwise panic("ScaleCorpus: rung dir name must be valid")
	let dir = root.join(dirName)
	if not Directory.create(dir) 'noDir'
		throw ScaleError.reason("could not create the rung directory {dir.toString()}")
	end 'noDir'

	let fileCount = knobAt(FilesBase, rung: rung)
	let funcCount = knobAt(FunctionsBase, rung: rung)
	let localCount = knobAt(LocalsPerFunctionBase, rung: rung)

	var sourceBytes = 0 as SourceByteCount
	var written = StringArray.create()
	// Every generated entry point `main` threads the accumulator through, IN CALL ORDER — filled by
	// the `writeRungFile` calls below as each file is written, read back by `main`. The generation
	// order below IS the call order, which is what lets one append-as-you-go list serve both.
	var drivers = StringArray.create()

	// `a_` / `b_` / ... prefixes only order the directory listing for a human reading a dumped
	// corpus; shv2 merges files in listing order and the merge is order-independent.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "a_seed", source: seedSource(), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threadsNothing)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeManyFunctions(dir, fileCount: fileCount, funcCount: funcCount, written: written, drivers: drivers)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "c_long", source: longFunctionSource(knobAt(LongIfsBase, rung: rung), localCount: localCount), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(LongIfDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "d_blocks", source: deepBlocksSource(knobAt(DeepBlocksBase, rung: rung), localCount: localCount), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(DeepBlocksDriver))
	// The chain writes and registers itself, like `writeManyFunctions`: its driver is its TOP link,
	// whose name is known only to the loop that mints the links (see `writeCallChain`).
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeCallChain(dir, length: knobAt(CallChainBase, rung: rung), written: written, drivers: drivers)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "f_loops", source: pressuredLoopsSource(knobAt(PressuredLoopsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(PressuredDriver))
	// Beside `f_loops` because it is the other half of the same subject — values live across a call —
	// asked of the COLORER rather than of the splitter. See `ConfinedDefsBase`.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "f_confined", source: confinedDefsSource(knobAt(ConfinedDefsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(ConfinedDefsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "g_nested", source: nestedLoopsSource(knobAt(LoopNestBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(NestedDriver))
	// The two COVERAGE files sit together under `h_`, because that is what they both are: fixed-size
	// shapes whose PRESENCE matters and whose COUNT does not. They dilute `assertCorpusCompiled`'s
	// code-growth ratio (a fixed block does not double), so they stay small relative to the knobs.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "h_cover", source: scalarCoverageSource(), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(CoverageDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "h_shifts", source: shiftCoverageSource(), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(ShiftsDriver))
	// The float knobs thread the seed exactly as the int ones do — each takes the opaque `acc` and
	// hands back a `trunc`ed int, so a float function is as load-bearing as any other and no DCE pass
	// could take the float dimension away again.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "i_floats", source: floatOpsSource(knobAt(FloatOpsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(FloatsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "j_fspill", source: floatSpillSource(knobAt(FloatSpillLoopsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(FloatSpillDriver))
	// `scaleGlobalConsts` is threaded exactly as the others are even though the folder eats its
	// constants: the call keeps the DECLARATIONS alive, which is the whole of what that knob measures
	// (see `globalConstsSource`).
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "l_consts", source: globalConstsSource(knobAt(GlobalConstsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(GlobalConstsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "m_globals", source: globalVarsSource(knobAt(GlobalVarsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(GlobalVarsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "n_stypes", source: structTypesSource(knobAt(StructTypesBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(StructTypesDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "o_sallocs", source: structAllocsSource(knobAt(StructAllocsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(StructAllocsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "p_divide", source: intDivideSource(knobAt(IntDivideBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(DivideDriver))
	// Its A1 twin — the THROWING divide shape, on its own knob for the reason `CheckedDivideBase` gives.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "p_cdivide", source: checkedDivideSource(knobAt(CheckedDivideBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(CheckedDivideDriver))
	// Beside the two divide knobs because it is the third door whose per-site cost is BLOCKS rather than
	// ops — and the only one in the corpus that reaches a ranged-`return` guard at all (A2a).
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "p_rreturn", source: rangedReturnSource(knobAt(RangedReturnSitesBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(RangedReturnDriver))
	// The ten knobs for what the language gained between P1.1b and P1.7a. Every one is
	// cross-target-safe: no `where` clause, no witness dispatch, no `Set` — see their section header.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "q_enums", source: enumTypesSource(knobAt(EnumTypesBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(EnumTypesDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "r_match", source: matchSitesSource(knobAt(MatchSitesBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(MatchSitesDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "s_strings", source: stringOpsSource(knobAt(StringOpsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(StringOpsDriver))
	// The REST of `String`'s member surface, on FOUR knobs — one per `dispatchStringMethod` family. Four
	// FILES rather than four drivers in one, because a knob's growth is expressed as a rung file and
	// because each of these is a separately-sized dimension; see `StringSearchOpsBase` for why the
	// families are not one knob.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "s_strsearch", source: stringSearchOpsSource(knobAt(StringSearchOpsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(StringSearchOpsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "s_strindex", source: stringIndexOpsSource(knobAt(StringIndexOpsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(StringIndexOpsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "s_strtrim", source: stringTrimOpsSource(knobAt(StringTrimOpsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(StringTrimOpsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "s_strview", source: stringViewOpsSource(knobAt(StringViewOpsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(StringViewOpsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "s_chars", source: charOpsSource(knobAt(CharOpsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(CharOpsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "t_mstruct", source: managedStructTypesSource(knobAt(ManagedStructTypesBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(ManagedStructsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "u_arrays", source: arrayOpsSource(knobAt(ArrayOpsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(ArrayOpsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "v_marray", source: managedArrayOpsSource(knobAt(ManagedArrayOpsBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(ManagedArrayOpsDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "w_gbase", source: genericBasesSource(knobAt(GenericBasesBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(GenericBasesDriver))
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "x_ginst", source: genericInstancesSource(knobAt(GenericInstancesBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(GenericInstancesDriver))
	// Beside the two generic knobs because it shares their base emitter, and separate from them because
	// it is a per-SITE dimension over FIXED instances (BATCH25): the `try` sites where a substituted
	// managed `T` result's co-own is lifted onto the ok edge. Nothing in this corpus reached that
	// mechanism before it — the rung that built the lift measured Δ0 in all three columns and said so.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "x_trycoown", source: tryCoOwnSource(knobAt(TryCoOwnSitesBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(TryCoOwnDriver))
	// The interface knob writes and registers itself across MANY files, like `writeManyFunctions`: its
	// per-file drivers' names are known only inside the loop that mints them, and holding
	// `InterfacesPerFile` constant is what keeps the per-file interface scan bounded (see its knob).
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeInterfaceTypes(dir, count: knobAt(InterfaceTypesBase, rung: rung), written: written, drivers: drivers)
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "y_ifdisp", source: ifaceDispatchSource(knobAt(IfaceDispatchBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(IfaceDispatchDriver))
	// The `__ManagedMemory` BUFFER SURFACE (BATCH9 G7). Its marks and the array-method dispatches that ASK
	// about them share this ONE file deliberately: `bufferSurfaceValues` is per-PARSER, i.e. per file, so the
	// product a value-keyed regression would cost only exists where both halves are.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "y_bufsurf", source: bufferSurfacesSource(knobAt(BufferSurfaceSitesBase, rung: rung)), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(BufferSurfacesDriver))
	// The NAMESPACE knob (N1b) — the only one that creates DIRECTORIES, because a namespace IS a
	// directory. It writes and registers itself, and registers ONE driver for M modules; see there.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeNamespacedModules(dir, moduleCount: knobAt(NamespacedModulesBase, rung: rung), written: written, drivers: drivers)
	// `z_`, not `k_`, so `main` sorts last however many knobs are added above it. The prefixes are
	// cosmetic (shv2 merges in listing order and the merge is order-independent), but a `main` wedged
	// mid-alphabet would have to be renamed by every future knob, and `pruneRungDir` deletes the old
	// `k_main.maxon` from a stale rung directory rather than leaving it to join the measurement.
	sourceBytes = sourceBytes + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "z_main", source: mainSource(drivers), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threadsNothing)

	try pruneRungDir(dir, generated: written)

	return ScaleRung.create(rung, dir: dir, sourceBytes: sourceBytes)
end 'generateRung'

// WHAT `main` CALLS IN ONE GENERATED FILE — the question every `writeRungFile` must answer, so that a
// knob CANNOT be added without deciding it.
//
// It is a REQUIRED parameter rather than a convention because the failure it prevents is silent. The
// call list used to be re-spelled by hand in `mainSource`, one line per knob; a knob whose author
// forgot the line still compiled, still generated its file, and still measured — right up until a DCE
// pass lands, at which point its now-uncalled function is legitimately deleted and that knob's
// dimension vanishes from the ladder while the numbers go on looking perfectly linear. There is no
// DCE today, which is exactly why this had to be fixed before there is.
union RungDriver
	// The file's single entry point, which `main` threads the accumulator through: `acc = name(acc)`.
	threads(name String)

	// The file contributes no entry point for `main` to thread — either because THIS CALL cannot name one, or
	// because naming one would measure the wrong thing. Three shapes qualify, all real, and the third is why
	// this reads "no entry point to thread" rather than "no entry point this call can name": that narrower
	// wording was true of the first two only, and the third arrived with a name right there in the caller.
	//
	//  • A knob that WRITES AND REGISTERS ITSELF, because its driver names are minted inside its own
	//    loop and are not knowable here — `writeManyFunctions` (N functions in one file, each
	//    registering by name), `writeCallChain` (the driver is the chain's TOP link) and
	//    `writeInterfaceTypes` (one driver per generated file). Each pushes onto `drivers` itself.
	//  • The two files `main` does not thread at all — `a_seed`, whose `scaleSeed()` SEEDS the
	//    accumulator rather than taking it, and `z_main`, which IS `main`.
	//  • ⭐ A file whose functions are reached TRANSITIVELY, through another file's driver, because the
	//    REFERENCE SHAPE is what that knob measures (N1b). `main` threads only `writeNamespacedModules`'
	//    root consumer; every module's own functions hang off it by a QUALIFIED reference, and a driver
	//    entry for them would make `main` call them BARE — measuring precisely what the knob exists to
	//    stop measuring. The DCE property `RungDriver` defends is unaffected: reachable from `main` is
	//    reachable from `main` however many calls away, and the chain is spelled out at that generator.
	threadsNothing
end 'RungDriver'

// Write one generated file, returning its byte length so the caller can total the rung's source
// size without stat-ing the directory back, recording its NAME so the prune below knows it belongs
// there, and registering its entry point so `main` can call it without re-spelling the name.
//
// ⭐ **`name` MAY CARRY A RUNG-RELATIVE DIRECTORY PREFIX (`"ns3/mod"`), WHICH IS HOW THE NAMESPACE KNOB
// WRITES INTO A SUBDIRECTORY (N1b).** `FilePath.join` normalizes the separator to the host's, so the
// PATH is right on either host, while `written` records the `/`-joined RELATIVE spelling — which is
// exactly the key `pruneRungDir` rebuilds as it walks. Nothing else about this routine changes, and no
// flat-directory call site moves: a name with no separator IS its own relative path.
//
// ⚠ The caller owns creating the directory. This writes a file and does not mkdir, because the knob that
// needs a subdirectory also needs it recorded in `written` — one caller, one place, rather than a
// create-if-missing here that would leave the prune's set to be filled in somewhere else.
function writeRungFile(dir FilePath, name String, source String, written StringArray, drivers StringArray, driver RungDriver) returns SourceByteCount throws ScaleError
	let fileName = "{name}{MaxonExtension}"
	let path = dir.join(fileName)
	try File.writeText(path, content: source) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not write {path.toString()}")
	written.push(fileName)

	match driver 'driver'
		threads(driverName) then drivers.push(driverName)
		threadsNothing then break 'driver'
	end 'driver'

	return source.byteLength() as SourceByteCount
end 'writeRungFile'

// Delete everything in the rung directory TREE that this run did not just generate — see the header.
//
// A rung directory is not a place, it is a VALUE: it is exactly the program this rung's knobs
// describe. Anything else in it — a stale `b_funcs_9.maxon` from a run with a bigger `FilesBase`, an
// executable an older version of the runner wrote there — is compiled or listed by `build <dir>`, and
// so is part of the measurement, silently and unreproducibly.
//
// ⭐ **IT RECURSES, BECAUSE A RUNG NOW HAS SUBDIRECTORIES AND A NAMESPACE IS A DIRECTORY (N1b).** The
// invariant is unchanged and now covers the whole tree: every `.maxon` file under the rung is one this
// run wrote. `generated` therefore holds `/`-joined RUNG-RELATIVE paths (`"ns3/mod.maxon"`) rather than
// bare filenames, and names each generated DIRECTORY with a trailing separator (`"ns3/"`) — a directory
// has to be IN the set for the same reason a file does, or the walk could not tell a namespace this run
// created from one left behind by a run with a bigger `NamespacedModulesBase`.
//
// A stale SUBDIRECTORY is still a THROW rather than a delete, at every depth: `build <dir>` walks
// recursively, so its contents would be compiled, and the stdlib has no recursive remove with which to
// fix it. Refusing with the path is the honest answer; the fix is one `rm -rf` and it should be a
// person's decision, not this generator's.
function pruneRungDir(dir FilePath, generated StringArray) throws ScaleError
	try pruneRungSubtree(dir, generated: generated, prefix: "")
end 'pruneRungDir'

// One level of the prune, and its own recursion. `prefix` is the `/`-joined path of `dir` relative to
// the rung root, with a trailing separator (empty at the root) — so `"{prefix}{entry.filename()}"` is
// exactly the key `writeRungFile` and `writeNamespacedModules` recorded.
function pruneRungSubtree(dir FilePath, generated StringArray, prefix String) throws ScaleError
	let entries = try Directory.list(dir) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not list the rung directory {dir.toString()}")

	for entry in entries 'each'
		let relative = "{prefix}{entry.filename()}"

		if Directory.isDirectory(entry) 'subdir'
			if not generated.contains("{relative}{RungRelativePathSeparator}") 'staleSubdir'
				throw ScaleError.reason("{entry.toString()} is a directory inside a rung that this generator did not write, and `build <dir>` walks recursively — so it would be COMPILED as part of this rung and the measurement would be of a program this generator did not write. Delete the corpus root and re-run.")
			end 'staleSubdir'

			try pruneRungSubtree(entry, generated: generated, prefix: "{relative}{RungRelativePathSeparator}")
			continue
		end 'subdir'

		if generated.contains(relative) 'generated'
			continue
		end 'generated'

		try File.delete(entry) otherwise throw ScaleError.reason("could not delete the stale file {entry.toString()} from the rung directory, and leaving it there would put it in the measurement")
	end 'each'
end 'pruneRungSubtree'

// ===========================================================================================
// The knobs, as source
// ===========================================================================================

// The seed, and the opaque callee — the two generated functions OTHER emitters name. `main` seeds the
// accumulator with `SeedName` (which is why `a_seed` is `threadsNothing`: it PRODUCES the accumulator
// rather than taking it), and the two spill knobs call `OpaqueCalleeName` across their live values.
// Named here, beside the generator that defines them, so a rename cannot leave a caller behind.
let SeedName = "scaleSeed"
let OpaqueCalleeName = "scaleOpaque"

// THE ORIGIN OF EVERY VALUE IN THE CORPUS, and the reason none of it folds away.
//
// `acc` is a loop-carried `var`: at the top of the loop it is a phi of its initializer and what the
// body last wrote it. `foldConstOperands` moves a CONSTANT operand into an immediate; it cannot
// prove a phi is a constant, and shv2 has no SCCP that could. So `scaleSeed()` is opaque, its
// return value is opaque, and everything downstream that derives from it is opaque too.
//
// This is the same trick `specs-shv2/register-pressure.md` uses to hold 17 values live without the
// folder eating them.
// ⭐⭐ **THE HEADER OF A FUNCTION `z_main` CALLS — AND `z_main` IS IN ANOTHER FILE (N1a).** A cross-file
// call to a file-private callee is E3008, so every name that lands in `drivers` (plus the seed, which
// `mainSource` calls directly) has to be `export`ed. Spelled ONCE, beside the array that decides
// membership, so a knob added later cannot forget it — the failure it prevents is a corpus that does not
// COMPILE, which is the one failure `scale-test` reports as a broken RUN rather than as a number.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY OTHER GENERATED FUNCTION STAYS FILE-PRIVATE ON PURPOSE.** The visibility check's `global` arm
// is an enum compare and its `file` arm is a path-string compare — the expensive one, and the one real
// code takes most often. Exporting the whole corpus would make the ladder measure only the cheap arm.
// ⚠ **`public`, NOT `export`, AND THAT IS FORCED BY `checkUnusedExports` RATHER THAN CHOSEN.** A generated
// driver is called by the rung's main file, but a generated CHAIN LINK is called only by the link above it
// in its own file — so under E3092 every `scaleChainN` was "never referenced outside its declaring file"
// and the whole ladder became a BROKEN RUN. That diagnostic is correct about the generated code; what the
// generated code means is "this is the corpus's surface, do not ask who calls it", which is what `public`
// says. Visibility is unchanged (`isVisibleFrom` answers identically for both), so the arm-cost note below
// still holds exactly.
//
// ⚠ **THE COST THIS HIDES, STATED SO A ZERO IS NOT READ AS EVIDENCE:** a `public` declaration is filtered
// at `UnusedExportCheck.tierIsAudited` and never reaches `TrackedDecls.add` or the emit walk, so this
// ladder measures that pass's SEED WALK and its whole REFERENCE COLLECTION — which is the bulk — but not
// its indexing or its emission. A regression confined to those two would read flat here.
function exportedFunctionHeader(name String, parameters String) returns String
	return "public function {name}({parameters}) returns int\n"
end 'exportedFunctionHeader'

// The shape every knob's DRIVER has: it takes the threaded accumulator and hands one back.
function driverHeader(name String) returns String
	return exportedFunctionHeader(name, parameters: "a int")
end 'driverHeader'

function seedSource() returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus. The opaque seed every other value derives from.\n")
	out.append(exportedFunctionHeader(SeedName, parameters: ""))
	out.append("\tvar acc = {SeedInitial}\n")
	out.append("\tvar i = 0\n")
	out.append("\twhile i < {GeneratedLoopTrips} 'seedLoop'\n")
	out.append("\t\tacc = acc + i * {SeedStep}\n")
	out.append("\t\ti = i + 1\n")
	out.append("\tend 'seedLoop'\n")
	out.append("\treturn acc\n")
	out.append("end '{SeedName}'\n")
	out.append("\n")
	// A callee with a branch in it, so a call to it is neither trivially foldable nor free. Every
	// pressured loop calls this ACROSS its live accumulators, which is what forces the spills.
	out.append(driverHeader(OpaqueCalleeName))
	out.append("\tif a > {OpaqueBigThreshold} 'big'\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn a - {OpaqueBigThreshold}\n")
	out.append("\tend 'big'\n")
	out.append("\treturn a + 1\n")
	out.append("end '{OpaqueCalleeName}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'seedSource'

// The branch constant in `scaleOpaque`. Named because `opaqueCalleeValue` decides which arm a caller
// takes by comparing against it, and the two must be the same number.
let OpaqueBigThreshold = 100

// What `scaleOpaque` hands back for `argument` — the tracked twin of the two-arm body emitted directly
// above it, and the ONE place a caller's expectation of it is derived.
//
// ⚠ `scaleOpaque` is NOT a driver and deliberately does NOT witness itself: `main` never threads it, and
// a self-witnessing callee would have to return its ARGUMENT, which is the one thing its two callers
// need it not to do. It is witnessed TRANSITIVELY and completely — every trip of every pressured and
// float-spill loop folds its answer into a total this generator predicts, so a miscompiled `scaleOpaque`
// moves both of those witnesses.
function opaqueCalleeValue(argument TrackedValue) returns TrackedValue
	if argument > OpaqueBigThreshold 'big'
		return argument - OpaqueBigThreshold
	end 'big'
	return argument + 1
end 'opaqueCalleeValue'

// The `manyFunctions` + `manyFiles` knobs, which are one piece of work: N functions spread over M
// real files. Both double per rung, so functions-per-file is constant and the two knobs stay
// independent — M is what `load`/`merge`/the query spine see, N is what the pass pipeline sees.
//
// It appends every function's NAME to `drivers`, in the order it generated them. `main` calls them
// back from that list — so the enumeration (the ceiling division and the `>= funcCount` break) lives
// HERE, once. When it lived twice, `main`'s copy had to reproduce this one's names exactly or it would
// call an undefined `sfX_Y` (a broken run) or skip one (an uncalled function a future DCE deletes,
// silently flattening the knob — the very failure `RungDriver` now generalises to every knob).
//
// This is the ONE file whose functions register individually rather than through `writeRungFile`'s
// `driver` parameter: it writes N files holding N*k functions, so there is no single entry point to
// declare, and the names are known only inside the loop that mints them.
function writeManyFunctions(dir FilePath, fileCount KnobSize, funcCount KnobSize, written StringArray, drivers StringArray) returns SourceByteCount throws ScaleError
	let perFile = bucketCount(funcCount, perBucket: fileCount)

	var total = 0 as SourceByteCount
	for fileIndex in 0 upto fileCount 'eachFile'
		var out = StringBuilder.create()
		out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the many-small-functions knob, file {fileIndex}.\n")
		for slot in 0 upto perFile 'eachFunc'
			let funcIndex = fileIndex * perFile + slot
			if funcIndex >= funcCount 'past'
				break
			end 'past'
			drivers.push(smallFunctionName(fileIndex, slot: slot))
			out.append(smallFunctionSource(fileIndex, slot: slot, salt: funcIndex))
		end 'eachFunc'
		total = total + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "b_funcs_{fileIndex}", source: out.build(), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threadsNothing)
	end 'eachFile'
	return total
end 'writeManyFunctions'

// One small function of the realistic-codebase shape: a couple of arithmetic ops and a guarded
// branch. Everything derives from the parameter `a`, which is a call argument at every call site and
// therefore opaque to the folder.
//
// The guard is a SHORT-CIRCUIT `and`. This is the knob that doubles the FUNCTION COUNT, so it is
// where a per-function cost shows up — and short-circuit lowering is not free: it mints two blocks, a
// merge phi and a seed literal per `and`. The corpus contained no `and`/`or` at all until 2026-07-14,
// which meant the compare/branch fusion path — the commonest conditional shape in the language — was
// measured by nothing.
// ⭐ THE `grew` ARM IS TAKEN BY CONSTRUCTION, AND THE FALL-THROUGH IS THE WITNESS. With `c = 3(a + k)`
// and `k >= 1`, `c > a` is `2a + 3k > 0` and `b < c` is `2a + 2k > 0` — both true for every `a >= 0`,
// and `a` is the seed. So the guard's arm is known, `c - a` is `2a + 3k`, and the path that returns the
// failure offset is the one that cannot be reached: this single site witnesses the short-circuit `and`'s
// OUTCOME and the arithmetic behind it at once. (The old fall-through `return c + a` is what the
// failure path replaces; nothing else about the body moves.)
function smallFunctionSource(fileIndex KnobIndex, slot KnobIndex, salt KnobIndex) returns String
	let name = smallFunctionName(fileIndex, slot: slot)
	// `salt` varies the constants across functions so they are not textually identical — identical
	// bodies would be a fair benchmark of nothing but the parser's cache.
	let k = salt mod 7 + 1
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\n")
	out.append(driverHeader(name))
	out.append("\tlet b = a + {k}\n")
	out.append("\tlet c = b * 3\n")
	out.append("\tif c > a and b < c 'grew'\n")
	out.append("\t\tif c - a == {witnessExpr(2 * corpusSeedValue() + 3 * k)} 'witness'\n")
	out.append("\t\t\treturn a\n")
	out.append("\t\tend 'witness'\n")
	out.append("\tend 'grew'\n")
	out.append("\treturn a + {CorpusWitnessFailedOffset}\n")
	out.append("end '{name}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'smallFunctionSource'

function smallFunctionName(fileIndex KnobIndex, slot KnobIndex) returns String
	return "sf{fileIndex}_{slot}"
end 'smallFunctionName'

// Each knob's ENTRY POINT is named once, in a constant like this one, and read by the two parties that
// must agree: the generator below, which emits the function under this name, and `generateRung`, which
// registers it as the knob's `RungDriver` so `main` calls it. They cannot drift, because there is
// nothing to drift from. (The two knobs that mint their names in a loop — `writeManyFunctions` and
// `writeCallChain` — register from inside that loop instead, for the same reason.)
let LongIfDriver = "scaleLongIf"

// The `longFunction` knob: ONE function, N sequential `if`s, over a bounded working set and
// `localCount` scope-fillers. At rung 5 this is the 3,200-`if` benchmark ARCHITECTURE.md quotes a
// measurement for. It grows OPS in a near-straight line; the CFG grows with it but stays a simple
// chain.
//
// Three things here are load-bearing, and the knob measured nothing without the first two (see
// `LocalsPerFunctionBase`):
//
//   • `localCount` SCOPE-FILLERS, so V climbs and the parser's per-branch O(vars-in-scope) snapshot
//     has something to climb WITH. Every filler is in scope at every `if` below it, and there are V of
//     them at the top rung. It used to be a fixed handful.
//   • Each branch mutates a ROTATING SUBSET of the bounded WORKING set, never the whole thing. A branch
//     that wrote every var in scope would make "cost O(vars in scope)" and "cost O(vars assigned)" the
//     same number, and the corpus could not tell the two apart — precisely the distinction that
//     decides whether a write-trail is worth building. Here in-scope is V+6 and assigned is ≤3.
//   • The guard is a SHORT-CIRCUIT `and`, which is what real code writes and which — measured
//     2026-07-14, while fixing the `const` flags bug — appeared in this corpus exactly nowhere. A
//     codegen change to the commonest conditional shape in the language was invisible to the instrument.
//
// ⭐⭐ **ITS ARITHMETIC IS UNCHANGED, AND THAT IS A DECISION.** `w{q} = w{q} + w{r}` is a coupled
// six-variable recurrence that DOUBLES every `WorkingLocalsPerFunction` branches, so by rung 1 the
// tracked values leave i64 and wrap — 2^533 worth of wrapping at rung 5. It would have been easy to
// replace it with a bounded increment and buy a tidy closed form; that would have taken a reg-reg ADD
// and a reg-reg COMPARE out of 3,200 sites at the top rung, which is a reduction in what the knob
// PRICES, and this rung's whole purpose forbids that. So the emitted text is byte-for-byte what it was
// and the GENERATOR follows it instead: the same update and the same guard, applied to `tracked` in the
// loop that writes each site. Maxon `int` is i64 and MEASURED to wrap identically under both compilers,
// so a tracked value that overflows is still the value the program computes.
function longFunctionSource(ifCount KnobSize, localCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the long-function knob — {ifCount} sequential ifs over {WorkingLocalsPerFunction} working locals and {localCount} scope-fillers.\n")
	out.append(driverHeader(LongIfDriver))
	out.append("\tvar wit = 0\n")
	var tracked = TrackedValueArray.create()
	out.append(scopedLocalsDecl(localCount, tracked: tracked))
	for i in 0 upto ifCount 'eachIf'
		let p = i mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		let q = (i + 1) mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		let r = (i + 2) mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		out.append("\tif w{p} > {i} and w{q} < w{r} 'c{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tw{p} = w{p} + {i mod 5 + 1}\n")
		out.append("\t\tw{q} = w{q} + w{r}\n")
		out.append("\tend 'c{i}'\n")

		if trackedAt(tracked, slot: p) > i and trackedAt(tracked, slot: q) < trackedAt(tracked, slot: r) 'branchTaken'
			trackedSet(tracked, slot: p, value: trackedAt(tracked, slot: p) + (i mod 5 + 1))
			trackedSet(tracked, slot: q, value: trackedAt(tracked, slot: q) + trackedAt(tracked, slot: r))
		end 'branchTaken'
	end 'eachIf'
	out.append(workingLocalsSumAndWitness(LongIfDriver, tracked: tracked))
	return out.build()
end 'longFunctionSource'

let DeepBlocksDriver = "scaleDeepBlocks"

// The `deepBlocks` knob: ONE function whose BLOCK count is huge. Each if/else is three blocks (then,
// else, continuation), so this lands near 3x its knob — ~19,200 blocks at rung 5, which is the
// shape that used to SIGSEGV and the reason "no CFG traversal may recurse" is a rule rather than a
// preference. Distinct from `longFunction`: that grows ops along a chain, this grows the CFG. It
// carries the same `localCount` scope-fillers, so the V dimension is measured against a DIAMOND CFG
// here and a straight chain there.
// ⚠ THE TWO ARMS MUTATE DIFFERENT WORKING LOCALS, DELIBERATELY. That is what makes the merge do real
// work: each arm leaves a different subset changed, so the join genuinely has to reconcile them. When
// both arms wrote the SAME single `acc`, every merge was the trivial one-phi case and the
// snapshot-and-reconcile cost this knob is supposed to provoke never appeared.
function deepBlocksSource(ifElseCount KnobSize, localCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the deep-blocks knob — {ifElseCount} if/elses, ~{ifElseCount * 3} blocks, {WorkingLocalsPerFunction} working locals and {localCount} scope-fillers.\n")
	out.append(driverHeader(DeepBlocksDriver))
	out.append("\tvar wit = 0\n")
	var tracked = TrackedValueArray.create()
	out.append(scopedLocalsDecl(localCount, tracked: tracked))
	for i in 0 upto ifElseCount 'eachIfElse'
		let p = i mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		let q = (i + 1) mod WorkingLocalsPerFunction
		out.append("\tif w{p} > {i} 'd{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tw{p} = w{p} + {i mod 3 + 1}\n")
		out.append("\tend 'd{i}' else 'e{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tw{q} = w{q} - {i mod 2 + 1}\n")
		out.append("\tend 'e{i}'\n")

		// The same two arms, applied to `tracked` — which arm runs is a property of the values, so the
		// tracker cannot assume one. (It happens to take the `then` arm at every rung today, because the
		// fillers leave every `w` far above any `i` the knob reaches and the `then` arm only raises it.
		// That is not written down as an invariant anywhere: if a retune ever makes an `else` run, this
		// follows it and the witness stays correct, which is the point of tracking rather than deriving.)
		if trackedAt(tracked, slot: p) > i 'branchTaken'
			trackedSet(tracked, slot: p, value: trackedAt(tracked, slot: p) + (i mod 3 + 1))
		end 'branchTaken' else 'branchNotTaken'
			trackedSet(tracked, slot: q, value: trackedAt(tracked, slot: q) - (i mod 2 + 1))
		end 'branchNotTaken'
	end 'eachIfElse'
	out.append(workingLocalsSumAndWitness(DeepBlocksDriver, tracked: tracked))
	return out.build()
end 'deepBlocksSource'

// One link of the chain, by index — the `smallFunctionName` pattern: ONE name function, read by the
// generator that emits the links and by the driver registration below. The chain takes a name
// FUNCTION where every other knob takes a name constant, because it generates N functions rather
// than one.
function chainLinkName(link KnobIndex) returns String
	return "scaleChain{link}"
end 'chainLinkName'

// The `callChain` knob: N functions, each calling the one below it and holding values LIVE ACROSS
// that call. The live values are the point — a bare call chain would measure call lowering, but
// values live across a call can only live in the 5 callee-saved registers, so this is what
// exercises the cross-call spill bracket.
//
// ⭐ IT WRITES AND REGISTERS IN ONE FUNCTION, for the same reason `writeManyFunctions` does: this is
// the other knob whose entry point's name is known only inside the loop that mints it, so the name
// is READ OFF THE GENERATOR rather than recomputed from the knob's size. `topName` is literally the
// last link this loop emitted.
//
// This used to be a `callChainSource` + `chainTopName(length)` pair returning `chainLinkName(length
// - 1)`, which was the file's own signature bug: "the links are numbered 0..length-1" was written
// TWICE — once as this loop's bound, once as that subtraction — with nothing making the copies
// agree. The failure was SILENT, which is what made it worth closing: a stale `chainTopName` names a
// MIDDLE link, `main` still calls a real function, everything still compiles, and the knob quietly
// measures a shorter chain than the ladder says it does. Now the two cannot disagree, because there
// is only one of them.
// ⭐ **THE BASE LINK IS THE ONE FUNCTION HERE THAT DOES NOT WITNESS ITSELF, AND THAT IS THE POINT.**
// `scaleChain0` is a single `a + 1`; a witness on it would compare `a + 1` against `a + 1`, which is two
// spellings of one expression and catches nothing — a check that cannot fail, in the rung that exists to
// remove them. It is witnessed instead by the link ABOVE it, whose `z` must arrive as `a + 1` exactly;
// every link from 1 up returns its argument unchanged, so link 2 and above expect a bare `a`. A
// miscompiled base therefore moves link 1's witness, which is a real failure and not a tautology.
//
// ⚠ A one-link chain would make the base the DRIVER, and a driver must hand back its argument. The knob
// cannot reach that (`CallChainBase` is 8 and rungs only double it), and the check below says so rather
// than leaving the next person to retune it to 1 and get a rung that exits 1 for no visible reason.
function writeCallChain(dir FilePath, length KnobSize, written StringArray, drivers StringArray) returns SourceByteCount throws ScaleError
	if length < MinCallChainLength 'baseWouldBeTheDriver'
		panic("ScaleCorpus.writeCallChain: a chain of {length} makes the un-witnessed base link the knob's DRIVER, and a driver must return its argument — raise CallChainBase back to at least {MinCallChainLength}")
	end 'baseWouldBeTheDriver'

	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the call-chain knob — {length} chained calls, values live across each.\n")
	// The base of the chain: takes no call, so the recursion of generated text terminates.
	out.append(driverHeader(chainLinkName(0)))
	out.append("\treturn a + {ChainBaseIncrement}\n")
	out.append("end '{chainLinkName(0)}'\n")

	var topName = chainLinkName(0)
	// What THIS link's callee hands back: the base's `a + 1` for link 1, and a bare `a` from link 2 up,
	// because every link above the base returns its argument once its own witness holds. Carried rather
	// than recomputed, so the two facts — what a link returns, and what its caller expects — are one.
	var calleeValue = corpusSeedValue() + ChainBaseIncrement

	for i in 1 upto length 'eachLink'
		let linkName = chainLinkName(i)
		let x = corpusSeedValue() + (i mod 9 + 1)
		let y = corpusSeedValue() * 2
		out.append("\n")
		out.append(driverHeader(linkName))
		out.append("\tvar wit = 0\n")
		out.append("\tlet x = a + {i mod 9 + 1}\n")
		out.append("\tlet y = a * 2\n")
		out.append("\tlet z = {chainLinkName(i - 1)}(a)\n")
		out.append(driverWitnessTail(linkName, valueExpr: "x + y + z", expected: x + y + calleeValue))
		topName = linkName
		calleeValue = corpusSeedValue()
	end 'eachLink'

	return try writeRungFile(dir, name: "e_chain", source: out.build(), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(topName))
end 'writeCallChain'

// The base link's only arithmetic, and the amount link 1 expects to see arrive in `z`. One constant, read
// by the emitter and by the tracker beside it.
let ChainBaseIncrement = 1

// Below this the chain's un-witnessed base link would BE the driver — see `writeCallChain`.
let MinCallChainLength = 2 as KnobSize

let PressuredDriver = "scalePressured"

// The `pressuredLoops` knob: ONE function, N loops, each holding PressuredValuesPerLoop
// accumulators live across a call to `scaleOpaque`. The splitter must spill the accumulators that do
// not fit the five callee-saved registers, so this is the knob that exercises the splitter hardest.
//
// ⚠ IT IS LINEAR NOW — see `PressuredLoopsBase`, which carries the full history. It USED to be the
// superlinear knob, because the splitter's driver recomputed liveness from scratch after every split
// (O(function × splits)); `49b160ce2` made that driver incremental and `regalloc:splitting` /
// `regalloc:liveness` both read ~x2.0 since. Do not restore a "KNOWN SUPERLINEAR" claim here without
// measuring it: that claim outlived its bug twice already (once here, once at the parser).
//
// It does NOT trip E5001 — see PressuredValuesPerLoop.
function pressuredLoopsSource(loopCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the pressured-loops knob — {loopCount} loops, {PressuredValuesPerLoop} values live across a call in each.\n")
	out.append(driverHeader(PressuredDriver))
	out.append("\tvar wit = 0\n")
	out.append("\tvar total = a\n")
	var total = corpusSeedValue()

	for loopIndex in 0 upto loopCount 'eachLoop'
		// Fresh accumulator names per loop, so the loops' live ranges do not overlap and peak
		// pressure stays at one loop's worth however many loops there are.
		var live = TrackedValueArray.create()
		for v in 0 upto PressuredValuesPerLoop 'declValues'
			out.append("\tvar s{loopIndex}_{v} = a + {v + 1}\n")
			live.push(corpusSeedValue() + v + 1)
		end 'declValues'
		out.append("\tvar i{loopIndex} = 0\n")
		out.append("\twhile i{loopIndex} < {GeneratedLoopTrips} 'p{loopIndex}'\n")
		// The call is what makes the loop pressured: every accumulator is live across it, and a
		// value live across a call is forbidden all 9 caller-saved registers.
		out.append("\t\tlet r{loopIndex} = {OpaqueCalleeName}(i{loopIndex})\n")
		for v in 0 upto PressuredValuesPerLoop 'updateValues'
			out.append("\t\ts{loopIndex}_{v} = s{loopIndex}_{v} + r{loopIndex}\n")
		end 'updateValues'
		out.append("\t\ti{loopIndex} = i{loopIndex} + 1\n")
		out.append("\tend 'p{loopIndex}'\n")

		// The loop this function just wrote runs `GeneratedLoopTrips` times with its counter as
		// `scaleOpaque`'s argument, and every live value takes every trip's answer. The TEXT of the body
		// is emitted once and EXECUTED that many times, so the tracker's loop is here rather than beside
		// the append above — it is the same fact, counted where the count is.
		for trip in 0 upto GeneratedLoopTrips 'eachTrip'
			for v in 0 upto PressuredValuesPerLoop 'perTrip'
				trackedSet(live, slot: v, value: trackedAt(live, slot: v) + opaqueCalleeValue(trip))
			end 'perTrip'
		end 'eachTrip'

		for v in 0 upto PressuredValuesPerLoop 'sumValues'
			out.append("\ttotal = total + s{loopIndex}_{v}\n")
			total = total + trackedAt(live, slot: v)
		end 'sumValues'
	end 'eachLoop'

	out.append(driverWitnessTail(PressuredDriver, valueExpr: "total", expected: total))
	return out.build()
end 'pressuredLoopsSource'

let ConfinedDefsDriver = "scaleConfinedDefs"

// The `confinedDefs` knob — see `ConfinedDefsBase` for the shape and for why it exists.
//
// ⚠ WHY IT IS NOT `pressuredLoopsSource` WITH ANOTHER PARAMETER, though both end in "values live across
// a call": they provoke DIFFERENT PASSES, and the difference is the whole reason for a second knob.
// `pressuredLoops` makes the SPLITTER work — its accumulators genuinely outnumber the callee-saved half
// at a point, so the peak-finder sees an over-pool point and Hall's verdict there is OVERFLOW. Here the
// verdict at every point is FEASIBLE and the splitter owes nothing; what fails is the greedy's ORDER,
// and the only pass that runs is the colorer's repair. Merging them would make one knob whose tuning
// decides which of two passes it measures, and the two must move independently.
//
// It is STRAIGHT-LINE, deliberately: a loop would put the confined values on a back edge and turn them
// into phis, which is `pressuredLoops`' shape again. The confinement here is created by ordinary
// sequential code, which is the point of the defect it was written for.
function confinedDefsSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	// Read ONCE, so the header line and the three emit loops cannot disagree about how many values this
	// knob is generating — the counts are properties of the target, not of the group they are in.
	let unconstrained = confinedUnconstrainedInts()
	let crossing = confinedCrossCallDefs()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the confined-defs knob — {groupCount} group(s), each holding {unconstrained} int(s) that cross no call and {crossing} that do.\n")
	out.append(driverHeader(ConfinedDefsDriver))
	out.append("\tvar wit = 0\n")
	out.append("\tvar total = a\n")
	var total = corpusSeedValue()

	for groupIndex in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		// Fresh names per group, so the groups' live ranges do not overlap and peak pressure stays at one
		// group's worth however many groups there are — `pressuredLoops`' rule, in straight-line code.
		out.append("\tlet n{groupIndex} = {OpaqueCalleeName}(a)\n")
		let base = opaqueCalleeValue(corpusSeedValue())

		// The unconstrained values, and the SUM expression that keeps every one of them live to the call
		// below. The expression is built as the values are declared, so the two cannot fall out of step.
		var sumExpr = StringBuilder.create()
		var sum = 0 as TrackedValue
		for v in 0 upto unconstrained 'declValues'
			out.append("\tlet u{groupIndex}_{v} = n{groupIndex} + {v + 1}\n")
			if v > 0 'notTheFirstTerm'
				sumExpr.append(" + ")
			end 'notTheFirstTerm'
			sumExpr.append("u{groupIndex}_{v}")
			sum = sum + base + v + 1
		end 'declValues'

		// The confined half: a product of each of the first `crossing` values, live ACROSS the call below
		// because `total` reads it after. Each is a two-address `*` whose input is still live, which is
		// what makes it hold a register of its own at an op it is in no live set of.
		var productExpr = StringBuilder.create()
		var products = 0 as TrackedValue
		for v in 0 upto crossing 'declProducts'
			out.append("\tlet m{groupIndex}_{v} = u{groupIndex}_{v} * 2\n")
			productExpr.append(" + m{groupIndex}_{v}")
			products = products + (base + v + 1) * 2
		end 'declProducts'

		out.append("\tlet s{groupIndex} = {sumExpr.build()}\n")
		out.append("\tlet c{groupIndex} = {OpaqueCalleeName}(s{groupIndex})\n")
		out.append("\ttotal = total{productExpr.build()} + c{groupIndex}\n")
		total = total + products + opaqueCalleeValue(sum)
	end 'eachGroup'

	out.append(driverWitnessTail(ConfinedDefsDriver, valueExpr: "total", expected: total))
	return out.build()
end 'confinedDefsSource'

// The condition every nested loop tests. Large enough that the loop is not trivially one iteration.
// The bound is irrelevant to what is MEASURED — only the loop's SHAPE is compiled — but since BATCH10
// it is also what the nest actually executes, ~10^6 additions, which costs microseconds. It is
// compared against the opaque `acc`, so the folder cannot see through it.
let NestedLoopLimit = 1000000

let NestedDriver = "scaleNested"

// The `loopNest` knob: ONE function, ONE chain of loops NESTED to `depth`. Distinct from
// `pressuredLoops` (loop COUNT at depth 1): this grows loop DEPTH, which is what the loop-phi token
// pre-scan climbs — `parseWhileStatement` re-scans a loop's whole token extent, and a loop nested
// inside another is re-scanned once per enclosing loop, so this drives that scan O(depth x tokens).
//
// EVERY loop tests and mutates the SINGLE carried accumulator `acc`. That is deliberate: it keeps peak
// register pressure at one value however deep the nest goes (a per-level counter would be live across
// every enclosing loop and trip E5001 at depth ~14 — see `LoopNestBase`), and it keeps the loops
// fold-resistant, because `acc` originates in the opaque seed.
//
// ⚠ EXPECT THIS TO READ ~LINEAR IN MEMORY EVEN IF THE SCAN IS QUADRATIC IN TIME. The scan allocates
// ~one carried-position list per loop (linear in depth) and each loop mints one phi for `acc` (linear in
// depth); its O(depth x tokens) cost is spent WALKING tokens, which allocates nothing. scale-test
// measures memory only, so a time-only superlinearity is invisible here BY DESIGN — this knob is the
// manifest's worked example of that blind spot, not a curve expected to bend.
// ⭐ **THIS KNOB IS WITNESSED BY A CLOSED FORM RATHER THAN BY TRACKING, AND THE CHOICE WAS MEASURED.**
// Tracking would mean stepping the generator through every trip the nest takes — about 10^6 per rung,
// six million across a default ladder — to re-derive a value three lines of arithmetic already give.
// The nest's whole execution is: descend once, adding `d + 1` at each of the `depth` levels; then repeat
// the INNERMOST body, which adds `depth`, until the accumulator reaches the limit; then unwind, every
// enclosing condition now false. So the answer is the first value at or above the limit that the descent
// total can reach in steps of `depth`. Nothing here is a second model of the loop — it is the loop's
// exit condition, solved instead of iterated.
//
// ⚠ IT ASSUMES THE DESCENT COMPLETES, and says so rather than assuming it quietly: if the descent total
// ever reached the limit on the way down, the inner levels would never be entered and the shape would be
// a different one. `depth` tops out at 256 on an eight-rung ladder, so the descent is at most ~33,000
// against a limit of a million; the check below is what keeps that true if either constant moves.
//
// ⚠ **`NestedLoopLimit` IS DELIBERATELY NOT LOWERED.** Now that the rungs are RUN, the 10^6 trips cost
// microseconds — and lowering it would shrink an `imm32` to an `imm8` and move every byte column in the
// ladder for nothing. The old hang was never this bound; it was an accumulator that arrived negative.
function nestedLoopsSource(depth KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the loop-nest knob — one chain of {depth} loops nested to depth {depth}, all carrying one accumulator.\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(NestedDriver))

	// One pass: append each loop's opener, and PREPEND its `end` to a separate string. Prepending is
	// what puts the closers in innermost-first order (`n{depth-1}` … `n0`) with no reverse index — a
	// backward count would have to reach 0, which a ranged loop counter cannot represent.
	var closes = ""
	var descended = corpusSeedValue()
	for d in 0 upto depth 'open'
		out.append("{tabs(d + 1)}while acc < {NestedLoopLimit} 'n{d}'\n")
		out.append("{tabs(d + 2)}acc = acc + {d + 1}\n")
		closes = "{tabs(d + 1)}end 'n{d}'\n{closes}"
		descended = descended + (d + 1)
	end 'open'

	if descended >= NestedLoopLimit 'descentOutranTheLimit'
		panic("ScaleCorpus.nestedLoopsSource: descending {depth} levels reaches {descended}, at or past the limit {NestedLoopLimit} — the inner levels would never be entered and this knob would stop being a nest. Raise NestedLoopLimit or lower LoopNestBase")
	end 'descentOutranTheLimit'

	// The innermost body then adds `depth` until the limit is reached — a ceiling division, and the one
	// place this knob's answer is stated.
	let remaining = NestedLoopLimit - descended
	let settled = descended + depth * ((remaining + depth - 1) / depth)

	out.append(closes)
	out.append(driverWitnessTail(NestedDriver, valueExpr: "acc", expected: settled))
	return out.build()
end 'nestedLoopsSource'

// The float knob's OPAQUE ORIGIN, and the conversion band's first direction.
//
// `a + 0.5` promotes the opaque int PARAMETER into the float domain, so a float seed is
// fold-resistant for exactly the reason every other value in this corpus is — shv2 has no
// interprocedural constant propagation, so `a` is opaque at every call site. A pile of arithmetic on
// float LITERALS would fold to nothing and measure an empty compile, which is this file's founding
// trap.
//
// It is also the int→float promotion itself (`5 + 2.0` is 7.0 — specs-shv2/float-type.md's
// `float-promotion`), so the way into the float domain is exercised by the same line that defeats
// the folder. `trunc` is the way out, and `floatSumAndReturn` is where every float function takes it.
// One tracked float accumulator, boxed.
//
// ⚠ A ONE-FIELD RECORD BECAUSE `Array with float` IS E2062 BY POLICY (`Parser.maxon`), and an
// `Array with <struct>` is not. Boxing is what lets the tracker's width follow `WorkingFloatsPerFunction`
// rather than being written down as a four in a `match` — the same constant-and-its-realization pair
// `MatchDispatchShapeCount` guards, avoided rather than guarded.
// The generator's own float register, matching the emitted programs' unconstrained `float` — a bare
// `float` is not nameable as a type (E3005), exactly as a bare `int` is not.
typealias TrackedFloatValue = float(f64.min to f64.max)

type TrackedFloat
	export var value as TrackedFloatValue

	static function create(value TrackedFloatValue) returns Self
		return Self{value: value}
	end 'create'
end 'TrackedFloat'

typealias TrackedFloatArray = Array with TrackedFloat

function trackedFloatAt(tracked TrackedFloatArray, slot KnobIndex) returns TrackedFloatValue
	let box = try tracked.get(slot) otherwise panic("ScaleCorpus: float slot {slot} was read before it was declared — the tracker and the emitted prelude have gone out of step")
	return box.value
end 'trackedFloatAt'

function trackedFloatSet(tracked TrackedFloatArray, slot KnobIndex, value TrackedFloatValue)
	try tracked.set(slot, value: TrackedFloat.create(value)) otherwise panic("ScaleCorpus: float slot {slot} was written before it was declared — the tracker and the emitted prelude have gone out of step")
end 'trackedFloatSet'

// The multiplier a float group applies, as a value and as the source text of the same literal.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE THREE MULTIPLIERS MOVED FROM 2.5 / 3.5 / 4.5 TO 0.75 / 0.85 / 0.95, AND IT IS THE ONE PLACE
// IN THIS RUNG WHERE A KNOB'S CONTENT CHANGED. MEASURED, on this knob's own recurrence:** at the old
// constants `fsum` left i64 range at rung 1 (3.3e22), and was **NaN from rung 5** — the DEFAULT top rung
// — so the program `trunc`ed a NaN. That was invisible while the corpus was only ever compiled; the
// moment the rungs are RUN it is a wrong answer, and a target-dependent one (x64's `cvttsd2si` answers
// `i64.min` for NaN where arm64's `fcvtzs` answers 0), so it would have made the ladder's exit code a
// property of the host.
//
// The blow-up is the ADDITIVE coupling (`f{p} = f{p} + f{q}`), not the multiply: candidates keeping the
// old multipliers overflow whichever accumulator is divided, and every multiplier above ~1 does too. A
// sub-unit multiplier is the smallest change that bounds it — measured finite and identical at every
// rung from 0 to 7, settling near 7.6/8.4.
//
// **WHAT IT COST, exactly: nothing in what the knob PRICES.** Same four arithmetic ops in the same
// order, same two compares, same two float phis, same THREE distinct `.rdata` constants, same site
// count, same live-value count. Only the numbers in them are smaller. What it did cost is DYNAMIC RANGE:
// the accumulators now reach a fixed point, so at the top rungs `fsum` no longer varies with the seed.
// That is invisible to the compiler either way — there is no SCCP and `a` is an opaque parameter — so
// the witness stays a live runtime compare; but it does mean this knob's RUNTIME check is weaker than
// the others, and it is the one place that is true.
//
// ⚠ THE VALUE AND THE TEXT ARE ONE FACT: `(k + 0.5) / 10.0` is the correctly-rounded double nearest
// `0.k5`, which is bit-for-bit the double the emitted literal `0.k5` parses to — for 0.75 exactly, and
// for 0.85/0.95 as the same nearest neighbour. Spelling it any other way (`k / 10.0 + 0.05`) would give
// a DIFFERENT double and the witness would be wrong by an ulp at the top rung.
function floatGroupMultiplierDigit(group KnobIndex) returns KnobIndex
	return group mod 3 + 7
end 'floatGroupMultiplierDigit'

function floatGroupMultiplier(group KnobIndex) returns TrackedFloatValue
	return (floatGroupMultiplierDigit(group) + 0.5) / 10.0
end 'floatGroupMultiplier'

// The divisor a float group applies — unchanged, and exactly representable: `n.25` for a small `n`.
//
// The whole part carries its OWN narrow range rather than the generic knob index, because it is what
// makes the divisor provably non-zero one line down: `int(0 to 1048575) + 0.25` admits 0.25 and every
// value above it, and a divisor whose range admits zero is E3057 however obvious the modulus is.
typealias FloatGroupDivisorWhole = int(2 to 5)
typealias FloatGroupDivisor = float(2.25 to 5.25)

function floatGroupDivisorWhole(group KnobIndex) returns FloatGroupDivisorWhole
	return (group mod 4 + 2) as FloatGroupDivisorWhole
end 'floatGroupDivisorWhole'

function floatGroupDivisor(group KnobIndex) returns FloatGroupDivisor
	return floatGroupDivisorWhole(group) + 0.25
end 'floatGroupDivisor'

function floatWorkingDecl(tracked TrackedFloatArray) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	for f in 0 upto WorkingFloatsPerFunction 'decl'
		out.append("\tvar f{f} = a + {f}.5\n")
		tracked.push(TrackedFloat.create(corpusSeedValue() + f + 0.5))
	end 'decl'
	return out.build()
end 'floatWorkingDecl'

// The shared float epilogue: sum the accumulators and LEAVE the float domain through `trunc`.
//
// `trunc` is not a flourish, it is the only exit: a float RETURN needs the xmm0 return slot Wave 1
// does not model (specs-shv2/float-type.md's disabled `float-return-from-function`), so an `int`
// return through `fpToSi` is how a float value reaches `main` at all — and it is the conversion
// band's second direction, the one `floatWorkingDecl` does not cover.
function floatSumAndWitness(driverName String, tracked TrackedFloatArray) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\tvar fsum = 0.0\n")
	var fsum = 0.0
	for f in 0 upto WorkingFloatsPerFunction 'sum'
		out.append("\tfsum = fsum + f{f}\n")
		fsum = fsum + trackedFloatAt(tracked, slot: f)
	end 'sum'
	out.append(driverWitnessTail(driverName, valueExpr: "trunc(fsum)", expected: trunc(fsum)))
	return out.build()
end 'floatSumAndWitness'

let FloatsDriver = "scaleFloats"

// The `floatOps` knob: ONE function, N groups of float arithmetic and float compares over a bounded
// set of float accumulators. The float twin of `longFunction` — it grows float ops along a chain.
//
// Each group covers, deliberately, the things a float codegen change can move:
//   • ALL FOUR arithmetic ops. `/` matters most: it is the ONE operator whose float form is a
//     different Std op from its integer form (integer `/` traps and lowers to `StdOp.div`; float `/`
//     is `binOp(div)`), so a corpus without it measures three quarters of the arithmetic band.
//   • A float compare in BRANCH form, feeding a `jcc` — and the FLOAT PHI its merge takes, because
//     the accumulator is assigned on one arm only.
//   • The same compare MATERIALIZED as a bool, which is a different lowering: it lands `ucomisd`'s
//     answer in a GPR instead of feeding a jump, and it is the shape that crosses register FILES.
function floatOpsSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the float-ops knob — {groupCount} float arith/compare groups over {WorkingFloatsPerFunction} float accumulators.\n")
	out.append(driverHeader(FloatsDriver))
	out.append("\tvar wit = 0\n")
	var tracked = TrackedFloatArray.create()
	out.append(floatWorkingDecl(tracked))

	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		let p = i mod WorkingFloatsPerFunction
		let q = (i + 1) mod WorkingFloatsPerFunction
		let r = (i + 2) mod WorkingFloatsPerFunction

		out.append("\tf{p} = f{p} + f{q}\n")
		trackedFloatSet(tracked, slot: p, value: trackedFloatAt(tracked, slot: p) + trackedFloatAt(tracked, slot: q))
		out.append("\tf{q} = f{q} - f{r}\n")
		trackedFloatSet(tracked, slot: q, value: trackedFloatAt(tracked, slot: q) - trackedFloatAt(tracked, slot: r))
		out.append("\tf{p} = f{p} * 0.{floatGroupMultiplierDigit(i)}5\n")
		trackedFloatSet(tracked, slot: p, value: trackedFloatAt(tracked, slot: p) * floatGroupMultiplier(i))
		out.append("\tf{r} = f{r} / {floatGroupDivisorWhole(i)}.25\n")
		// Bound rather than divided by in place: the non-zero range travels with the BINDING, which is
		// the same shape `intDivideSource` emits for `ScaleDivisor` one knob over. `FloatGroupDivisor` is
		// `float(2.25 to 5.25)`, so the divide is provably safe and takes no `try` — and this line is what
		// found that shv2 was not reading a ranged FLOAT alias as a proof at all (see `Parser.divisorProof`).
		let divisor = floatGroupDivisor(i)
		trackedFloatSet(tracked, slot: r, value: trackedFloatAt(tracked, slot: r) / divisor)

		// The compare that feeds a branch, and the float phi the continuation takes for `f{p}`.
		out.append("\tif f{p} > f{q} 'fc{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tf{p} = f{p} + 1.5\n")
		out.append("\tend 'fc{i}'\n")
		if trackedFloatAt(tracked, slot: p) > trackedFloatAt(tracked, slot: q) 'branchTaken'
			trackedFloatSet(tracked, slot: p, value: trackedFloatAt(tracked, slot: p) + 1.5)
		end 'branchTaken'

		// The compare whose answer becomes a bool VALUE in a GPR before anything branches on it.
		out.append("\tlet fb{i} = f{r} < f{q}\n")
		out.append("\tif fb{i} 'fm{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tf{r} = f{r} + 0.25\n")
		out.append("\tend 'fm{i}'\n")
		if trackedFloatAt(tracked, slot: r) < trackedFloatAt(tracked, slot: q) 'materializedTaken'
			trackedFloatSet(tracked, slot: r, value: trackedFloatAt(tracked, slot: r) + 0.25)
		end 'materializedTaken'
	end 'eachGroup'

	out.append(floatSumAndWitness(FloatsDriver, tracked: tracked))
	return out.build()
end 'floatOpsSource'

let FloatSpillDriver = "scaleFloatSpill"

// The `floatSpill` knob: ONE function, N loops, each holding `floatsLivePerSpillLoop()` floats live
// across a call to `scaleOpaque` — `FloatSpillHeadroom` more than the callee-saved XMM half is wide,
// so at least that many spill in every loop. See `floatsLivePerSpillLoop` for why that count is
// DERIVED from the allocator's masks rather than chosen.
//
// The call is an INTEGER function, and that is not a compromise: a float ARGUMENT and a float RETURN
// are both Wave 2, so an int callee is the only call a float can currently be live across — and it
// is precisely the shape that forces the spill, because the floats are live across the bracket and
// have nowhere caller-saved to survive it.
//
// Its accumulators are assigned INSIDE the loop, so each is a loop-carried FLOAT PHI. Before the
// `mintPhi` fix this knob's every rung would have panicked the x64 emitter outright — which is the
// most direct evidence available that the ladder needed this dimension.
//
// ⚠ WHY THIS IS NOT `pressuredLoopsSource` WITH A PREFIX PARAMETER, though the two emit the same
// SHAPE (declare, loop, call, update, sum). They are the same shape asked of two register files that
// answer it DIFFERENTLY, and the difference is the entire point of having both:
//
//   • `pressuredLoops` measures a CHOICE. Its eight GPRs have five callee-saved registers to compete
//     for, so the allocator decides which three go to memory, and the knob is tuned (eight, against a
//     14-GPR pool) to make exactly five spills fall out of that competition.
//   • `floatSpill` measures the SAME choice in the OTHER FILE. Since Wave 2 its floats compete for
//     ten callee-saved XMMs exactly as the GPRs compete for five, so the knob is tuned (twelve,
//     against a 16-XMM file) to make the overflow fall out of that competition. Under Wave 1 it
//     measured no choice at all — the allowed set was ∅ and every float spilled at any knob value —
//     and that is why the count needed no tuning then and does now.
//
// Merging them would take a `varPrefix`, a `counterPrefix`, a `resultPrefix`, a per-loop count, a
// literal suffix, an accumulator initializer and a return expression — seven parameters to fold two
// twenty-line text emitters, and the merged body would state neither reason above. Worse, the merge
// would couple them: retuning the GPR knob to keep provoking five spills would silently retune a
// float knob for which the number is irrelevant. Two knobs that a future change must be free to move
// independently are not one knob. (The file already works this way — `longFunction` and `deepBlocks`
// share a shape and stay apart for the same kind of reason.)
function floatSpillSource(loopCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	// Read ONCE, so the header line and the three emit loops cannot disagree about how many floats
	// this knob is generating — the count is a property of the target, not of the loop it is in.
	let floatsPerLoop = floatsLivePerSpillLoop()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the float-spill knob — {loopCount} loops, {floatsPerLoop} floats live across a call in each (more than the callee-saved XMM half, so the overflow is a forced spill).\n")
	out.append(driverHeader(FloatSpillDriver))
	out.append("\tvar wit = 0\n")
	out.append("\tvar total = 0.0\n")
	var total = 0.0

	for loopIndex in 0 upto loopCount 'eachLoop'
		// Fresh names per loop, so the loops' live ranges do not overlap and peak XMM pressure stays
		// at one loop's worth however many loops there are — `pressuredLoops`' rule, in the other file.
		var live = TrackedFloatArray.create()
		for v in 0 upto floatsPerLoop 'declValues'
			out.append("\tvar g{loopIndex}_{v} = a + {v + 1}.25\n")
			live.push(TrackedFloat.create(corpusSeedValue() + v + 1.25))
		end 'declValues'
		out.append("\tvar j{loopIndex} = 0\n")
		out.append("\twhile j{loopIndex} < {GeneratedLoopTrips} 'q{loopIndex}'\n")
		out.append("\t\tlet k{loopIndex} = {OpaqueCalleeName}(j{loopIndex})\n")

		for v in 0 upto floatsPerLoop 'updateValues'
			out.append("\t\tg{loopIndex}_{v} = g{loopIndex}_{v} + (k{loopIndex} + 0.5)\n")
		end 'updateValues'

		out.append("\t\tj{loopIndex} = j{loopIndex} + 1\n")
		out.append("\tend 'q{loopIndex}'\n")

		// The loop above runs `GeneratedLoopTrips` times with its counter as `scaleOpaque`'s argument —
		// `pressuredLoops`' tracking, in the other register file. Every value here is a quarter-integer
		// and stays under 2^40, so every float step is EXACT and `trunc` is not a rounding question.
		for trip in 0 upto GeneratedLoopTrips 'eachTrip'
			for v in 0 upto floatsPerLoop 'perTrip'
				trackedFloatSet(live, slot: v, value: trackedFloatAt(live, slot: v) + (opaqueCalleeValue(trip) + 0.5))
			end 'perTrip'
		end 'eachTrip'

		for v in 0 upto floatsPerLoop 'sumValues'
			out.append("\ttotal = total + g{loopIndex}_{v}\n")
			total = total + trackedFloatAt(live, slot: v)
		end 'sumValues'
	end 'eachLoop'

	out.append(driverWitnessTail(FloatSpillDriver, valueExpr: "trunc(total)", expected: trunc(total)))
	return out.build()
end 'floatSpillSource'

// The name prefixes the five knobs below declare and then read back. Each is spelled in a DECLARATION
// and in a USE many lines apart, so it is named once here for the reason `smallFunctionName` exists: a
// prefix written down twice is a prefix that can drift, and the failure is not a bad measurement but a
// generated program that does not compile at all.
let GlobalConstPrefix = "gc"
let GlobalVarPrefix = "gv"
let StructTypePrefix = "ScaleType"
let AllocTypePrefix = "ScaleAlloc"

let GlobalConstsDriver = "scaleGlobalConsts"

// The `globalConsts` knob: N top-level `let`s, each derived from the TWO below it, and a function
// that reads every one of them.
//
// ⚠ THIS KNOB EMITS ALMOST NO CODE FOR ITS SIZE, AND THAT IS THE FOLDER DOING ITS JOB RATHER THAN
// BEATING THE CORPUS. This file's founding trap — a benchmark that folds away — does NOT apply here,
// and a reader who "fixes" these to be fold-resistant will have made them not-constants and measured
// something else. A top-level `let` IS a constant: `let A = 5` then `acc + A` folds to `acc + 5`, and
// the fold is CORRECT. What this knob measures is the FRONT END — the constant arena, the initializer
// DFS, the memo key — all of which are reached at DECLARATION, before codegen has an opinion. The
// other knobs carry `assertCorpusCompiled`'s code-growth floor; this one is not asked to.
//
// It is still USED, on purpose: an unread constant is one a future pass may drop before the arena is
// ever built, which would take the dimension away again exactly as an uncalled function would (see
// `mainSource`). Reading it is what makes the declaration load-bearing.
//
// EACH CONSTANT REFERENCES THE TWO BELOW IT, and that is the property here worth defending. Over a
// CHAIN a DFS is O(N) whether it memoizes or not, so a chain cannot tell the two apart. Over a
// 2-PARENT DAG the memoized DFS is O(N) and the unmemoized one is O(2^N) — so if a change ever drops
// the memo, this knob does not bend politely, it HANGS at the first rung where 2^N stops being
// payable. That is the loudest failure this file can arrange for a cost that is otherwise invisible:
// the DFS allocates almost nothing, and a memory instrument would never see it bend.
function globalConstsSource(constCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the global-consts knob — {constCount} top-level `let`s, each derived from the two below it.\n")

	// The two seeds are literals; every constant above them derives from its two predecessors, which
	// is what makes the initializer graph a DAG rather than a chain.
	//
	// ⭐ THE VALUES ARE ACCUMULATED IN THE LOOP THAT DECLARES THEM. The emitted text is the SYMBOLIC
	// recurrence — it has to be, or there would be no DAG for the initializer DFS to walk and the knob
	// would measure nothing — and `values` is the same recurrence's answer, stepped once per emitted
	// line. That is one model with two outputs, not two models: nothing re-reads the generated text and
	// nothing computes the sequence a second time.
	var values = TrackedValueArray.create()
	out.append("let {GlobalConstPrefix}0 = {GlobalConstSeedLow}\n")
	values.push(GlobalConstSeedLow)
	out.append("let {GlobalConstPrefix}1 = {GlobalConstSeedHigh}\n")
	values.push(GlobalConstSeedHigh)
	for c in 2 upto constCount 'eachConst'
		out.append("let {GlobalConstPrefix}{c} = ({GlobalConstPrefix}{c - 1} + {GlobalConstPrefix}{c - 2}) mod {GlobalConstModulus}\n")
		values.push((trackedAt(values, slot: c - 1) + trackedAt(values, slot: c - 2)) mod GlobalConstModulus)
	end 'eachConst'

	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(GlobalConstsDriver))
	for c in 0 upto constCount 'eachUse'
		out.append("\tacc = acc + {GlobalConstPrefix}{c}{neutralizeText(trackedAt(values, slot: c))}\n")
		out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	end 'eachUse'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(GlobalConstsDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'globalConstsSource'

// The DAG's two literal roots. Named because each is spelled in the emitted declaration and pushed into
// the tracker on the line below it, and a reader changing one must change the other.
let GlobalConstSeedLow = 1
let GlobalConstSeedHigh = 2

let GlobalVarsDriver = "scaleGlobalVars"

// The `globalVars` knob: N top-level `var`s, each stored to and loaded back through the opaque seed.
//
// ⚠ A GLOBAL IS FOLD-RESISTANT FOR FREE, AND IT HAS TO BE, BECAUSE IT CANNOT BE SEEDED. A top-level
// `var`'s initializer must be a CONSTANT expression — `var b = scaleSeed()` is E2045, and even
// `var b = a + 1` over another global is E2004 (`Undefined constant 'a'`) — so the two tricks the rest
// of this corpus relies on (a loop-carried var, a call result) are both unavailable at the point of
// declaration. They are also unnecessary: a global READ is opaque to the folder no matter what the
// global was initialized to, because `FoldConstOperands.stdConstInfo` answers `notConst` for the whole
// `binOp to osAllocPages` band — the arm `loadIndirect` falls in. The store below writes the opaque
// `acc`, and the load reads a location the folder must assume anything could have written.
//
// That opacity is a PROPERTY, not an accident, and `specs-shv2/global-load-not-hoisted.md` verifies the
// ANSWER a program gets. ⚠ **This comment used to name `StdOp.loadIndirect`'s `isPure: false` as the
// mechanism. IT IS NOT: `StdOpMeta.isPure` has ZERO readers (OPEN.md #27)**, so it holds nothing off —
// the folder is stopped by the match arm named above, and nothing HOISTS because no pass hoists.
//
// The knob must still never be "fixed" by making the load pure to tidy up a number: that declaration
// starts being read the moment DCE/CSE/LICM or the inliner lands, and a wrong one is a silent wrong
// answer — and it would be read long before anyone re-read this comment.
//
// Only `acc` and one loaded value are ever live, so the knob measures `.data` layout and the
// address/load/store triple, and can never quietly become a register-pressure knob and trip E5001.
function globalVarsSource(varCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the global-vars knob — {varCount} top-level `var`s in .data, each stored to and loaded back.\n")
	for v in 0 upto varCount 'eachVar'
		out.append("var {GlobalVarPrefix}{v} = {v mod GlobalVarSeedModulus}\n")
	end 'eachVar'

	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(GlobalVarsDriver))
	// The load reads back what the store one line above put there — this global's literal initializer
	// plus the accumulator — so the fold is neutralized against exactly that. It is the store that is
	// really under test here: a `storeIndirect` that did not land, or a `loadIndirect` that read the
	// wrong slot, moves this and nothing else does.
	//
	// ⚠ EACH GLOBAL IS WRITTEN EXACTLY ONCE, which is what makes its prior value the literal initializer.
	// `main` calls this driver once; a second call would read back a value this line does not predict.
	for v in 0 upto varCount 'eachUse'
		out.append("\t{GlobalVarPrefix}{v} = {GlobalVarPrefix}{v} + a\n")
		out.append("\tacc = acc + {GlobalVarPrefix}{v}{neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue() + (v mod GlobalVarSeedModulus))}\n")
		out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	end 'eachUse'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(GlobalVarsDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'globalVarsSource'

// One `type` declaration: two fields, and the explicit `static create` that is the only way to build
// one today.
//
// ⚠ THE `static create` IS NOT OPTIONAL, AND ITS ABSENCE IS NOT A DIAGNOSTIC YOU WOULD ENJOY. A `type`
// with no explicit `static function create` does not resolve at the USE site, and the field read fails
// with `E2015: a field access on 'p', which is declared 'unknown'` — a diagnostic that points at the
// reader of the field rather than at the missing constructor. (Measured, while writing this knob.)
//
// The fields are plain `int` rather than a ranged typealias. specs-shv2/structs.md reaches for an
// alias, but every generated function in this corpus already takes `a int`, an `int` field lays out
// the same 8-byte slot the alias would, and going without drops a file-private typealias from each
// struct file for no loss of coverage — ranged typealiases are `shiftCoverageSource`'s job.
//
// SHARED BY BOTH STRUCT KNOBS, so the two measure the SAME declaration against different dimensions:
// how many of them exist, and how many times one is built.
//
// It also emits the per-type READER — a free function taking the struct BY PARAMETER and reading its
// fields, which is a struct crossing a CALL BOUNDARY. That shape shipped (`specs-shv2/structs.md`'s
// `struct-literal-as-arg`, `struct-managed-field.md`'s `param-consumed-into-field`) while this file's
// manifest went on calling it "P1.4, deferred on the borrow-vs-consume ruling" — see `printCorpusManifest`.
function structTypeDecl(name String) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\ntype {name}\n")
	out.append("\texport var x as int\n")
	out.append("\texport var y as int\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("\tstatic function create(x int, y int) returns Self\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn Self\{x: x, y: y\}\n")
	out.append("\tend 'create'\n")
	out.append("end '{name}'\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("function {structReaderName(name)}(p {name}) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn p.x + p.y\n")
	out.append("end '{structReaderName(name)}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'structTypeDecl'

// The per-type reader's name — spelled in the DECLARATION (`structTypeDecl`) and at the CALL
// (`structCreateInBlock`), so it is derived in one place rather than written down twice.
function structReaderName(typeName String) returns String
	return "scaleRead{typeName}"
end 'structReaderName'

// ⭐ EVERY OWNED VALUE IN THIS CORPUS IS BORN INSIDE ITS OWN BLOCK, AND THE REASON IS MEASURED, NOT
// STYLISTIC. Every knob that creates heap — both struct knobs, the String knob, both Array knobs, both
// generic knobs, the interface knobs — emits through here, so the rule is stated exactly once.
//
// AN OWNED BINDING DOES NOT DIE WHEN YOU STOP READING IT. At function-body scope it stays on the
// parser's `ownedBindings` list until the `return`, where `emitScopeDrops` (Parser.maxon:2524) decrefs
// every one of them. So N creates at function scope put N heap pointers LIVE SIMULTANEOUSLY at the
// return, against a 14-GPR pool — and the splitter must then spill nearly all of them. It does not
// refuse (they are spillable, so this is never E5001); it goes QUADRATIC.
//
// MEASURED, on a private ladder of 32/64/128/256/512 creates in one function:
//   • at function scope: `regalloc:splitting` reads x3.49, x3.70, x3.84, x3.91 per doubling —
//     converging on x4.00, and 65% of the whole compile at the top rung.
//   • in per-create blocks: x1.82, x1.90, x1.95, x1.97. LINEAR, and the knob measures what it says.
// The mechanism is `SplitLiveRanges`' driver: it is incremental in DIRTY BLOCKS, so one enormous
// block means every one of the ~N splits re-sweeps all O(N) of its ops.
//
// ⚠ IT DOES NOT DEPEND ON THE `closeBlock` LEAK, WHICH IS THE FIRST THING A READER WILL SUSPECT.
// `closeBlock` (Parser.maxon:2343) drains the binding and deliberately emits NO drop, so a struct in
// a block was STATED to leak (exit 101, a P1.4 gap this instrument could not observe while it only
// COMPILED these programs — it now RUNS them, so a leak in a rung would surface here as a non-zero exit).
//
// ⚠⚠ **THAT CONSEQUENCE DID NOT REPRODUCE WHEN SOMEBODY FINALLY WENT AND RAN IT, 2026-08-02.** BATCH9's
// review BUILT and RAN two shapes — a struct with a `String` field created inside an `if`, and an
// `Array with String` plus a heap `String` across two blocks — and both exit **0**, with
// `___mm_leak_check` confirmed present in each entry stub by `otool -tV`, so the gate was live and
// silent rather than absent. Either P1.4 closed it, or the claim was always narrower than its wording.
// It is QUALIFIED here rather than deleted because two probes are not a proof of absence, and the
// measurement is recorded at the one moment it is known.
//
// ⇒ **AND NOTHING BELOW DEPENDS ON WHICH WAY IT WENT**, which is the point of the paragraph: that leak
// is not what bounds the live range, and the knob does not want it to be. When the drop is emitted at
// the block's END, the live range is bounded by the block either way. Correct before the fix and after
// it — which is the only reason it is safe to lean on a construct carrying a claimed gap.
//
// The `if` is SCOPE SCAFFOLDING, not a CFG dimension — `deepBlocks` owns that one, and this adds ~1
// branch per site against its ~3 blocks per if/else. `pressuredLoops` uses `while` the same way, for
// the same reason: a knob may spend control flow to set up the thing it actually measures.
//
// The condition is `acc > 0` in every caller, which is why it is not a parameter: `acc` is the opaque
// accumulator every generated driver declares, so the guard is unfoldable and the block is unremovable.
// `body` is indented TWO tabs by its caller — one for the function, one for this block.
// ⭐⭐ **AND IT MARKS THAT IT RAN, WHICH IS THE ONE THING NEUTRALIZING THE FOLDS COULD NOT CATCH.**
// Every site inside this block subtracts what it contributed, so the block leaves `acc` exactly as it
// found it — which means a block that NEVER EXECUTED leaves `acc` exactly as it found it too, and the
// two are indistinguishable. That is not a hypothetical: MEASURED on the rung-0 binary before this rung,
// `acc > 0` was FALSE for seven consecutive knobs (the accumulator had wrapped negative), so every array,
// generic, interface and buffer site in the corpus was skipped at run time and no reading could tell.
//
// The mark closes it: added INSIDE the guard, taken off OUTSIDE it. A block that did not run is short by
// exactly `SiteEnteredMark`, and the driver's witness fails. It is one `add` and one `sub` per site — the
// price of a guard that can fail.
function ownedSiteBlock(label String, body String) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\tif acc > 0 '{label}'\n")
	out.append(body)
	out.append("\t\tacc = acc + {SiteEnteredMark}\n")
	out.append("\tend '{label}'\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc - {SiteEnteredMark}\n")
	return out.build()
end 'ownedSiteBlock'

// What an owned site adds on the way through, and what its caller takes off afterwards. One, because the
// value is irrelevant and the SIGN is not: it must be positive, so a site whose guard is somehow taken
// twice cannot cancel against one that was skipped.
let SiteEnteredMark = 1

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO SHAPES EVERY PREDICATE SITE IN THIS FILE IS WRITTEN AS, AND THEY ARE A PAIR ON PURPOSE.**
// A predicate has two directions and only one of them is witnessed by the true arm: a `contains` stuck at
// `true` and a correct one are indistinguishable from a site that only ever asks a question whose answer
// is yes. So every predicate the generator can build FALSE gets `falsePredicateProbe` beside its true one,
// and that half is the half a corpus forgets.
//
// Both emit at TWO tabs, because both are `ownedSiteBlock` BODY helpers by construction — every caller
// hands its output to `ownedSiteBlock`, whose body is one level inside the driver's own. That is why they
// take no `indent` where `witnessProbe` does: an unused parameter to make them look like their neighbour
// would be a shape nothing asks for.
//
// ⚠ FOUR KNOBS WROTE ONE OF THESE INLINE BEFORE THEY EXISTED — `charOpsSource` twice, `stringOpsSource`
// and `managedStructTypesSource` once each — which is four places for the `+ mark` / `- mark` pairing to
// lose a sign, in a file where an inverted sign pins `acc` to the wrong value at every rung at once.
function truePredicateProbe(condition String, label String) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\t\tif {condition} '{label}'\n")
	out.append("\t\t\tacc = acc + {SiteEnteredMark}\n")
	out.append("\t\tend '{label}'\n")
	out.append("\t\tacc = acc - {SiteEnteredMark}\n")
	return out.build()
end 'truePredicateProbe'

// The FALSE twin. There is nothing to take off afterwards: the arm must not run at all, so its body IS the
// failure marker — the same shape `charOpsSource`'s `match` default arm uses for the same reason.
function falsePredicateProbe(condition String, label String) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\t\tif {condition} '{label}'\n")
	out.append("\t\t\tacc = acc + {CorpusWitnessFailedOffset}\n")
	out.append("\t\tend '{label}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'falsePredicateProbe'

// The managed field the THREE String-owning knobs declare — named once, because the field name is
// spelled in the declaration, in `create`'s parameter and in the `Self{}` literal, and the buffer knob's
// holder proves the emitter can spell a different one.
let ManagedLabelFieldName = "label"
let StringTypeSpelling = "String"

// ONE TYPE OWNING A MANAGED FIELD, beside a trivial one — the declaration whose SYNTHESIZED
// `__destruct_<T>` must free the first and skip the second. A type with only the managed field would not
// distinguish "walks the managed offsets" from "frees everything", which is why the trivial field is
// part of the shape rather than filler.
//
// SHARED BY THE FOUR KNOBS THAT NEED A MANAGED AGGREGATE, and they need the same one: the
// managed-struct knob (which grows how many such types EXIST), the managed-array knob (whose ELEMENT is
// one), the generic-instances knob (whose managed type ARGUMENT is one) and the buffer-surface knob
// (whose holder carries a field DECLARED `__ManagedMemory`). Four near-copies is four places for the
// shape to drift, and the drift would be silent — each knob would still compile and each would be
// measuring a slightly different declaration.
//
// `valueTypeName` is a NAME rather than a fixed `int` because each caller's file declares its own
// element alias, and a ranged alias and a bare `int` do not lay out the same slot everywhere.
//
// ⚠ THE MANAGED FIELD'S TYPE IS A PARAMETER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE OF WHY THIS IS ONE EMITTER AND NOT
// TWO. `String` and `__ManagedMemory` are two different managed types over ONE declaration shape; the
// destructor cascade the shape exists to measure does not care which, and the buffer knob needs the
// FIELD SPELLING (`Parser.markDeclaredSurface`'s struct-field carrier) rather than a new layout.
function managedFieldTypeDecl(name String, managedFieldName String, managedTypeName String, valueTypeName String) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\ntype {name}\n")
	out.append("\texport var {managedFieldName} as {managedTypeName}\n")
	out.append("\texport var value as {valueTypeName}\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("\tstatic function create({managedFieldName} {managedTypeName}, value {valueTypeName}) returns Self\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn Self\{{managedFieldName}: {managedFieldName}, value: value\}\n")
	out.append("\tend 'create'\n")
	out.append("end '{name}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'managedFieldTypeDecl'

// Whether a generated generic base carries a THROWING `T`-returning method beside its total `get`.
//
// It is a union and not an empty-string error name because the two answers are different SHAPES, not one
// shape with a blank in it: the throwing arm needs an error type to name in its `throws` clause, and a
// base that has no such method has no error type to offer. An absent name spelled `""` would compile a
// `throws ` clause with nothing after it into the corpus and take the whole ladder down as a broken run.
union GenericBoxFetch
	// `get` only — what both generic knobs want. Neither calls a throwing method, and a body nothing
	// calls would price a cost their dimension does not claim to carry.
	none

	// A `fetch() returns T throws <errorTypeName>` beside `get` — the `tryCoOwn` knob's shape, and the
	// only route in this corpus to a substituted MANAGED `T` result under a `try`.
	throwing(errorTypeName String)
end 'GenericBoxFetch'

// ONE UNCONSTRAINED SINGLE-PARAMETER GENERIC BASE — an opaque `T` field, a `create` that takes one, and
// a `get` that hands it back.
//
// SHARED BY BOTH GENERIC KNOBS, which is the whole reason they can be two knobs at all: one grows how
// many such BASES exist (each costing one shared method body, since shv2 passes dictionaries rather than
// monomorphizing) and the other holds ONE of them fixed and grows the INSTANTIATIONS. Emitting the
// declaration twice would let the two knobs measure different bases while claiming to isolate the two
// costs of the same one.
//
// ⭐ **AND SHARED BY THE `tryCoOwn` KNOB THROUGH ITS `fetch` PARAMETER RATHER THAN BY A THIRD COPY.**
// That knob needs the identical base with one THROWING method added; a near-copy would be a fourth
// declaration of one shape, and the drift would be silent — each knob would go on compiling while
// measuring a base slightly different from the one the manifest describes.
function genericBoxDecl(name String, fetch GenericBoxFetch) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\ntype {name} uses T\n")
	out.append("\texport var value as T\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("\texport static function create(v T) returns Self\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn Self\{value: v\}\n")
	out.append("\tend 'create'\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("\texport function get() returns T\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn self.value\n")
	out.append("\tend 'get'\n")

	match fetch 'fetchArm'
		none then break 'fetchArm'
		throwing(errorTypeName) then out.append(genericBoxFetchDecl(errorTypeName))
	end 'fetchArm'

	out.append("end '{name}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'genericBoxDecl'

// The throwing sibling of `get`: the same substituted `T` return, and a `throws` clause that is the
// whole difference. It is what puts the caller's `+1` for a substituted managed result under a `try` —
// the promotion BATCH25 lifts onto the ok edge.
//
// ⭐ **THE `live` FLAG IS WHAT MAKES THE ERROR EDGE RUN, and a corpus whose error edge never runs cannot
// check the half of this rung that emits NOTHING.** The lift's rule is that the promotion goes on the ok
// edge ALONE, because on the error edge the throw never wrote the result register; a retain that leaked
// onto that edge would take a reference to whatever the register held. That is a RUNTIME fact, so the
// caller passes an opaque bool (`tryCoOwnLiveText`) and the ladder takes both edges at every rung.
function genericBoxFetchDecl(errorTypeName String) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("\texport function fetch(live bool) returns T throws {errorTypeName}\n")
	out.append("\t\tif not live 'absent'\n")
	out.append("\t\t\tthrow {errorTypeName}.bad\n")
	out.append("\t\tend 'absent'\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn self.value\n")
	out.append("\tend 'fetch'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'genericBoxFetchDecl'

// One struct create SITE: build it, read both fields, WRITE a field, and pass the whole struct across a
// call. Shared by both struct knobs, so all four are per-SITE costs that ride whichever knob doubles.
//
// The binding is `var` rather than `let` because of the field write — `challenge-struct-field-assign.md`'s
// `struct-field-reassignment` is the shape, and assigning through a `let` is E2013.
// ⭐ EVERY FOLD HERE IS NEUTRALIZED AGAINST WHAT THIS FUNCTION JUST WROTE. `x` is the accumulator
// because the `create` call above puts it there; `y` is the accumulator plus the literal this line
// chose; the reader hands back their sum. Subtracting that leaves `acc` where it arrived — and the
// SECOND fold reads `y` back AFTER the field write, so a store that did not land moves the witness.
//
// Before this, the site read `acc` three times and added all three back: `acc -> 7*acc + k`, which is
// how eight sites at rung 0 turned the seed into 10^8 and 256 sites at rung 5 wrapped i64 outright.
function structCreateInBlock(index KnobIndex, typeName String, binding String) returns String
	let created = index mod 5 + 1
	let rewritten = index mod 3 + 1
	var body = StringBuilder.create()
	body.append("\t\tvar {binding} = {typeName}.create(a, y: a + {created})\n")
	body.append("\t\tacc = acc + {binding}.x + {binding}.y{neutralizeText(2 * corpusSeedValue() + created)}\n")
	body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
	body.append("\t\t{binding}.y = a + {rewritten}\n")
	body.append("\t\tacc = acc + {structReaderName(typeName)}({binding}){neutralizeText(2 * corpusSeedValue() + rewritten)}\n")
	body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
	return ownedSiteBlock("g{index}", body: body.build())
end 'structCreateInBlock'

let StructTypesDriver = "scaleStructTypes"

// The `structTypes` knob: N `type` declarations, each built exactly ONCE.
//
// The per-TYPE cost is the point — the type table and the layout descriptor — so what doubles is how
// many types EXIST, not how hard they are used. Each is instantiated once anyway, because a type
// nothing ever builds is a type a future pass may legitimately never lay out, and the dimension would
// evaporate silently. That is `mainSource`'s rule one level down: an unbuilt type is an uncalled
// function's twin.
//
// ⚠ NO `__destruct_<T>` CASCADE RIDES THIS KNOB, and that is a property of the TYPE it emits, not of the
// compiler: `structTypeDecl` declares two `int` fields, so the type owns nothing and its drop is trivial.
// The SYNTHESIZED cascade does exist (`installStructDestructors`, P1.4a wave 2 — one `__destruct_<T>` body
// per managed-field type, rooted and installed), and the knob that doubles it is `managedStructTypes`,
// whose types own a `String`. Keep the two apart: a change to destructor synthesis must move that column
// and leave this one flat, which is the whole reason both knobs exist.
//
// (This paragraph read "THERE IS NO `__destruct_*` CASCADE TO MEASURE YET … a per-type destructor is a
// later rung" for ten rungs after it landed, citing `LowerMaxonToStd.maxon:9` — which lists the
// `own.drop` → `__destruct_T` lowering as one of the 1:N desugarings that pass PERFORMS, not as future
// work. A gap named with a reason that has stopped being true is this file's worst failure mode, because
// the reason is what a reader uses to decide the gap is not theirs to close.)
function structTypesSource(typeCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the struct-types knob — {typeCount} type declarations, each built once.\n")
	for t in 0 upto typeCount 'eachType'
		out.append(structTypeDecl("{StructTypePrefix}{t}"))
	end 'eachType'

	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(StructTypesDriver))
	for t in 0 upto typeCount 'eachUse'
		out.append(structCreateInBlock(t, typeName: "{StructTypePrefix}{t}", binding: "s{t}"))
	end 'eachUse'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(StructTypesDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'structTypesSource'

let StructAllocsDriver = "scaleStructAllocs"

// The `structAllocs` knob: M `create` sites over a FIXED `AllocStructTypeCount` types.
//
// The per-SITE cost is the point — an `__mm_alloc` call, a field store per field, and the release the
// value's death forces — so what doubles is the number of ALLOCATION SITES while the number of types
// stays put. That is the whole of the difference from `structTypes`, and the reason the two are not one
// knob with a bigger number: this is the column that moves when P1.1a wave 2 changes what a `create`
// emits, and that one is the column that moves when the layout descriptor changes.
//
// Every create is block-scoped for the reason `structCreateInBlock` states — measured at x3.96 per
// doubling without it, x1.97 with it.
function structAllocsSource(allocCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the struct-allocs knob — {allocCount} create sites over {AllocStructTypeCount} types.\n")
	for t in 0 upto AllocStructTypeCount 'eachType'
		out.append(structTypeDecl("{AllocTypePrefix}{t}"))
	end 'eachType'

	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(StructAllocsDriver))
	for i in 0 upto allocCount 'eachAlloc'
		out.append(structCreateInBlock(i, typeName: "{AllocTypePrefix}{i mod AllocStructTypeCount}", binding: "p{i}"))
	end 'eachAlloc'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(StructAllocsDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'structAllocsSource'

let DivideDriver = "scaleDivide"

// The `intDivide` knob: N `/` and `mod` groups over a divisor derived from the opaque seed.
//
// BOTH OPERATORS, because they share one `idiv` and read DIFFERENT result registers — the quotient
// from RAX, the remainder from RDX. A corpus with only one of them measures half the lowering.
//
// The dividend is `acc`, which the group has just written and the next group will read, so `acc` is
// live ACROSS an `idiv` that clobbers both RAX and RDX. That is the constraint the whole path exists
// to satisfy, and a corpus of `20 / 4` could never provoke it — that folds before it reaches an
// allocator at all.
function intDivideSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the int-divide knob — {groupCount} `/` and `mod` groups over an opaque, non-zero divisor.\n")
	out.append("typealias {ScaleDivisorAlias} = int(1 to {DivisorMask + 1})\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(DivideDriver))
	// The dividend is the pinned accumulator and the divisor is derived from it by a mask this line
	// wrote, so both the quotient and the remainder are known here — and knowing them SEPARATELY is what
	// makes this witness see the RAX/RDX split it exists for: a lowering that read the quotient out of
	// the remainder's register moves one fold and not the other.
	let divisor = corpusDivisor()
	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		// The CAST is what keeps this the BARE `idiv` knob after A1 — see `DivisorMask`'s second ⚠.
		out.append("\tlet d{i} = ((a and {DivisorMask}) + 1) as {ScaleDivisorAlias}\n")
		out.append("\tacc = acc + a / d{i}{neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue() / divisor)}\n")
		out.append(accProbe("\t"))
		out.append("\tacc = acc + a mod d{i}{neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue() mod divisor)}\n")
		out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	end 'eachGroup'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(DivideDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'intDivideSource'

let CheckedDivideDriver = "scaleCheckedDivide"

// ⭐ **THE `checkedDivide` KNOB (A1): N `try (a / d) otherwise …` / `try (a mod d) otherwise …` groups.**
//
// The divisor is derived from the seed and DELIBERATELY NOT CAST, which is the whole difference from
// `intDivideSource` above: uncast it is `possiblyZero`, so each group becomes a checked divide — three
// extra ops and a three-block `try` fork — and the accumulator is live across every one of them.
//
// ⚠ **WITHOUT THIS KNOB THE LADDER COULD NOT SEE THE CONSTRUCT A1 ADDED AT ALL**, and a flat reading
// would have meant nothing: `docs/optimization-log.md` already carries the lesson under its own name —
// "'0 corpus hits' measured the instrument's blind spot, not the cost". The cost this exposes is not the
// three ops (a constant per site); it is the BLOCKS, which every block-walking pass downstream — the
// liveness sweep, the splitter, SSA destruction — is indexed by.
//
// The `otherwise` value is a plain `0` rather than a managed one on purpose: a String there would make
// this knob measure the drop-set fork as well, and that is `s_strings`' subject, not this one.
function checkedDivideSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the checked-divide knob — {groupCount} throwing `/` and `mod` groups over an opaque, POSSIBLY-ZERO divisor.\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(CheckedDivideDriver))
	// The same two answers as the bare knob, through the checked door — and the `otherwise` arm is
	// UNREACHABLE by construction (the mask leaves the divisor in 1..8), so a `try` that took its
	// fallback would fold 0 where a quotient belongs and this witness would say so. That is the one thing
	// a corpus which is compiled and never run could not check about a fallible divide.
	let divisor = corpusDivisor()
	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		out.append("\tlet d{i} = (a and {DivisorMask}) + 1\n")
		out.append("\tacc = acc + (try (a / d{i}) otherwise 0){neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue() / divisor)}\n")
		out.append(accProbe("\t"))
		out.append("\tacc = acc + (try (a mod d{i}) otherwise 0){neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue() mod divisor)}\n")
		out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	end 'eachGroup'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(CheckedDivideDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'checkedDivideSource'

let RangedReturnDriver = "scaleRangedReturns"
let RangedReturnPickName = "scaleRangedReturnPick"
let RangedReturnNarrowAlias = "ScaleRetNarrow"

// ⭐ **THE RANGED-`return` KNOB (A2a): N guarded `return` sites, all in ONE function and all returning
// the SAME parameter.** See `RangedReturnSitesBase` for what this axis is and why the corpus had none of
// it; this is the shape `Testing/ladders/genretsites.sh` generates in its `onefunc` mode, so a reading
// here and a reading there are about the same construct.
//
// THREE properties of the emitted text are load-bearing, and each closes a way the site could stop being
// a site:
//   * THE RETURNED VALUE IS A PARAMETER. `needsRuntimeGuard` settles a LITERAL `return` at compile time
//     and emits no code, so a knob returning constants would generate N sites and zero guards — the
//     blindness this exists to escape, reintroduced one level down.
//   * THE RETURN TYPE'S RANGE IS NOT FULL. `rangeIsFull` discards a full range before a site is
//     recorded, which is exactly why the corpus's existing aliases reach no guard.
//   * EVERY SITE RETURNS THE SAME PARAMETER, so all N share ONE ValueId. That is the shape a value-keyed
//     lookup must CONSUME rather than merely answer, and it is what an ordinary wide `match` returning a
//     ranged alias looks like — it is also the shape A1h's first, reverted cure got WRONG.
function rangedReturnSource(siteCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the ranged-`return` knob — {siteCount} guarded `return` sites in ONE function, every one returning the SAME parameter through a NARROWED alias.\n")
	// The upper bound IS the site count, so site `i` — which runs only when the parameter equals `i` —
	// returns a value the guard admits. The compiler cannot see that (the value is an opaque parameter,
	// so the guard is emitted at every site either way), which is the only thing the measurement needs;
	// what the derivation buys is a program that RUNS, rather than one that panics at the first site the
	// top rung adds past a bound written down as a constant.
	out.append("typealias {RangedReturnNarrowAlias} = int(0 to {siteCount})\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("function {RangedReturnPickName}(n int) returns {RangedReturnNarrowAlias}\n")

	for i in 0 upto siteCount 'eachSite'
		out.append("\tif n == {i} 'rr{i}'\n")
		out.append("\t\treturn n\n")
		out.append("\tend 'rr{i}'\n")
		out.append("\n")
	end 'eachSite'

	// A LITERAL `return` closes the body, and it does not move the knob: it is settled at compile time
	// and emits no guard.
	out.append("\treturn 0\n")
	out.append("end '{RangedReturnPickName}'\n")
	out.append("\n")
	// The driver returns a plain `int`, so it contributes no ranged site of its own and the knob stays
	// exactly `siteCount` — the same reason `genretsites.sh`'s `spread` dispatcher returns the wide alias.
	out.append(driverHeader(RangedReturnDriver))
	out.append("\tvar wit = 0\n")
	out.append("\tlet picked = {RangedReturnPickName}(a)\n")
	// WHICH site fires is the answer here, and it is known because the sites are `n == 0` … `n == N-1`
	// against a pinned argument: site `seed` if the knob is wide enough to have one, and otherwise the
	// literal `return 0` that closes the body. Both outcomes are real at different rungs — the knob is 8
	// wide at rung 0 and 256 at rung 5, and the seed is 19 — so this witness watches the guarded-return
	// path AND the un-guarded literal path as the ladder climbs, without either being written down.
	out.append(driverWitnessTail(RangedReturnDriver, valueExpr: "picked as int", expected: rangedReturnPicked(siteCount)))
	return out.build()
end 'rangedReturnSource'

// What `scaleRangedReturnPick` hands back for the pinned accumulator: the accumulator itself when one of
// the `siteCount` sites matches it, and the closing literal otherwise.
function rangedReturnPicked(siteCount KnobSize) returns TrackedValue
	if corpusSeedValue() < siteCount 'someSiteMatches'
		return corpusSeedValue()
	end 'someSiteMatches'
	return 0
end 'rangedReturnPicked'

// The mask that bounds a dynamic shift COUNT to a sane, non-negative range. A count derived this way
// is a runtime value the folder cannot see, so it takes the GUARDED shift path — the 6-7 ops that
// saturate an out-of-range count — which a constant count folds away and which nothing in this corpus
// exercised before. This is also the corpus's bitwise `and` on plain ints (the guards' `and` is the
// short-circuit one, over bools).
let ShiftCountMask = 63

let ShiftsDriver = "scaleShifts"

// The shift COVERAGE shapes. These do NOT scale — they are about COVERAGE, not the ladder: their
// value is that a future codegen change to shifts is no longer invisible, the way the `const`-flags
// fix was invisible because the corpus had no `and`/`or`. A shift codegen change moves these functions'
// emitted bytes, and the trend delta reports ANY movement, constant or not.
//
// Four axes are covered that the corpus had at ZERO before:
//   • CONSTANT count (folded at compile time) vs DYNAMIC count (a runtime value, guarded).
//   • SIGNED operand (arithmetic / sign fill) vs RANGED `int(0 to u64.max)` operand (logical / zero
//     fill). The fill is decided by the LEFT operand's type, and this is the shape that once hid a
//     fold-vs-codegen disagreement (const=0 vs dyn=-4294967296).
function shiftCoverageSource() returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: shift COVERAGE (fixed, not scaled) — const/dynamic counts over signed and ranged operands.\n")
	out.append("typealias ScaleWide = int(0 to u64.max)\n")
	out.append("typealias ScaleSigned = int(i64.min to i64.max)\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// Dynamic counts, signed operand: the guarded shl then shr.
	//
	// ⛔ **TWO COUNTS, AND THEY MUST STAY TWO.** It was `shl c` then `shr c` — one count on both sides,
	// so it computed a ROUND TRIP and any defect that moved BOTH shifts by the same amount (both counts
	// masked to 3 bits instead of 6, say) cancelled exactly and the witness read clean. A relational
	// assertion cannot catch a bug that moves both of its operands, which is what a single `c` made this.
	out.append("function scaleShiftDyn(v ScaleSigned, c ScaleSigned, d ScaleSigned) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tlet x = v shl c\n")
	out.append("\treturn x shr d\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleShiftDyn'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// Constant count, ranged (unsigned) operand: zero-fill decided by the LEFT operand's range.
	out.append("function scaleShiftWideConst(v ScaleWide) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn v shr {ShiftWideConstantCount}\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleShiftWideConst'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// Dynamic count, ranged operand: guarded, and the fill still decided by the operand's range.
	out.append("function scaleShiftWideDyn(v ScaleWide, c ScaleSigned) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn v shr c\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleShiftWideDyn'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// Constant count, signed operand: arithmetic (sign) fill.
	out.append("function scaleShiftSignedConst(v ScaleSigned) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn v shr {ShiftSignedConstantCount}\n")
	out.append("end 'scaleShiftSignedConst'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// The driver threads the opaque seed through every shift function. The seed `a` flows straight into
	// every operand, signed OR ranged: shv2's argument check is CLASS-based (numeric agrees with
	// numeric), so a plain `int` coerces into a `ScaleWide` parameter with no cast and no mask. The
	// operand's VALUE never matters here — the shift codegen inside each callee is exercised because the
	// parameter is opaque (shv2 has no interprocedural constant propagation), not because of what flows
	// in. Dynamic counts are the seed masked to a small non-negative range so they stay runtime values.
	//
	// ⭐ THE OPERAND'S VALUE NOW MATTERS AFTER ALL — NOT TO THE CODEGEN, BUT TO THE ANSWER. Each call is
	// witnessed against the same shift this generator wrote, applied to the pinned seed with the same
	// operators. The paragraph above is still true of what is MEASURED.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **BUT THE FILL IS NOT AMONG WHAT THESE SIX WITNESS, AND THIS COMMENT CLAIMED IT WAS** (BATCH10
	// review). It said a shift "which filled with sign where it should have filled with zero changes one of
	// these six numbers", naming the `const=0` vs `dyn=-4294967296` defect — and then said, four lines
	// down, that the seed is non-negative and small so the logical and arithmetic forms AGREE on it. Both
	// cannot be true, and it is the second one: with the seed at 19, arithmetic and logical `shr` answer
	// identically on every operand here, so **no fill disagreement is representable in this block.**
	//
	// ⚠ **WORSE, FOUR OF THE SIX ARE DEGENERATE AT THIS SEED, AND THE EMITTED CORPUS SHOWS IT.** `19 shr 60`
	// is 0 and `19 shr 19` is 0, so `neutralizeText` appends NOTHING to the wide-const, wide-dyn and
	// signed-const sites — read `rung0/h_shifts.maxon` and they are bare `acc = acc + scaleShift…(a)`. Each
	// asserts only *"the callee returned zero"*, which is what a `shr` hard-answering 0 also does. The
	// fourth, `scaleShiftDyn`, is `(v shl c) shr c` with ONE `c`: a defect moving both operands together —
	// both counts masked to 3 bits rather than 6 — is exactly cancelled. Only the two INLINE sites
	// (`a shl 3`, `a shr 2`) carry non-zero expectations, and both counts are constants the folder sees.
	//
	// ⇒ **What this block genuinely witnesses today is that four shift callees are reached and return the
	// right small number; it does NOT witness the fill, and it cannot while the operand is a small
	// non-negative seed.** Making it discriminating needs an operand with a high bit set — a different
	// emitted program, so a different ladder — which is why the correction here is to the CLAIM and the
	// widening is left to be scheduled rather than smuggled into a review.
	//
	// ⚠ The seed is NON-NEGATIVE and far below 2^60, which is why the wide (logical) and signed
	// (arithmetic) forms agree on it and why the round trip below recovers its operand — the check on the
	// next line is what keeps that true if the seed ever moves.
	let firstCount = corpusSeedValue() and ShiftCountMask
	let secondCount = (corpusSeedValue() shr ShiftSecondCountShift) and ShiftSecondCountMask
	let highOperand = corpusSeedValue() - ShiftHighBitOffset

	// The three properties every expectation below is derived under, checked rather than assumed — the
	// seed is one `let` away from moving, and each of these fails SILENTLY if it does (see the header:
	// this block shipped once with three sites whose expectations were all zero).
	if corpusSeedValue() < 0 or corpusSeedValue() >= ShiftHighBitOffset 'seedOutOfShiftRange'
		panic("ScaleCorpus.shiftCoverageSource: the seed is {corpusSeedValue()}, and this block needs it non-negative AND below {ShiftHighBitOffset} so that `a - {ShiftHighBitOffset}` is NEGATIVE — bit 63 set is the whole reason the logical and arithmetic fills answer differently here")
	end 'seedOutOfShiftRange'
	if firstCount == secondCount 'countsWouldCancel'
		panic("ScaleCorpus.shiftCoverageSource: both dynamic shift counts derive to {firstCount}, so `shl c` then `shr d` is a round trip again and a defect moving both shifts together cancels exactly — the two counts must be independent")
	end 'countsWouldCancel'

	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(ShiftsDriver))

	// ⭐ **THE OPERAND WITH BIT 63 SET, AND IT IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE BLOCK.** Every i64 below zero
	// has its top bit set, so `a - {ShiftHighBitOffset}` is the shortest opaque way to get one; it stays
	// opaque because `a` is (shv2 has no interprocedural constant propagation), and the generator knows
	// its value because the seed is pinned. Fed to BOTH constant-count callees below, whose bodies are
	// identical but for the operand's declared RANGE — so `scaleShiftWideConst` must answer 15 and
	// `scaleShiftSignedConst` must answer -1, and a compiler that picked one fill for both is caught by
	// whichever it got wrong. On the old non-negative `a` the two agreed and neither could be wrong.
	out.append("\tlet hi = a - {ShiftHighBitOffset}\n")

	// Two INDEPENDENT dynamic counts — see `scaleShiftDyn`. Both are runtime values derived from the
	// opaque seed, so neither folds, and they are DIFFERENT so the shl/shr pair cannot cancel.
	out.append("\tlet c1 = a and {ShiftCountMask}\n")
	out.append("\tlet c2 = (a shr {ShiftSecondCountShift}) and {ShiftSecondCountMask}\n")

	out.append("\tacc = acc + scaleShiftWideConst(hi){neutralizeText(logicalShr(highOperand, count: ShiftWideConstantCount))}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	out.append("\tacc = acc + scaleShiftWideDyn(hi, c: c1){neutralizeText(logicalShr(highOperand, count: firstCount))}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	out.append("\tacc = acc + scaleShiftSignedConst(hi){neutralizeText(highOperand shr ShiftSignedConstantCount)}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	out.append("\tacc = acc + scaleShiftDyn(hi, c: c1, d: c2){neutralizeText((highOperand shl firstCount) shr secondCount)}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	out.append("\tacc = acc + (a shl {ShiftInlineLeftCount}){neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue() shl ShiftInlineLeftCount)}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	// The inline right shift takes the high-bit operand too, so the ARITHMETIC fill is witnessed at an
	// inline site as well as through a callee — the two reach different codegen (`binOpImm` vs a call).
	out.append("\tacc = acc + (hi shr {ShiftInlineRightCount}){neutralizeText(highOperand shr ShiftInlineRightCount)}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	out.append(accWitnessTail(ShiftsDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'shiftCoverageSource'

// ⭐⭐ **ZERO-FILL `shr`, COMPUTED WITHOUT AN UNSIGNED TYPE — the generator's half of the fill witness.**
// The emitted program gets its logical fill from the operand's declared RANGE (`ScaleWide`'s low bound
// is 0, which is what makes `shr` zero-fill). This must predict that answer WITHOUT asking the same
// question the same way, or the witness would be a relational assertion over one mechanism: a fill
// defect would move the prediction and the prediction's subject together, and cancel.
//
// So it is stated as arithmetic instead: shift, then MASK OFF the sign bits the arithmetic fill dragged
// in. `(x shr n) and ((1 shl (64 - n)) - 1)` — the surviving bits of a 64-bit logical shift are exactly
// the low `64 - n`. VERIFIED against the compiler for both counts this block uses: at `hi = -81` it
// answers 15 for n=60 and 35,184,372,088,831 for n=19, which is what `scaleShiftWideConst` and
// `scaleShiftWideDyn` return.
//
// ⚠ A cast — `(x as ScaleWide) shr n` — was the obvious spelling and is the wrong one twice over: it
// asks the fill question through the very mechanism under test, and a negative value cast into a
// `0 to u64.max` alias is a range violation rather than a reinterpretation.
function logicalShr(value TrackedValue, count TrackedValue) returns TrackedValue
	if count <= 0 or count >= TrackedValueBits 'countOutOfRange'
		panic("ScaleCorpus.logicalShr: a count of {count} has no 64-bit logical form here — the mask `(1 shl (64 - count)) - 1` is only a mask for a count strictly inside (0, {TrackedValueBits})")
	end 'countOutOfRange'

	let surviving = (1 shl (TrackedValueBits - count)) - 1
	return (value shr count) and surviving
end 'logicalShr'

// The width the mask above is taken over. Named because it is a property of `TrackedValue`'s i64
// representation and not a free choice — change the tracked width and the mask is wrong, silently.
let TrackedValueBits = 64

// The four shift counts this block writes. Each is a bare literal in two programs at once — the emitted
// shift, and the tracker's reading of it.
//
// The two constant counts are 60 because a 64-bit value shifted right by 60 is what makes the FILL
// visible, and they are DELIBERATELY EQUAL: `scaleShiftWideConst` and `scaleShiftSignedConst` are then
// the same body over the same count, differing in the operand's declared RANGE alone, so the pair is a
// controlled experiment on the fill and on nothing else. At `hi = -81` they must answer 15 and -1.
let ShiftWideConstantCount = 60
let ShiftSignedConstantCount = 60
let ShiftInlineLeftCount = 3
let ShiftInlineRightCount = 2

// How the SECOND dynamic count is taken off the seed. It only has to be independent of `a and 63` and
// stay a runtime value; shifting first and masking narrower is the cheapest way to get both. At seed 19
// it gives 4 against the first count's 19, and `shiftCoverageSource` refuses if they ever coincide.
let ShiftSecondCountShift = 2
let ShiftSecondCountMask = 7

// What is subtracted from the opaque seed to build an operand with BIT 63 SET. Any negative i64 has it,
// so the only requirement is that the result is below zero — which is what the seed guard enforces, and
// why this is larger than the seed rather than any particular bit pattern.
//
// ⚠ It replaced `ShiftRoundTripHeadroom`, which existed so that `(v shl c) shr c` returned `v`. That
// round trip is gone on purpose: returning `v` is precisely the property that let a defect moving both
// shifts cancel, so there is no longer a round trip to leave headroom for.
let ShiftHighBitOffset = 100

// The remaining SCALAR coverage shapes, and the one aggregate shape whose COUNT is not interesting.
// Fixed-size, like `shiftCoverageSource` and for the same reason: a codegen change to any of these must
// stop being invisible, and the trend delta reports ANY movement, constant or not.
//
// Six axes are covered that the corpus had at ZERO before:
//   • LOGICAL `or` / `xor` / `not` over bools — `or` is short-circuit control flow (two blocks and a
//     merge phi, like the `and` guards the corpus already had), `xor` is NOT (it must evaluate both), and
//     `not` picks its opcode from its operand's type. Three different lowerings behind one keyword each.
//   • BITWISE `or` / `xor` / `not` over ints — the same three keywords, a different band entirely.
//   • An `as` cast over an OPAQUE value, which emits a runtime RANGE GUARD. A cast of a literal is
//     checked at compile time and emits nothing, so only an opaque operand measures the guard.
//   • `print` — the whole formatted-output path, which nothing in the corpus reached.
//   • A loop that actually `break`s. `break` was in the subset from M4b and the manifest said, in plain
//     text, "in the subset, but no generated loop exits early": a loop EXIT phi carrying live values is a
//     different merge from a loop header phi, and no knob was minting one.
//   • A struct-typed FIELD written through the loaded box (`o.inner.x = ...`). This is a SHAPE, not a
//     dimension — `structAllocs` already doubles the create-site count, and nesting depth is capped at 2
//     on purpose, so what matters here is that the shape exists at all.
function scalarCoverageSource() returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: scalar + aggregate COVERAGE (fixed, not scaled) — logical and bitwise or/xor/not, an `as` guard, print, a break, a nested field write.\n")
	out.append("typealias {CoverageNarrowAlias} = int(0 to {CoverageNarrowUpperBound})\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// LOGICAL or / xor / not: `or` short-circuits (blocks + a merge phi), `xor` cannot, `not` inverts.
	out.append("function {CoverageBoolName}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tlet hi = a > {CoverageBoolHighBound}\n")
	out.append("\tlet lo = a < {CoverageBoolLowBound}\n")
	out.append("\tif hi or lo 'either'\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn a + 1\n")
	out.append("\tend 'either'\n")
	out.append("\tif hi xor lo 'oneOnly'\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn a + 2\n")
	out.append("\tend 'oneOnly'\n")
	out.append("\tif not hi 'small'\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn a + 3\n")
	out.append("\tend 'small'\n")
	out.append("\treturn a + 4\n")
	out.append("end '{CoverageBoolName}'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// BITWISE or / xor / not: the same three keywords over ints, which is a different band.
	out.append("function {CoverageBitsName}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tlet m = a or {CoverageBitSetMask}\n")
	out.append("\tlet x = m xor {CoverageBitFlipMask}\n")
	out.append("\treturn x and not {CoverageBitClearMask}\n")
	out.append("end '{CoverageBitsName}'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// An `as` cast over an OPAQUE value — a runtime range guard. The mask keeps the value inside the
	// alias's range, but the compiler cannot prove that, so the guard is emitted either way. What is
	// MEASURED is the guard's branch; what is now also CHECKED, because the rungs run, is that the mask
	// really does keep the value inside the range — a guard that fired would abort the rung.
	out.append("function {CoverageCastName}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tlet n = (a and {CoverageNarrowUpperBound}) as {CoverageNarrowAlias}\n")
	out.append("\treturn n as int\n")
	out.append("end '{CoverageCastName}'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// A loop that EXITS EARLY: the break's edge carries the loop-carried values into the exit phi.
	out.append("function {CoverageBreakName}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar total = a\n")
	out.append("\tvar i = 0\n")
	out.append("\twhile i < {CoverageBreakTrips} 'loop'\n")
	out.append("\t\ttotal = total + i\n")
	out.append("\t\tif total > {CoverageBreakLimit} 'enough'\n")
	out.append("\t\t\tbreak\n")
	out.append("\t\tend 'enough'\n")
	out.append("\t\ti = i + 1\n")
	out.append("\tend 'loop'\n")
	out.append("\treturn total\n")
	out.append("end '{CoverageBreakName}'\n")
	out.append("\n")

	// A struct-typed FIELD, written through the loaded box. Nesting stops at depth 2 deliberately: the
	// cloner/destructor cascades are a needs-FIXPOINT whose pass count is the deepest chain, and a
	// machine-generated deep chain is exactly what makes that superlinear.
	//
	// The inner type is the SHARED plain-struct declaration, so this shape cannot drift from the one both
	// struct knobs measure — only the OUTER type, whose field is struct-typed, is bespoke here.
	out.append(structTypeDecl(CoverageInnerName))
	out.append("\ntype {CoverageOuterName}\n")
	out.append("\texport var inner as {CoverageInnerName}\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("\tstatic function create(inner {CoverageInnerName}) returns Self\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn Self\{inner: inner\}\n")
	out.append("\tend 'create'\n")
	out.append("end '{CoverageOuterName}'\n")
	out.append("\n")
	// FILE-PRIVATE, so it spells its own header rather than taking `witnessedDriverHeader`'s exported one
	// — but it carries the same pair of accumulators and the same tail, because it has TWO expectations
	// and two is all it takes to cancel. Its caller then expects it to hand back the argument, exactly as
	// `main` expects of a driver.
	out.append("function {CoverageNestedName}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = a\n")
	out.append("\tvar wit = 0\n")
	var nested = StringBuilder.create()
	nested.append("\t\tvar o = {CoverageOuterName}.create({CoverageInnerName}.create(a, y: a + 1))\n")
	nested.append("\t\to.inner.x = a + {CoverageNestedFieldOffset}\n")
	// Read back through the loaded box, so the witness is on the WRITE: `o.inner.x` holds what the line
	// above stored, and a nested field store that landed at the wrong offset moves it.
	nested.append("\t\tacc = acc + o.inner.x{neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue() + CoverageNestedFieldOffset)}\n")
	nested.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
	// `structTypeDecl` emits a per-type READER beside its type, and `deadFunctions` is a REAL pass in
	// this pipeline — an uncalled one is legitimately deleted, and this file's own rule (see `mainSource`)
	// is that a generated declaration nothing reaches is a dimension that evaporates silently. So the
	// inner type's reader is called here, which also puts a struct across a CALL inside the coverage file.
	nested.append("\t\tvar q = {CoverageInnerName}.create(a, y: a + {CoverageReaderFieldOffset})\n")
	nested.append("\t\tacc = acc + {structReaderName(CoverageInnerName)}(q){neutralizeText(2 * corpusSeedValue() + CoverageReaderFieldOffset)}\n")
	nested.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
	out.append(ownedSiteBlock("nested", body: nested.build()))
	out.append(accWitnessTail(CoverageNestedName))
	out.append("\n")

	// The driver threads the opaque seed through every coverage function, and PRINTS — the one `print` in
	// the whole corpus, which is the whole of what makes the formatted-output path visible here.
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(CoverageDriver))
	out.append("\tacc = acc + {CoverageBoolName}(a){neutralizeText(coverageBoolValue(corpusSeedValue()))}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	out.append("\tacc = acc + {CoverageBitsName}(a){neutralizeText(coverageBitsValue(corpusSeedValue()))}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	out.append("\tacc = acc + {CoverageCastName}(a){neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue() and CoverageNarrowUpperBound)}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	out.append("\tacc = acc + {CoverageBreakName}(a){neutralizeText(coverageBreakValue(corpusSeedValue()))}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	// The nested block pins its own accumulator and hands it back, so this call contributes the seed.
	out.append("\tacc = acc + {CoverageNestedName}(a){neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue())}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	out.append("\tprint(\"scale coverage \{acc\}\\n\")\n")
	out.append(accWitnessTail(CoverageDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'scalarCoverageSource'

// What the LOGICAL or/xor/not chain answers — the same four-arm ladder the emitter writes, over the
// pinned seed. The arms are a real ladder rather than a formula because that is what the emitted function
// is: `or` short-circuits, `xor` does not, and which of the four `return`s is reached is exactly what a
// miscompiled short-circuit would change.
function coverageBoolValue(argument TrackedValue) returns TrackedValue
	let hi = argument > CoverageBoolHighBound
	let lo = argument < CoverageBoolLowBound

	if hi or lo 'either'
		return argument + 1
	end 'either'

	if hi xor lo 'oneOnly'
		return argument + 2
	end 'oneOnly'

	if not hi 'small'
		return argument + 3
	end 'small'
	return argument + 4
end 'coverageBoolValue'

// The BITWISE band's answer, with the same three operators in the same order as the emitted body.
function coverageBitsValue(argument TrackedValue) returns TrackedValue
	let m = argument or CoverageBitSetMask
	let x = m xor CoverageBitFlipMask
	return x and not CoverageBitClearMask
end 'coverageBitsValue'

// Where the early-`break` loop stops, SOLVED rather than stepped: after adding index `i` the running
// total is `argument + i(i+1)/2`, so the loop leaves at the first triangular number that carries it past
// the limit — and if none does inside the trip budget, at the budget's own total.
//
// ⚠ THE `break` MUST ACTUALLY HAPPEN for this knob to be what its comment claims, and the two outcomes
// are different numbers here, so a trip budget or a limit that stopped the exit edge from being taken
// would move this witness rather than quietly turning the knob into an ordinary loop.
function coverageBreakValue(argument TrackedValue) returns TrackedValue
	for i in 0 upto CoverageBreakTrips 'eachTrip'
		let total = argument + (i * (i + 1)) / 2
		if total > CoverageBreakLimit 'breaks'
			return total
		end 'breaks'
	end 'eachTrip'
	return argument + ((CoverageBreakTrips - 1) * CoverageBreakTrips) / 2
end 'coverageBreakValue'

// ===========================================================================================
// The ten knobs for P1.1b..P1.7a, as source
// ===========================================================================================

// The names each of the ten knobs below declares and then reads back — spelled in a DECLARATION and in a
// USE many lines apart, so each is named once here for the reason `smallFunctionName` exists: a prefix
// written down twice is a prefix that can drift, and the failure is not a bad measurement but a generated
// program that does not compile at all.
let EnumTypePrefix = "ScaleEnum"
let EnumCasePrefix = "ec"
let EnumPickPrefix = "scaleEnumPick"
let EnumTypesDriver = "scaleEnumTypes"

let MatchDenseEnumName = "ScaleMatchDense"
let MatchDenseCasePrefix = "md"
let MatchDensePickName = "scaleMatchDensePick"
let MatchSparseEnumName = "ScaleMatchSparse"
let MatchSparseCasePrefix = "mp"
let MatchSparsePickName = "scaleMatchSparsePick"
let MatchSitesDriver = "scaleMatchSites"

let StringOpsDriver = "scaleStringOps"
let StringSearchOpsDriver = "scaleStringSearchOps"
let StringIndexOpsDriver = "scaleStringIndexOps"
let StringTrimOpsDriver = "scaleStringTrimOps"
let StringViewOpsDriver = "scaleStringViewOps"

let CharOpsDriver = "scaleCharOps"

let ManagedStructPrefix = "ScaleNamed"
let ManagedStructValueAlias = "ScaleNamedValue"
let ManagedStructsDriver = "scaleManagedStructs"

let ArrayElementAlias = "ScaleElement"
let IntArrayAlias = "ScaleIntArray"
let ArrayOpsDriver = "scaleArrayOps"

let StringArrayAlias = "ScaleStringArray"
let ManagedRowValueAlias = "ScaleRowValue"
let ManagedRowName = "ScaleRow"
let ManagedRowArrayAlias = "ScaleRowArray"
let ManagedArrayOpsDriver = "scaleManagedArrayOps"

let GenericElementAlias = "ScaleGenElement"
let GenericBasePrefix = "ScaleGBase"
let GenericBaseInstPrefix = "ScaleGBaseInst"
let GenericBasesDriver = "scaleGenericBases"

let GenericInstanceBaseName = "ScaleXBox"
let GenericInstanceElemPrefix = "ScaleXElem"
let GenericInstanceHoldPrefix = "ScaleXHold"
let GenericInstanceHoldValueAlias = "ScaleXHoldValue"
let GenericInstancePrefix = "ScaleXInst"
let GenericInstancesDriver = "scaleGenericInstances"

let TryCoOwnBaseName = "ScaleTcBox"
let TryCoOwnErrorName = "ScaleTcBoom"
let TryCoOwnHolderName = "ScaleTcHolder"
let TryCoOwnHolderValueAlias = "ScaleTcHoldValue"
let TryCoOwnHolderBoxAlias = "ScaleTcHolderBox"
let TryCoOwnStringBoxAlias = "ScaleTcStringBox"
let TryCoOwnHolderConsumerName = "scaleTcConsumeHolder"
let TryCoOwnStringConsumerName = "scaleTcConsumeString"
let TryCoOwnDriver = "scaleTryCoOwn"

let InterfacePrefix = "ScaleProto"
let ConformerPrefix = "ScaleUnit"
let InterfaceMeasurePrefix = "ScaleMeasure"
let InterfaceFileDriverPrefix = "scaleIfaceFile"

let DispatchInterfacePrefix = "ScaleDispProto"
let DispatchConformerPrefix = "ScaleDisp"
let DispatchMeasureAlias = "ScaleDispMeasure"
let IfaceDispatchDriver = "scaleIfaceDispatch"

// ⚠⚠ **`ByteAliasName` IS NOT THIS FILE'S TO CHOOSE, AND RENAMING IT BREAKS THE KNOB SILENTLY-ISH.**
// `__ManagedMemory` in a DECLARED position resolves to the `Array` instance whose element is the interned
// name `Byte` (`SignatureIndex.internArrayByteInstance`, over `ByteElementTypeName`) — the element is
// matched by NAME, not by range. So `typealias ScaleBufByte = int(0 to u8.max)` produces
// `Array_ScaleBufByte`, and `function f(a ScaleBufArray) returns __ManagedMemory` is then **E3005 Cannot
// return 'Array_ScaleBufByte' from function declared to return 'Array_Byte'** — measured, and it is a hard
// compile failure that would take the whole ladder down as a BROKEN RUN rather than skew a number.
let ByteAliasName = "Byte"
let ManagedMemoryTypeSpelling = "__ManagedMemory"

let BufferSurfaceArrayAlias = "ScaleBufArray"
let BufferSurfaceOfBuffersAlias = "ScaleBufOfBufs"
let BufferSurfaceHoldName = "ScaleBufHold"
let BufferSurfaceHoldFieldName = "buf"
let BufferSurfaceHoldValueAlias = "ScaleBufHoldValue"
let BufferSurfaceReadName = "scaleBufLength"
let BufferSurfaceReturnPrefix = "scaleBufferOf"
let BufferSurfacesDriver = "scaleBufferSurfaces"

let CoverageNarrowAlias = "ScaleCoverNarrow"
let CoverageBoolName = "scaleCoverBool"
let CoverageBitsName = "scaleCoverBits"
let CoverageCastName = "scaleCoverCast"
let CoverageBreakName = "scaleCoverBreak"
let CoverageInnerName = "ScaleCoverInner"
let CoverageOuterName = "ScaleCoverOuter"
let CoverageNestedName = "scaleCoverNested"
let CoverageDriver = "scaleCoverage"

// The coverage block's bounds and masks. Named because a bare literal in emitted source is a magic
// value in two programs at once — this one and the one it generates.
let CoverageNarrowUpperBound = 4095
let CoverageBoolHighBound = 10
let CoverageBoolLowBound = 1000000
let CoverageBitSetMask = 5
let CoverageBitFlipMask = 9
let CoverageBitClearMask = 15
let CoverageBreakTrips = 100
let CoverageBreakLimit = 1000

// The two field offsets the nested-write shape uses: what `o.inner.x` is set to above the accumulator,
// and what the second inner struct's `y` is built with. Named because each is now read twice — once into
// the emitted source, once into the witness that reads it back.
let CoverageNestedFieldOffset = 1
let CoverageReaderFieldOffset = 2

// The two interface method names every conforming type in the corpus implements. Plain names, not
// per-interface ones: a method is TYPE-scoped, so `ScaleUnit0.probe` and `ScaleUnit1.probe` are distinct
// functions and the whole-program duplicate check (E3006) never sees a clash — exactly as every generated
// type's `create` already proves.
let InterfaceProbeMethod = "probe"
let InterfaceScaleMethod = "scaleBy"

// The `enumTypes` knob: N `enum` declarations, each with an opaque PICKER and one exhaustive `match`.
//
// THE SCRUTINEE COMES FROM A CALL, which is this file's founding rule reaching a new construct. A
// `match` on `Color.green` is a match on a value the front end knows, and while nothing folds a `match`
// today, a corpus whose every scrutinee is a literal is one SCCP away from measuring nothing. The picker
// branches on the opaque `acc` and returns a different case per arm, so the tag is a call result — the
// second of the exactly two things `foldConstOperands` cannot see through.
//
// The arms are `EnumCasesPerType` explicit BARE case names (a qualified `Color.red` in an arm is E3075,
// and a plain `default` on an enum is E2046). Contiguous ordinals at four intervals is the DENSE case, so
// each of these matches lowers to a jump table.
//
// ⚠ MANY SMALL MATCHES, NEVER ONE BIG ONE. The duplicate-pattern check (E2027) is pairwise O(arms²) —
// deliberately, since it must compare every pattern against every other — so hundreds of arms in one
// match would make compile time quadratic in that one construct and the knob would be measuring the
// check rather than the language. Four arms is sixteen comparisons.
function enumTypesSource(typeCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the enum-types knob — {typeCount} enum declarations of {EnumCasesPerType} cases, each picked opaquely and matched exhaustively.\n")

	for t in 0 upto typeCount 'eachType'
		let enumName = "{EnumTypePrefix}{t}"
		out.append("\nenum {enumName}\n")
		for c in 0 upto EnumCasesPerType 'eachCase'
			out.append("\t{EnumCasePrefix}{c}\n")
		end 'eachCase'
		out.append("end '{enumName}'\n")
		out.append(enumPickerSource("{EnumPickPrefix}{t}", enumName: enumName, casePrefix: EnumCasePrefix, salt: t))
	end 'eachType'

	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(EnumTypesDriver))
	// ⭐ THE ARMS ARE UNTOUCHED AND THE NEUTRALIZATION SITS AFTER THE `match`, WHICH IS THE POINT. Folding
	// the expectation into each arm's own constant would make the arm this scrutinee actually selects read
	// `acc = acc + 0` — a statement the folder can settle, in the one arm whose continuation phi the
	// statement-form dispatch exists to mint. Subtracting once afterwards leaves all four arms exactly as
	// they were and still catches a dispatch that landed on the wrong one.
	for t in 0 upto typeCount 'eachUse'
		out.append("\tlet v{t} = {EnumPickPrefix}{t}(a)\n")
		out.append("\tmatch v{t} 'em{t}'\n")
		for c in 0 upto EnumCasesPerType 'eachArm'
			out.append("\t\t{EnumCasePrefix}{c} then acc = acc + {c + 1}\n")
		end 'eachArm'
		out.append("\tend 'em{t}'\n")
		out.append("\tacc = acc{neutralizeText(enumPickerCase(t) + 1)}\n")
		out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	end 'eachUse'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(EnumTypesDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'enumTypesSource'

// The OPAQUE TAG SOURCE every `match` in this corpus dispatches on: a function that branches on the
// opaque accumulator and returns a different enum case per arm, so its result is a call result and
// therefore invisible to the folder.
//
// Shared by both enum knobs, so the two measure the same opacity trick against different dimensions —
// how many enums exist, and how many sites dispatch over a fixed pair of them. `salt` varies the branch
// constant so the pickers are not textually identical, which would benchmark the parser's cache.
function enumPickerSource(pickerName String, enumName String, casePrefix String, salt KnobIndex) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\nfunction {pickerName}(a int) returns {enumName}\n")
	out.append("\tif a > {enumPickerThreshold(salt)} 'hi'\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn {enumName}.{casePrefix}{EnumCasesPerType - 1}\n")
	out.append("\tend 'hi'\n")
	out.append("\treturn {enumName}.{casePrefix}0\n")
	out.append("end '{pickerName}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'enumPickerSource'

// The branch constant the picker for `salt` tests against, and the ORDINAL it therefore hands back for
// the pinned accumulator. Two facts about one emitted `if`, stated beside it: every `match` witness in
// the corpus needs the second, and the second cannot be right unless it reads the first from here.
function enumPickerThreshold(salt KnobIndex) returns KnobSize
	return salt mod 7 + 1
end 'enumPickerThreshold'

function enumPickerCase(salt KnobIndex) returns KnobIndex
	if corpusSeedValue() > enumPickerThreshold(salt) 'highArm'
		return EnumCasesPerType - 1
	end 'highArm'
	return 0
end 'enumPickerCase'

// The `matchSites` knob: M `match` sites over a FIXED pair of enums, ROTATING OVER ALL THREE DISPATCH
// STRATEGIES — because `Parser.emitDispatchRange` picks one of three from the normalized interval list,
// and a corpus that generated only one of them would measure a third of the lowering. (That is exactly
// what `IntDivideBase` records: the corpus was full of `/` and every one of them was a FLOAT `/`.)
//
//   • SHAPE 0 — STATEMENT form (`then`), `EnumCasesPerType` explicit arms over CONTIGUOUS ordinals. Four
//     intervals covering four slots is 100% dense, so this is the JUMP TABLE: an `.rdata` slot table and
//     an indirect jump. It is also the only shape whose arms ASSIGN rather than give, so it is the one
//     that mints the continuation phis a statement match needs.
//   • SHAPE 1 — EXPRESSION form (`gives`) with a RANGE arm folding all but the last case. Two intervals
//     is below `MinIntervalsForDispatch`, so this is the LINEAR COMPARE CHAIN. Range arms are in ORDINAL
//     (declaration) order, which is what `lo to hi` means here.
//   • SHAPE 2 — EXPRESSION form over the SPARSE enum, whose cases are `MatchSparseCaseStride` apart. Four
//     intervals again, but the covered span is far too wide for a table to be worth its slots, so this is
//     the BALANCED BINARY SEARCH.
//
// All three take their scrutinee from a picker call, for the reason `enumPickerSource` states.
function matchSitesSource(siteCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the match-sites knob — {siteCount} match sites rotating over all {MatchDispatchShapeCount} dispatch strategies (jump table, linear chain, binary search).\n")

	out.append("\nenum {MatchDenseEnumName}\n")
	for c in 0 upto EnumCasesPerType 'eachDense'
		out.append("\t{MatchDenseCasePrefix}{c}\n")
	end 'eachDense'
	out.append("end '{MatchDenseEnumName}'\n")
	out.append(enumPickerSource(MatchDensePickName, enumName: MatchDenseEnumName, casePrefix: MatchDenseCasePrefix, salt: MatchDenseSalt))

	out.append("\nenum {MatchSparseEnumName}\n")
	for c in 0 upto EnumCasesPerType 'eachSparse'
		out.append("\t{MatchSparseCasePrefix}{c} = {c * MatchSparseCaseStride}\n")
	end 'eachSparse'
	out.append("end '{MatchSparseEnumName}'\n")
	out.append(enumPickerSource(MatchSparsePickName, enumName: MatchSparseEnumName, casePrefix: MatchSparseCasePrefix, salt: MatchSparseSalt))

	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(MatchSitesDriver))
	for i in 0 upto siteCount 'eachSite'
		match i mod MatchDispatchShapeCount 'shape'
			0 then out.append(matchDenseStatementSite(i))
			1 then out.append(matchRangeExpressionSite(i))
			2 then out.append(matchSparseExpressionSite(i))
			// `MatchDispatchShapeCount` and this arm set are the same fact stated twice — the count, and the
			// shapes that realize it. This is the CHECK between the copies: raise the constant without
			// adding a shape and the ladder stops loudly here rather than silently generating fewer
			// strategies than the manifest claims.
			default panic("matchSitesSource: MatchDispatchShapeCount is {MatchDispatchShapeCount} but shape {i mod MatchDispatchShapeCount} has no emitter — add one beside matchDenseStatementSite / matchRangeExpressionSite / matchSparseExpressionSite, or lower the constant")
		end 'shape'
	end 'eachSite'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(MatchSitesDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'matchSitesSource'

// The two pickers this knob's sites dispatch on, by the salt each was emitted with — read back here so
// every shape below knows which ordinal reaches its arms.
let MatchDenseSalt = 2 as KnobSize
let MatchSparseSalt = 5 as KnobSize

// SHAPE 0 — a STATEMENT match whose arms assign: dense contiguous ordinals, so a jump table.
function matchDenseStatementSite(index KnobIndex) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\tlet mv{index} = {MatchDensePickName}(a)\n")
	out.append("\tmatch mv{index} 'ms{index}'\n")
	for c in 0 upto EnumCasesPerType 'eachArm'
		out.append("\t\t{MatchDenseCasePrefix}{c} then acc = acc + {(index + c) mod 7 + 1}\n")
	end 'eachArm'
	out.append("\tend 'ms{index}'\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc{neutralizeText((index + enumPickerCase(MatchDenseSalt)) mod 7 + 1)}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	return out.build()
end 'matchDenseStatementSite'

// SHAPE 1 — an EXPRESSION match with a RANGE arm: two intervals, so a linear compare chain.
//
// Its two arms give different values, so which one the scrutinee reaches decides the expectation — and
// the RANGE arm covering all but the last case is exactly the interval a wrong lowering would widen or
// narrow. That is what this witness is for.
function matchRangeExpressionSite(index KnobIndex) returns String
	var arms = StringBuilder.create()
	arms.append("\t\t{MatchDenseCasePrefix}0 to {MatchDenseCasePrefix}{EnumCasesPerType - 2} gives {index mod 9 + 1}\n")
	arms.append("\t\t{MatchDenseCasePrefix}{EnumCasesPerType - 1} gives {index mod 6 + 2}\n")

	var given = index mod 9 + 1
	if enumPickerCase(MatchDenseSalt) == EnumCasesPerType - 1 'lastCase'
		given = index mod 6 + 2
	end 'lastCase'
	return matchExpressionSite(index, pickName: MatchDensePickName, arms: arms.build(), given: given)
end 'matchRangeExpressionSite'

// SHAPE 2 — an EXPRESSION match over the SPARSE enum: four far-apart intervals, so a binary search.
function matchSparseExpressionSite(index KnobIndex) returns String
	var arms = StringBuilder.create()
	for c in 0 upto EnumCasesPerType 'eachArm'
		arms.append("\t\t{MatchSparseCasePrefix}{c} gives {(index + c) mod 8 + 1}\n")
	end 'eachArm'
	return matchExpressionSite(index, pickName: MatchSparsePickName, arms: arms.build(), given: (index + enumPickerCase(MatchSparseSalt)) mod 8 + 1)
end 'matchSparseExpressionSite'

// The EXPRESSION-form wrapper both expression shapes sit inside: the opaque scrutinee, the `let … = match`
// binding, and the fold of the match's value back into `acc`. Written once because the two shapes differ
// only in their ARM SET — which enum they dispatch on and how its cases are grouped — and that difference
// is the point of having both; the scaffolding around it is not, and two copies of it would be two places
// for the result to stop flowing into `acc` and the whole site to become deletable dead code.
function matchExpressionSite(index KnobIndex, pickName String, arms String, given TrackedValue) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\tlet mv{index} = {pickName}(a)\n")
	out.append("\tlet mr{index} = match mv{index} 'ms{index}'\n")
	out.append(arms)
	out.append("\tend 'ms{index}'\n")
	out.append("\tacc = acc + mr{index}{neutralizeText(given)}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	return out.build()
end 'matchExpressionSite'

// The `stringOps` knob: M String operation groups, each in its own block for `ownedSiteBlock`'s reason.
//
// ⚠ THIS FILE'S FOUNDING TRAP BITES STRINGS HARDEST, AND NOT THE WAY IT LOOKS. A pile of string LITERALS
// does not fold — there is no string folding — but it also measures almost nothing that a real program
// does, because every literal is a fixed `.rdata` blob with no runtime work behind it. The opacity here
// therefore has to be structural rather than defensive: the INTERPOLATION formats the opaque accumulator
// (an int→String conversion that allocates), and `byteLength()` is the way back OUT of the String domain,
// exactly as `trunc` is for floats. Without that return path the whole group would be dead code that a
// future DCE pass could legitimately delete, taking the String dimension with it.
//
// The literals are deliberately LONG. A short string lives inline in its 56-byte record with no heap
// allocation at all, so a corpus of short literals would measure the String type and none of the heap
// underneath it.
//
// ⚠ THE INTERPOLATION IS NOT INSIDE A LOOP. A 14-GPR pool refuses a 16-live-value interpolation in a loop
// body with E5001 (PLAN.md #76) — a FATAL compile error that would take the whole ladder down, not a
// warning. Each group here is straight-line inside a single `if`, so peak pressure is a handful.
function stringOpsSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the string-ops knob — {groupCount} groups of literal + interpolation + append + byteLength + ==.\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(StringOpsDriver))

	// ⭐ THE APPEND'S RESULT IS WITNESSED WITHOUT KNOWING THE INTERPOLATION'S WIDTH. `sv{i}` is the literal
	// followed by `st{i}`, so its byte length is the literal's plus `st{i}`'s — a relation this generator
	// created by writing the `append`, and one that needs no arithmetic on how many digits the accumulator
	// prints as. That keeps the interpolation genuinely opaque while still being checked: a conversion
	// that produced the wrong number of bytes moves `st{i}.byteLength()` and the two sides part.
	//
	// ⭐ AND THE `==` IS FALSE BY CONSTRUCTION, so its arm is the witness. `sv{i}` had `st{i}` appended to
	// it one line above and `st{i}` is never empty, so a `__str_eq` answering true is a defect — and the
	// arm says so by adding the failure offset rather than the `+ 1` it used to.
	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		let literal = "scale string payload segment number {i} long enough to reach the heap"
		var body = StringBuilder.create()
		body.append("\t\tvar sv{i} = \"{literal}\"\n")
		body.append("\t\tlet st{i} = \" tail \{a\}\"\n")
		body.append("\t\tsv{i}.append(st{i})\n")
		body.append("\t\tacc = acc + sv{i}.byteLength() - st{i}.byteLength(){neutralizeText(literal.byteLength())}\n")
		body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		body.append(falsePredicateProbe("sv{i} == \"{literal}\"", label: "se{i}"))
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("sg{i}", body: body.build()))
	end 'eachGroup'

	out.append(accWitnessTail(StringOpsDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'stringOpsSource'

// ===========================================================================================
// THE REST OF `String`'S MEMBER SURFACE — four knobs, one per `dispatchStringMethod` family
// ===========================================================================================
//
// See `StringSearchOpsBase` for why there are four of these and not one. Three rules hold across all
// four, and each is a way this family could have generated code that measured nothing:
//
//   • EVERY PAYLOAD IS BUILT FROM PIECES THIS GENERATOR JOINS, and every expectation is stated from
//     those pieces. Nothing here measures a joined result to discover what it should be — that would
//     make the witness agree with whatever the compiler did, which is the one thing a witness may not
//     do. (The single exception is `byteLength()` of an escape-free literal, which `stringOpsSource`
//     already relies on and which is arithmetic on the pieces by another name.)
//   • EVERY MEMBER'S RESULT REACHES `acc`, either as an integer fold or through an `if` whose arm
//     contributes. A member whose result went nowhere is dead code a future DCE pass may legitimately
//     delete, taking its dimension with it — the property `RungDriver` exists to defend.
//   • EVERY PREDICATE IS GENERATED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS where a direction exists. A `contains` that always
//     answered true and one that answered correctly are indistinguishable from the true arm alone, so
//     the false arm is emitted with `CorpusWitnessFailedOffset` in it and the rung's exit code is the
//     difference.

// ⭐ SLICE C's payload, in the pieces every witness below is stated from: `<head> segment <i>` followed by
// `<needle> <gap>` repeated, then `<tail>`. Escape-free by construction, so this generator's
// `byteLength()` of a joined payload IS the generated program's — which is not true one family down; see
// `TrimWhitespaceLead`.
let SearchHeadWord = "head"
let SearchTailWord = "tail"
let SearchGapText = " gap "
let SearchNeedle = "MID"
let SearchReplacement = "MIDDLE"

// A needle no payload contains, so its predicate is FALSE by construction and its search THROWS.
let SearchAbsentNeedle = "zzq"

// ⭐ **THE TWO CASE PROBES, AND WHY THEY ARE NEEDLES RATHER THAN LENGTHS.** An ASCII case conversion does
// not change a string's length, so `toLower().byteLength()` is witnessed identically by a conversion that
// did its job and by one that handed the receiver straight back. These two are the difference: the payload
// holds `MID` in upper case and `head` in lower, so `mid` appears ONLY after a `toLower` and `HEAD` ONLY
// after a `toUpper` — and each is asked of the receiver first, where it must be absent.
let SearchLowerProbe = "mid"
let SearchUpperProbe = "HEAD"

// How many needles a payload carries — the count `replace` rewrites, `split` cuts at, and `findLast`
// walks past. It is the LOOP BOUND `searchPayloadText` writes them with rather than a fact stated beside
// it, so the number and the text cannot drift apart.
let SearchNeedleOccurrences = 3 as KnobIndex

// The piece before the FIRST needle — `split`'s element 0, and therefore that element's own expectation.
function searchPayloadFirstPart(index KnobIndex) returns String
	return "{SearchHeadWord} segment {index} "
end 'searchPayloadFirstPart'

function searchPayloadText(index KnobIndex) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append(searchPayloadFirstPart(index))
	for _ in 0 upto SearchNeedleOccurrences 'eachNeedle'
		out.append(SearchNeedle)
		out.append(SearchGapText)
	end 'eachNeedle'
	out.append(SearchTailWord)
	return out.build()
end 'searchPayloadText'

// The `stringSearchOps` knob: M groups of the BYTE/ASCII family, in two blocks per group.
//
// TWO BLOCKS, because they are two halves of the family and a change may move one without the other: the
// PREDICATES and case conversions read the receiver and answer a bool or a same-length String, while the
// REWRITES build a new String (or an Array of them) whose size the receiver's does not determine. The
// first half is `bytesEqual` and a byte-range map; the second is `findIn` plus a `StringBuilder` that is
// told its answer in advance.
function stringSearchOpsSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the string-search knob — {groupCount} groups of startsWith/endsWith/contains + toLower/toUpper, then replace/replaceFirst/split.\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(StringSearchOpsDriver))

	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		let payload = searchPayloadText(i)
		let payloadBytes = payload.byteLength()

		var predicates = StringBuilder.create()
		predicates.append("\t\tlet qv{i} = \"{payload}\"\n")
		predicates.append(truePredicateProbe("qv{i}.startsWith(\"{SearchHeadWord}\")", label: "qsw{i}"))
		predicates.append(truePredicateProbe("qv{i}.endsWith(\"{SearchTailWord}\")", label: "qew{i}"))
		predicates.append(truePredicateProbe("qv{i}.contains(\"{SearchNeedle}\")", label: "qct{i}"))
		// ⭐ THE THREE PREDICATES' FALSE DIRECTION, AND THE FIRST TWO ARE ASKED WITH EACH OTHER'S WORD ON
		// PURPOSE. `head` really is in the payload and `tail` really is in the payload — just not at the end
		// the question names — so these two catch a `startsWith`/`endsWith` that searched ANYWHERE, which an
		// absent needle could never do: it would answer false for a wrong reason and read as a pass.
		// `contains` has no position to get wrong, so it is the one that takes the genuinely absent needle.
		predicates.append(falsePredicateProbe("qv{i}.startsWith(\"{SearchTailWord}\")", label: "qsf{i}"))
		predicates.append(falsePredicateProbe("qv{i}.endsWith(\"{SearchHeadWord}\")", label: "qef{i}"))
		predicates.append(falsePredicateProbe("qv{i}.contains(\"{SearchAbsentNeedle}\")", label: "qab{i}"))
		// The case probes' "before" half — see `SearchLowerProbe`.
		predicates.append(falsePredicateProbe("qv{i}.contains(\"{SearchLowerProbe}\")", label: "qlb{i}"))
		predicates.append(falsePredicateProbe("qv{i}.contains(\"{SearchUpperProbe}\")", label: "qub{i}"))
		predicates.append(truePredicateProbe("qv{i}.toLower().contains(\"{SearchLowerProbe}\")", label: "qlo{i}"))
		predicates.append(truePredicateProbe("qv{i}.toUpper().contains(\"{SearchUpperProbe}\")", label: "qup{i}"))
		// ...and the conversions' LENGTHS, which the probes above cannot pin: an ASCII case map rewrites
		// bytes in place, so a result that lost or gained one lands here and nowhere else.
		predicates.append("\t\tacc = acc + qv{i}.toLower().byteLength() + qv{i}.toUpper().byteLength(){neutralizeText(2 * payloadBytes)}\n")
		predicates.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("qg{i}", body: predicates.build()))

		var rewrites = StringBuilder.create()
		rewrites.append("\t\tlet rv{i} = \"{payload}\"\n")
		// The replacement is LONGER than the needle on purpose. Equal widths would witness a `replace` that
		// found every match and one that found none identically, since both answer at the receiver's length.
		rewrites.append("\t\tacc = acc + rv{i}.replace(\"{SearchNeedle}\", with: \"{SearchReplacement}\").byteLength(){neutralizeText(payloadBytes + SearchNeedleOccurrences * searchReplacementDelta())}\n")
		rewrites.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		rewrites.append("\t\tacc = acc + rv{i}.replaceFirst(\"{SearchNeedle}\", with: \"{SearchReplacement}\").byteLength(){neutralizeText(payloadBytes + searchReplacementDelta())}\n")
		rewrites.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		// `split` at the same needle: N cuts make N+1 pieces, and element 0 is the head this generator
		// wrote. The COUNT alone would be witnessed identically by a split that handed back N+1 empty
		// strings, which is why the element's length is folded beside it — and reading a MANAGED element
		// out of an Array owes a retain, so the read is load-bearing twice over.
		rewrites.append("\t\tlet rp{i} = rv{i}.split(\"{SearchNeedle}\")\n")
		rewrites.append("\t\tacc = acc + rp{i}.count() + (try rp{i}.get(0) otherwise \"\").byteLength(){neutralizeText(SearchNeedleOccurrences + 1 + searchPayloadFirstPart(i).byteLength())}\n")
		rewrites.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("rg{i}", body: rewrites.build()))
	end 'eachGroup'

	out.append(accWitnessTail(StringSearchOpsDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'stringSearchOpsSource'

// How many bytes one `replace` of `SearchNeedle` by `SearchReplacement` adds. Derived from the two
// spellings rather than written down, so a longer needle cannot leave a stale delta behind.
function searchReplacementDelta() returns TrackedValue
	return SearchReplacement.byteLength() - SearchNeedle.byteLength()
end 'searchReplacementDelta'

// ⭐ SLICE B2's payload: `<head><needle><middle><needle><tail>`, ALL-ASCII on purpose. A grapheme index and
// a byte position coincide over ASCII, which is what lets every expectation below be a byte arithmetic
// this generator can state — and the family that needs them to DIVERGE is the materializer knob, which
// carries the non-ASCII payload for exactly that reason.
let IndexHeadPrefix = "index payload "
let IndexNeedle = "FIND"
let IndexMiddleText = " middle "
let IndexTailText = " tail"

// How many clusters the `length:` overload of `slice` asks for. Small enough to sit inside the head piece
// at every rung, so the answer is the count itself rather than a clamp at the end of the string.
let IndexSliceClusters = 5

function indexPayloadHead(index KnobIndex) returns String
	return "{IndexHeadPrefix}{index} "
end 'indexPayloadHead'

function indexPayloadText(index KnobIndex) returns String
	return "{indexPayloadHead(index)}{IndexNeedle}{IndexMiddleText}{IndexNeedle}{IndexTailText}"
end 'indexPayloadText'

// The `stringIndexOps` knob: M groups of the `StringIndex` family, in three blocks per group.
//
// THREE BLOCKS, and the split is by what the compiler must do rather than by member count: the two ENDS
// are pure construction (`startIndex` is two literals and no call at all), the two SEARCHES and
// `indexAfter` are THROWING runtime calls that each fork the CFG under their `try`, and `slice` is the
// only CONSUMER of an index and the only member with two overloads told apart by an argument LABEL.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY THROWING MEMBER IS GENERATED ON BOTH EDGES.** A `try` whose error edge is never taken emits
// the fork and never runs it, so a wrong lowering of the failure path costs nothing and witnesses nothing.
// `SearchAbsentNeedle` is what takes it: the payload cannot contain it, so that search throws at every
// site and the `otherwise` value is the one this generator predicts.
function stringIndexOpsSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the string-index knob — {groupCount} groups of startIndex/endIndex + findFirst/findLast/indexAfter (both edges) + both slice overloads.\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(StringIndexOpsDriver))

	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		let payload = indexPayloadText(i)
		let payloadBytes = payload.byteLength()
		let firstNeedleAt = indexPayloadHead(i).byteLength()
		let lastNeedleAt = firstNeedleAt + IndexNeedle.byteLength() + IndexMiddleText.byteLength()

		// THE TWO ENDS, and both accessors on each. `startIndex()` is zero in both columns, so it is the
		// end whose expectation is a constant and the one a lowering could not accidentally satisfy from
		// the receiver; `endIndex()` answers the grapheme count and the byte length, equal over ASCII.
		var ends = StringBuilder.create()
		ends.append("\t\tlet xv{i} = \"{payload}\"\n")
		ends.append("\t\tlet xs{i} = xv{i}.startIndex()\n")
		ends.append("\t\tlet xe{i} = xv{i}.endIndex()\n")
		ends.append("\t\tacc = acc + xs{i}.charIndex() + xs{i}.bytePos() + xe{i}.charIndex() + xe{i}.bytePos(){neutralizeText(2 * payloadBytes)}\n")
		ends.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("xg{i}", body: ends.build()))

		// THE THREE THROWING MEMBERS. `findFirst` and `findLast` land on the two needles this generator
		// placed, and reading a DIFFERENT accessor off each (`bytePos` from one, `charIndex` from the
		// other) is what stops a search that answered with its twin's offset from cancelling.
		var searches = StringBuilder.create()
		searches.append("\t\tlet yv{i} = \"{payload}\"\n")
		searches.append("\t\tlet yf{i} = try yv{i}.findFirst(\"{IndexNeedle}\") otherwise yv{i}.endIndex()\n")
		searches.append("\t\tlet yl{i} = try yv{i}.findLast(\"{IndexNeedle}\") otherwise yv{i}.startIndex()\n")
		searches.append("\t\tacc = acc + yf{i}.bytePos() + yl{i}.charIndex(){neutralizeText(firstNeedleAt + lastNeedleAt)}\n")
		searches.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		// The ERROR EDGE, taken at every site — see this function's header.
		searches.append("\t\tlet ym{i} = try yv{i}.findFirst(\"{SearchAbsentNeedle}\") otherwise yv{i}.endIndex()\n")
		searches.append("\t\tacc = acc + ym{i}.bytePos(){neutralizeText(payloadBytes)}\n")
		searches.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		// `indexAfter` off the START, which is the one position whose answer needs no search: the payload's
		// first cluster is one ASCII byte, so the step is exactly one in both columns.
		searches.append("\t\tlet yn{i} = try yv{i}.indexAfter(yv{i}.startIndex()) otherwise yv{i}.endIndex()\n")
		searches.append("\t\tacc = acc + yn{i}.charIndex() + yn{i}.bytePos() - 2\n")
		searches.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("yg{i}", body: searches.build()))

		// BOTH `slice` OVERLOADS. They are told apart by the argument LABEL and not by the argument's type,
		// so a corpus with one of them measures neither the resolution nor the second body.
		var slices = StringBuilder.create()
		slices.append("\t\tlet zv{i} = \"{payload}\"\n")
		slices.append("\t\tlet zs{i} = zv{i}.startIndex()\n")
		slices.append("\t\tlet zf{i} = try zv{i}.findFirst(\"{IndexNeedle}\") otherwise zv{i}.endIndex()\n")
		slices.append("\t\tacc = acc + zv{i}.slice(zs{i}, endIndex: zf{i}).byteLength(){neutralizeText(firstNeedleAt)}\n")
		slices.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		slices.append("\t\tacc = acc + zv{i}.slice(zs{i}, length: {IndexSliceClusters}).byteLength(){neutralizeText(IndexSliceClusters)}\n")
		slices.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("zg{i}", body: slices.build()))
	end 'eachGroup'

	out.append(accWitnessTail(StringIndexOpsDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'stringIndexOpsSource'

// ⭐⭐ **SLICE D's WHITESPACE PADDING — AND THE ONE PLACE IN THIS FILE WHERE `byteLength()` OF A PAYLOAD
// PIECE IS THE WRONG NUMBER.** These are SOURCE SPELLINGS: `\t` is two characters to this generator and
// ONE byte to the program it writes. So the emitted-byte count is stated beside each spelling rather than
// measured off it — deriving it the way every other payload here safely does would make every trim
// expectation three bytes too long and fail every rung.
//
// They are escapes rather than plain spaces deliberately: nothing else in this corpus emits a string
// ESCAPE at all, so the generated program's lexer never decoded one until this knob.
let TrimWhitespaceLead = "  \\t"
let TrimWhitespaceLeadBytes = 3
let TrimWhitespaceTrail = "\\n  "
let TrimWhitespaceTrailBytes = 3

// The DIGIT padding the explicit-`CharacterSet` form cuts, which needs no escape and so is measured.
let TrimDigitPad = "123"

// ⭐ **THE CORE OPENS AND CLOSES ON A MULTI-BYTE CLUSTER, WHICH IS WHERE A TRIM ACTUALLY WORKS.** Both
// trims stop at the FIRST KEPT cluster — the property that keeps a no-op trim from costing a full walk,
// stated by the synthesized `__str_trim`'s `stopsAtFirstKept` until W49 wave 4 and owned by
// `stdlib/String.maxon`'s trims ever since (the runtime file that held it went at W129) — so the only
// cluster either one has to CLASSIFY rather
// than skip is the one at the boundary. An all-ASCII core would settle that decision on a single byte and
// never decode a sequence.
let TrimCoreLead = "é"
let TrimCoreTrail = "😀"

function trimCoreText(index KnobIndex) returns String
	return "{TrimCoreLead}trim payload {index} body{TrimCoreTrail}"
end 'trimCoreText'

// The three members' spellings. They differ only in which end they cut, which is why they are a rotation
// over one emitter rather than three near-copies of it.
let TrimBothMethod = "trim"
let TrimStartMethod = "trimStart"
let TrimEndMethod = "trimEnd"

// The `CharacterSet` an EXPLICIT trim names. `decimalDigits()` and not `whitespacesAndNewlines()`, and the
// choice is measured: see `StringTrimOpsBase`.
let TrimExplicitCharacterSet = "CharacterSet.decimalDigits()"

// ⭐ **ONE PADDING SCHEME — what a trim CUTS, what it LEAVES, and how the call names the set.** It is a
// record rather than six parameters because a trim site needs all of it at once and `requireAbiParamCount`
// caps a signature at six slots; it is ONE record rather than two because the two schemes differ in their
// VALUES and not in their shape, which is the whole reason one emitter can serve both forms.
type TrimPadding
	// The padding's SOURCE SPELLING, and what it is worth in the generated program's BYTES. The two are
	// not the same number for the whitespace scheme — see `TrimWhitespaceLead`.
	export let lead as String
	export let leadBytes as TrackedValue
	export let trail as String
	export let trailBytes as TrackedValue

	// The argument text the call presents, EMPTY for the default form. The default is not a third member:
	// it is the same three calls with the argument list left empty, which is exactly the distinction this
	// field carries.
	export let argument as String

	// The prefix every block label and binding under this scheme is minted from, so the two schemes cannot
	// collide in one driver.
	export let tag as String

	export static function create(lead String, leadBytes TrackedValue, trail String, trailBytes TrackedValue, argument String, tag String) returns TrimPadding
		return Self{lead: lead, leadBytes: leadBytes, trail: trail, trailBytes: trailBytes, argument: argument, tag: tag}
	end 'create'
end 'TrimPadding'

function trimWhitespacePadding() returns TrimPadding
	return TrimPadding.create(TrimWhitespaceLead, leadBytes: TrimWhitespaceLeadBytes, trail: TrimWhitespaceTrail, trailBytes: TrimWhitespaceTrailBytes, argument: "", tag: "t")
end 'trimWhitespacePadding'

function trimDigitPadding() returns TrimPadding
	return TrimPadding.create(TrimDigitPad, leadBytes: TrimDigitPad.byteLength(), trail: TrimDigitPad, trailBytes: TrimDigitPad.byteLength(), argument: TrimExplicitCharacterSet, tag: "u")
end 'trimDigitPadding'

// ONE trim SITE, in ONE `ownedSiteBlock`. See `StringTrimOpsBase` for why a site gets a block to itself
// here where every other knob in this file puts a whole group in one.
function trimSiteBlock(label String, binding String, payload String, member String, padding TrimPadding, expectedBytes TrackedValue) returns String
	var body = StringBuilder.create()
	body.append("\t\tlet {binding} = \"{payload}\"\n")
	body.append("\t\tacc = acc + {binding}.{member}({padding.argument}).byteLength(){neutralizeText(expectedBytes)}\n")
	body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
	return ownedSiteBlock(label, body: body.build())
end 'trimSiteBlock'

// The three trims under ONE padding scheme. Each is witnessed by what it LEFT — the core for the two-ended
// form, and the core plus the padding at the end it did not touch for each one-ended form — so a trim that
// cut the wrong end lands on the other one-ended form's expectation and the two do not cancel, because
// they are folded in separate blocks against the same pinned accumulator.
function trimSchemeBlocks(index KnobIndex, padding TrimPadding, core String) returns String
	let coreBytes = core.byteLength()
	let payload = "{padding.lead}{core}{padding.trail}"
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append(trimSiteBlock("{padding.tag}b{index}", binding: "{padding.tag}vb{index}", payload: payload, member: TrimBothMethod, padding: padding, expectedBytes: coreBytes))
	out.append(trimSiteBlock("{padding.tag}s{index}", binding: "{padding.tag}vs{index}", payload: payload, member: TrimStartMethod, padding: padding, expectedBytes: coreBytes + padding.trailBytes))
	out.append(trimSiteBlock("{padding.tag}e{index}", binding: "{padding.tag}ve{index}", payload: payload, member: TrimEndMethod, padding: padding, expectedBytes: padding.leadBytes + coreBytes))
	return out.build()
end 'trimSchemeBlocks'

// The `stringTrimOps` knob: M groups of the three trims, over BOTH argument forms.
//
// The two schemes are the two argument FORMS rather than two of the members: with no argument the call
// site builds the default `CharacterSet.whitespacesAndNewlines()`, and with one it does not. That is a
// difference in what is EMITTED — and, measured, by far the largest per-site difference in this family.
function stringTrimOpsSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the string-trim knob — {groupCount} groups of trim/trimStart/trimEnd, in both the default-CharacterSet and explicit-CharacterSet forms, one site per block.\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(StringTrimOpsDriver))

	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		let core = trimCoreText(i)
		out.append(trimSchemeBlocks(i, padding: trimWhitespacePadding(), core: core))
		out.append(trimSchemeBlocks(i, padding: trimDigitPadding(), core: core))
	end 'eachGroup'

	out.append(accWitnessTail(StringTrimOpsDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'stringTrimOpsSource'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE NON-ASCII PAYLOAD IN THE CORPUS, AND EVERY DIGIT OF IT IS LOAD-BEARING.** Over ASCII text
// a String's grapheme count, codepoint count, UTF-16 length and byte length are ALL THE SAME NUMBER, so an
// ASCII corpus cannot tell the four materializers apart: `count()` answering with `bytes().count()`'s walk
// would witness perfectly. These three characters make all four differ at once —
//
//   `é`        2 bytes, 1 grapheme, 1 codepoint,  1 UTF-16 unit   (a two-byte sequence)
//   `😀`       4 bytes, 1 grapheme, 1 codepoint,  2 UTF-16 units  (a surrogate PAIR)
//   `👨‍👩‍👧`  18 bytes, 1 grapheme, 5 codepoints, 8 UTF-16 units  (a ZWJ sequence: three emoji, two joiners)
//
// — 24, 3, 7 and 11, four distinct numbers, and the ZWJ sequence is what separates the GRAPHEME count from
// the CODEPOINT count, which the first two alone cannot do.
//
// ⚠ THEY ARE MEASURED FACTS ABOUT UTF-8 AND UAX #29, NOT ABOUT THIS COMPILER, which is why they are
// written down rather than computed: asking this generator's own `count()` for them would state the
// expectation from the very implementation the emitted program is meant to check, and the two would agree
// however wrong they were.
let ViewSegmentText = "é😀👨‍👩‍👧"
let ViewSegmentBytes = 24
let ViewSegmentGraphemes = 3
let ViewSegmentCodepoints = 7
let ViewSegmentUtf16Units = 11

// What a `clone` is grown by after it is taken, so that a clone which ALIASED its source is caught. Escape
// -free, so its length is measured.
let ViewCloneTail = "TAIL"

function viewPayloadText(index KnobIndex) returns String
	return "view {index} {ViewSegmentText} tail"
end 'viewPayloadText'

// The `stringViewOps` knob: M groups of the materializers, plus `isEmpty` and `clone`.
//
// TWO BLOCKS: the four COUNTS, which each walk the receiver's bytes under a different rule, and the two
// LEFTOVERS, which do not walk at all — `isEmpty()` is a field load and a compare, and `clone()` is a
// record copy. Folding them into one block would price a field load beside four full walks.
function stringViewOpsSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	// The CHECK between this file's bytes and the roster above it. `ViewSegmentBytes` is the one of the
	// four constants that is cheap to verify here and the one that a re-encoding of this source file would
	// move — a normalizer decomposing `é` into `e` + U+0301 changes all four at once, and this is where a
	// reader would be told rather than left with a ladder that fails at every rung for no stated reason.
	if ViewSegmentText.byteLength() != ViewSegmentBytes 'segmentDrifted'
		panic("ScaleCorpus.stringViewOpsSource: ViewSegmentText is {ViewSegmentText.byteLength()} bytes but ViewSegmentBytes says {ViewSegmentBytes} — this source file's encoding has changed under the roster, and the grapheme / codepoint / UTF-16 counts beside it are wrong too. Re-measure all four against the new bytes.")
	end 'segmentDrifted'

	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the string-view knob — {groupCount} groups of count/bytes/toByteArray/codepoints/utf16, then isEmpty and clone.\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(StringViewOpsDriver))

	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		let payload = viewPayloadText(i)
		let payloadBytes = payload.byteLength()
		// The payload is the segment with ASCII on both sides, and every count is additive over it: an
		// ASCII byte is one byte, one grapheme, one codepoint and one UTF-16 unit alike.
		let asciiBytes = payloadBytes - ViewSegmentBytes

		var counts = StringBuilder.create()
		counts.append("\t\tlet vv{i} = \"{payload}\"\n")
		// Each of the four is folded in its OWN line with its OWN expectation. Summing them would let two
		// materializers answering each other's number cancel, which is precisely the confusion this
		// payload was built to detect.
		counts.append("\t\tacc = acc + vv{i}.count(){neutralizeText(asciiBytes + ViewSegmentGraphemes)}\n")
		counts.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		counts.append("\t\tacc = acc + vv{i}.codepoints().count(){neutralizeText(asciiBytes + ViewSegmentCodepoints)}\n")
		counts.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		counts.append("\t\tacc = acc + vv{i}.utf16().count(){neutralizeText(asciiBytes + ViewSegmentUtf16Units)}\n")
		counts.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		// `bytes()` and `toByteArray()` are ONE emitter in shv2 and two members in the reference, so both
		// spellings are written: the day they stop being one answer, the day they part is here.
		counts.append("\t\tacc = acc + vv{i}.bytes().count() + vv{i}.toByteArray().count(){neutralizeText(2 * payloadBytes)}\n")
		counts.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("vg{i}", body: counts.build()))

		var leftovers = StringBuilder.create()
		leftovers.append("\t\tlet wv{i} = \"{payload}\"\n")
		leftovers.append(falsePredicateProbe("wv{i}.isEmpty()", label: "wne{i}"))
		leftovers.append("\t\tlet we{i} = \"\"\n")
		leftovers.append(truePredicateProbe("we{i}.isEmpty()", label: "wie{i}"))
		// ⭐ THE CLONE IS WITNESSED BY ITS INDEPENDENCE, NOT BY ITS LENGTH. A `clone()` that handed back the
		// receiver would answer the right byte count all day; growing the copy and then asking BOTH is what
		// tells the two apart, because an alias would have grown the source with it and the difference
		// would read zero.
		leftovers.append("\t\tvar wc{i} = wv{i}.clone()\n")
		leftovers.append("\t\twc{i}.append(\"{ViewCloneTail}\")\n")
		leftovers.append("\t\tacc = acc + wc{i}.byteLength() - wv{i}.byteLength(){neutralizeText(ViewCloneTail.byteLength())}\n")
		leftovers.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("wg{i}", body: leftovers.build()))
	end 'eachGroup'

	out.append(accWitnessTail(StringViewOpsDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'stringViewOpsSource'

// ⭐ **EVERYTHING THE GENERATOR MUST KNOW ABOUT ONE CHARACTER TO WITNESS THE SITES IT WRITES FOR IT.**
// Every field is a fact this file can only state because it chose the character: a site whose answer the
// generator cannot predict cannot be neutralized, and an un-neutralized site is one the accumulator
// cannot be pinned across — which is how `ownedSiteBlock`'s header records seven knobs silently not
// running at all.
type CharSample
	// The literal's spelling, written between the quotes of the generated source.
	export let text as String

	// Its Unicode codepoint — what a character literal in an INTEGER position becomes
	// (`Parser.retypeCharLiteralAsCodepoint`), and therefore what the coercion site contributes.
	export let codepoint as TrackedValue

	// A character strictly BELOW `text` in byte-lexicographic order, so the `>` site is true by
	// construction and a `__char_cmp` that answered otherwise is short by the mark.
	export let below as String

	// The inclusive `match` interval that CONTAINS `text`. A range arm that a wrong lowering widened or
	// narrowed sends the scrutinee to the `default`, which is this knob's failure marker.
	export let rangeLow as String
	export let rangeHigh as String

	export static function create(text String, codepoint TrackedValue, below String, rangeLow String, rangeHigh String) returns CharSample
		return Self{text: text, codepoint: codepoint, below: below, rangeLow: rangeLow, rangeHigh: rangeHigh}
	end 'create'
end 'CharSample'

// How many distinct characters the knob rotates over. CONSTANT on purpose, for `AllocStructTypeCount`'s
// reason: this knob grows how many character SITES a program holds, not how many distinct characters
// exist. A corpus with one character per site would be measuring the interner instead.
let CharSampleCount = 4 as KnobIndex

// ⭐ **THE ROSTER SPANS BOTH RECORD WIDTHS, AND THAT IS THE ONE DISTINCTION THE OLD WIDTH RULE TURNED
// ON.** Until BATCH23 a one-byte literal was an `int` and a wider one was a `Character`, so a roster of
// ASCII would exercise exactly the half that USED to be trivial and none of the half that always was a
// record. `é` is `C3 A9` and `ü` is `C3 BC`: two bytes each, sharing a first byte, so the interval below
// tests the byte loop past its first step rather than settling on it.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY ORDERING FACT HERE IS BYTE-LEXICOGRAPHIC, WHICH IS WHAT `__char_cmp` IMPLEMENTS** — never
// codepoint order and never locale order. `'é' > 'z'` because `C3 > 7A`, not because é sorts later in any
// alphabet.
// ⛔ **IT TAKES THE ORDINAL ALREADY REDUCED, AND THE `mod` MUST STAY AT THE CALL SITE — A BOOTSTRAP
// MISCOMPILE, MEASURED 2026-08-05.** Written the obvious way — `match index mod CharSampleCount` with the
// wrap inside — this function returns the DEFAULT arm for every non-zero ordinal when built by the C#
// bootstrap, so `charOpsSource` panicked at its second group. Minimal repro, bootstrap only:
//
//     function byMod(i Ix) returns Val
//         match i mod Three 'a'
//             0 then return 10
//             1 then return 11
//             default then return 90
//         end 'a'
//     end 'byMod'
//
// `byMod(1)` answers 90. The VALUE is right — `print("{i mod Three}")` beside it reads 1 — and an
// `if m == 1` chain over the same expression is right too; only the `match` dispatch is wrong, and only
// when the dividend traces to a PARAMETER (`matchSitesSource`'s `match i mod MatchDispatchShapeCount` over
// a LOOP variable is correct, which is why no existing knob was affected). **shv2 compiles all three
// forms correctly**, so this is a bootstrap defect found from shv2, not a language question. Matching a
// BARE parameter is the shape that works, and it is the shape below.
function charSampleAt(ordinal KnobIndex) returns CharSample
	match ordinal 'sample'
		0 then return CharSample.create("a", codepoint: 97, below: "A", rangeLow: "a", rangeHigh: "z")
		1 then return CharSample.create("Q", codepoint: 81, below: "0", rangeLow: "A", rangeHigh: "Z")
		2 then return CharSample.create("7", codepoint: 55, below: "!", rangeLow: "0", rangeHigh: "9")
		3 then return CharSample.create("é", codepoint: 233, below: "z", rangeLow: "a", rangeHigh: "ü")
		// `CharSampleCount` and this arm set are the same fact stated twice; this is the CHECK between the
		// copies, exactly as `matchSitesSource`'s shape dispatch keeps its own.
		default panic("charSampleAt: CharSampleCount is {CharSampleCount} but ordinal {ordinal} has no sample — add one beside the four above, or lower the constant")
	end 'sample'
end 'charSampleAt'

// The character RANGE both `for` loops walk, and the number of trips it makes. `to` is INCLUSIVE, so
// `'a' to 'e'` is five characters — the closed form the loops' neutralizers are written from, stated once
// beside the bounds it is derived from so a widened range cannot leave a stale trip count behind.
let CharRangeLow = "a"
let CharRangeHigh = "e"
let CharRangeTrips = 5 as TrackedValue

// The one character inside that range the USED-element loop tests for, so exactly one trip of five hits.
let CharRangeHit = "c"

// The `charOps` knob: M `Character` operation groups, each in its own block for `ownedSiteBlock`'s reason.
//
// The five shapes are the five distinct things BATCH23 gave a character literal to do, and each reaches a
// different piece of the compiler:
//
//   • a `let` BINDING off an immortal `.rdata` byte record, then `==` against the same literal — the
//     `__str_eq` content compare a `Character` equality now lowers to, where a one-byte literal used to be
//     an immediate integer compare. This is the shape the rung's implementer flagged as a cost and
//     deliberately did not act on; it is measurable here for the first time.
//   • `>` against a known-lower character — `__char_cmp` plus `emitOrderingVerdict`'s `Ordering` test,
//     the ONE operation where `Character` and `String` part company.
//   • the literal in an INTEGER position (`acc + 'a'`), which is `integerizedOperand` finding a lone
//     `charLiteral` token and `retypeCharLiteralAsCodepoint` REWRITING the emitted `stringLiteral` op in
//     place. It is also the only site that fills `Parser.charLiteralOps`, the map BATCH23 added — so this
//     knob is what makes that map's growth a column rather than a claim.
//   • a `match` with a character INTERVAL arm — the linear compare chain a Character scrutinee keeps
//     (an integer-like scrutinee would get a binary search or a jump table, which the enum knob owns).
//   • two `for` loops over a character RANGE, one DISCARDING the element and one USING it. Each trip mints
//     a fresh owned `Character` through `__char_from_cp`, and the discarding form mints one it never reads
//     — the second cost the implementer flagged. The rung binary is RUN and its exit insisted on, so a
//     record minted per trip and not freed is exit 101 here rather than a number nobody explains.
//
// ⚠ **THE `for` LOOPS ARE THIS CORPUS'S FIRST `for`-in OF ANY KIND**, which is why the manifest's NOT
// GENERATED line narrowed rather than lost an entry: a character range is now generated and every other
// `for`-in source — an array, a String's clusters, a bare integer range — still is not.
function charOpsSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the character-ops knob — {groupCount} groups of Character binding + == + ordering + codepoint coercion + a match interval + two character-range for loops.\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(CharOpsDriver))

	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		// The wrap is spelled HERE and not inside the callee — see `charSampleAt`'s header for the
		// bootstrap miscompile that makes the position load-bearing.
		let sample = charSampleAt(i mod CharSampleCount)
		var body = StringBuilder.create()

		// EQUALITY. The same literal on both sides, so `__str_eq` answering false is the failure — and it
		// is the BINDING-versus-literal shape a real program writes, not two literals a constant folder
		// could in principle settle.
		body.append("\t\tlet cv{i} = '{sample.text}'\n")
		body.append(truePredicateProbe("cv{i} == '{sample.text}'", label: "ceq{i}"))

		// ORDERING through `__char_cmp`.
		body.append(truePredicateProbe("cv{i} > '{sample.below}'", label: "cgt{i}"))

		// THE CODEPOINT COERCION. `acc` is the opaque accumulator, so the add survives `foldConstOperands`
		// and the literal genuinely has to become an integer operand for it to compile at all.
		body.append("\t\tacc = acc + '{sample.text}'{neutralizeText(sample.codepoint)}\n")
		body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))

		// THE `match` INTERVAL. The `default` arm is the failure marker rather than a silent fallthrough:
		// the interval was chosen to contain the scrutinee, so reaching the default means the lowering
		// disagrees with the generator about what the arm covers, and the witness says so.
		body.append("\t\tmatch cv{i} 'cms{i}'\n")
		body.append("\t\t\t'{sample.rangeLow}' to '{sample.rangeHigh}' then acc = acc + {SiteEnteredMark}\n")
		body.append("\t\t\tdefault then acc = acc + {CorpusWitnessFailedOffset}\n")
		body.append("\t\tend 'cms{i}'\n")
		body.append("\t\tacc = acc - {SiteEnteredMark}\n")
		body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))

		// THE DISCARDED-ELEMENT RANGE LOOP — a Character minted per trip that nothing reads.
		body.append("\t\tfor _ in '{CharRangeLow}' to '{CharRangeHigh}' 'cdr{i}'\n")
		body.append("\t\t\tacc = acc + {SiteEnteredMark}\n")
		body.append("\t\tend 'cdr{i}'\n")
		body.append("\t\tacc = acc - {CharRangeTrips * SiteEnteredMark}\n")

		// THE USED-ELEMENT RANGE LOOP — the same mint, compared against a literal, so exactly one trip of
		// `CharRangeTrips` contributes and a loop that walked the wrong characters cannot come out level.
		body.append("\t\tvar cn{i} = 0\n")
		body.append("\t\tfor cc{i} in '{CharRangeLow}' to '{CharRangeHigh}' 'cur{i}'\n")
		body.append("\t\t\tif cc{i} == '{CharRangeHit}' 'cch{i}'\n")
		body.append("\t\t\t\tcn{i} = cn{i} + {SiteEnteredMark}\n")
		body.append("\t\t\tend 'cch{i}'\n")
		body.append("\t\tend 'cur{i}'\n")
		body.append("\t\tacc = acc + cn{i} - {SiteEnteredMark}\n")
		body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))

		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("cg{i}", body: body.build()))
	end 'eachGroup'

	out.append(accWitnessTail(CharOpsDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'charOpsSource'

// The `managedStructTypes` knob: N types each owning a `String` field, each built once and dropped.
//
// The per-TYPE cost is the point — one SYNTHESIZED `__destruct_<T>` body per type, rooted and installed —
// so what doubles is how many distinct cascades exist, not how hard any one of them is used. Each is
// built once anyway, for `structTypesSource`'s reason: a type nothing builds is a type a future pass may
// legitimately never lay out, and the dimension would evaporate silently.
function managedStructTypesSource(typeCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the managed-struct-types knob — {typeCount} types owning a String field, each built once and dropped through its cascade.\n")
	out.append("typealias {ManagedStructValueAlias} = int(i64.min to i64.max)\n")

	for t in 0 upto typeCount 'eachType'
		out.append(managedFieldTypeDecl("{ManagedStructPrefix}{t}", managedFieldName: ManagedLabelFieldName, managedTypeName: StringTypeSpelling, valueTypeName: ManagedStructValueAlias))
	end 'eachType'

	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(ManagedStructsDriver))
	// ⭐ THIS `==` IS THE OTHER WAY ROUND FROM `stringOpsSource`'S: the field holds exactly the literal it
	// was built with, so the arm MUST be taken — which is `truePredicateProbe`, where that one is the
	// FALSE twin. A comparison that answered false skips the mark and leaves the accumulator one short,
	// where an arm quietly contributing zero would not have been a witness at all.
	for t in 0 upto typeCount 'eachUse'
		let literal = "managed struct label number {t} long enough to reach the heap"
		var body = StringBuilder.create()
		body.append("\t\tlet nv{t} = {ManagedStructPrefix}{t}.create(\"{literal}\", value: a)\n")
		body.append("\t\tacc = acc + nv{t}.value{neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue())}\n")
		body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		body.append(truePredicateProbe("nv{t}.label == \"{literal}\"", label: "ml{t}"))
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("mn{t}", body: body.build()))
	end 'eachUse'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(ManagedStructsDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'managedStructTypesSource'

// The `arrayOps` knob: M TRIVIAL-element Array groups. Two blocks per group, and they are two blocks
// rather than one because they measure two different halves of the type:
//
//   • The GROWTH half — `create`/`reserve`/`push`/`get`/`count`. `reserve` is a distinct path from the
//     implicit growth a bare `push` triggers, so both are here.
//   • The SHARING half — `slice`/`clone`/`append`. `slice` and `clone` are O(1): they refcount the
//     element BUFFER rather than the record, which is the one place shv2 diverges from both reference
//     compilers, and `append` is what forces the detach. A corpus with only `push`/`get` measures the
//     growth path and none of this.
//
// Everything flows through `acc`: the pushed values are the opaque accumulator, and `count()`/`get()`
// flow back into it, so no group is dead code.

// How many elements each block pushes, and how wide the slice it then takes is. Named because each is a
// bare literal in TWO programs at once — the `push` lines this file writes, and the count those lines'
// witness is stated against — and because the `slice(0, endIndex: 1)` below must stay inside the pushed
// count or the `otherwise` fallback would silently become the measured path.
let ArrayGrowPushCount = 2
let ArraySharePushCount = 2
let ArraySliceWidth = 1

function arrayOpsSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the array-ops knob — {groupCount} trivial-element Array groups (create/reserve/push/get/count, then slice/clone/append).\n")
	out.append("typealias {ArrayElementAlias} = int(i64.min to i64.max)\n")
	out.append("typealias {IntArrayAlias} = Array with {ArrayElementAlias}\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(ArrayOpsDriver))

	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		var grow = StringBuilder.create()
		grow.append("\t\tvar ab{i} = {IntArrayAlias}.create()\n")
		grow.append("\t\tab{i}.reserve({i mod 4 + 2})\n")
		grow.append("\t\tab{i}.push(a)\n")
		grow.append("\t\tab{i}.push(a + {i mod 5 + 1})\n")
		// Slot 0 holds the accumulator because the first `push` above put it there, and the count is the
		// number of `push`es this loop wrote — so both halves of the growth path are known, and the
		// `otherwise 0` is a fallback a correct `get` never takes.
		grow.append("\t\tacc = acc + (try ab{i}.get(0) otherwise 0) + ab{i}.count(){neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue() + ArrayGrowPushCount)}\n")
		grow.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("ag{i}", body: grow.build()))

		var share = StringBuilder.create()
		share.append("\t\tvar ac{i} = {IntArrayAlias}.create()\n")
		share.append("\t\tac{i}.push(a)\n")
		share.append("\t\tac{i}.push(a + {i mod 3 + 1})\n")
		share.append("\t\tlet aq{i} = try ac{i}.slice(0, endIndex: {ArraySliceWidth}) otherwise {IntArrayAlias}.create()\n")
		share.append("\t\tvar ak{i} = ac{i}.clone()\n")
		share.append("\t\tak{i}.append(ac{i})\n")
		// The COW trio's arithmetic, all of it known from the calls above: the slice is one element wide
		// because this line asked for `[0, 1)`, and the clone-then-append is the pushed count twice. That
		// second number is the one worth having — a `.append` that failed to detach, or detached and lost
		// the appended half, lands here and nowhere else in the corpus.
		share.append("\t\tacc = acc + aq{i}.count() + ak{i}.count(){neutralizeText(ArraySliceWidth + 2 * ArraySharePushCount)}\n")
		share.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("as{i}", body: share.build()))
	end 'eachGroup'

	out.append(accWitnessTail(ArrayOpsDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'arrayOpsSource'

// The `managedArrayOps` knob: M CONCRETE-MANAGED-element Array groups, over both managed element shapes.
//
// TWO ELEMENT SHAPES, because the element's drop is reached differently for each: an `Array with String`
// calls `__str_decref` per slot, while an `Array with <struct owning a String>` calls that struct's
// `__destruct_<T>`, which then frees the String. One indirection apart, and a change to the element-drop
// threading can move one without the other.
//
// The `get` is not decoration either: reading a MANAGED element out of an array owes a RETAIN, and
// `byteLength()` on the result is what keeps that read load-bearing rather than a discarded value a
// future pass may delete.
//
// ⚠ OPAQUE-`T` MANAGED ELEMENTS ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT — `Array with <type parameter>` instantiated
// managed rides the descriptor's `funcAbs64InRdata` copyFunc/dropFunc relocation, which is x64-only. The
// ladder compiles for the HOST, so generating one would fail the compile on a non-x64 host and take the
// whole run down through `brokenRun`. See the knob section's header.
// How many elements each of this knob's two blocks pushes — `ArrayGrowPushCount`'s rule, one knob over.
let ManagedArrayPushCount = 2
let ManagedRowPushCount = 1

function managedArrayOpsSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the managed-array-ops knob — {groupCount} groups over `Array with String` and `Array with <struct owning a String>`.\n")
	out.append("typealias {ManagedRowValueAlias} = int(i64.min to i64.max)\n")
	out.append("typealias {StringArrayAlias} = Array with String\n")
	out.append(managedFieldTypeDecl(ManagedRowName, managedFieldName: ManagedLabelFieldName, managedTypeName: StringTypeSpelling, valueTypeName: ManagedRowValueAlias))
	out.append("\ntypealias {ManagedRowArrayAlias} = Array with {ManagedRowName}\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(ManagedArrayOpsDriver))

	// The `get` is the one worth witnessing here: reading a MANAGED element out of an array owes a RETAIN,
	// and what comes back must be the literal pushed into slot 0 — so its byte length is this generator's
	// to state, and a read that handed back the wrong slot or a released record moves it.
	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		let element = "managed array element number {i} long enough to reach the heap"
		var strings = StringBuilder.create()
		strings.append("\t\tvar ms{i} = {StringArrayAlias}.create()\n")
		strings.append("\t\tms{i}.push(\"{element}\")\n")
		strings.append("\t\tms{i}.push(\"managed array element \{a\}\")\n")
		strings.append("\t\tlet mg{i} = try ms{i}.get(0) otherwise \"\"\n")
		strings.append("\t\tacc = acc + ms{i}.count() + mg{i}.byteLength(){neutralizeText(ManagedArrayPushCount + element.byteLength())}\n")
		strings.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("mq{i}", body: strings.build()))

		var rows = StringBuilder.create()
		rows.append("\t\tvar mr{i} = {ManagedRowArrayAlias}.create()\n")
		rows.append("\t\tmr{i}.push({ManagedRowName}.create(\"row element label number {i} for the heap\", value: a))\n")
		rows.append("\t\tacc = acc + mr{i}.count(){neutralizeText(ManagedRowPushCount)}\n")
		rows.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("mw{i}", body: rows.build()))
	end 'eachGroup'

	out.append(accWitnessTail(ManagedArrayOpsDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'managedArrayOpsSource'

// The `genericBases` knob: N generic BASE declarations, alternating `uses T` and `uses A, B`, each
// instantiated once with a trivial argument.
//
// The per-BASE cost is the point. shv2 is DICTIONARY-PASSING, not monomorphizing, so a base compiles ONE
// shared method body however many instantiations it has — which is exactly why the base count and the
// instance count are two knobs and not one. What doubles here is the number of shared bodies, the
// type-parameter registries behind them, and the opaque-`T` field accesses inside them.
//
// BOTH ARITIES DOUBLE, at half the knob each: a one-parameter base and a two-parameter base differ in how
// the type arguments are bound and slotted, and generating only the first would leave the multi-parameter
// path measured by nothing. `uses A, B` also needs its instantiation's arguments PARENTHESIZED, which a
// single argument must not be — a small syntactic fork that only exists if both shapes are emitted.
//
// ⚠ NO `where` CLAUSE, ANYWHERE. A constrained type parameter dispatches through a witness table, whose
// blob rides the x64-only `funcAbs64InRdata` relocation — see the knob section's header for why that
// would break the run rather than merely skew it.
function genericBasesSource(baseCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the generic-bases knob — {baseCount} unconstrained generic bases alternating `uses T` and `uses A, B`, each instantiated once.\n")
	out.append("typealias {GenericElementAlias} = int(i64.min to i64.max)\n")

	for t in 0 upto baseCount 'eachBase'
		let baseName = "{GenericBasePrefix}{t}"
		let instName = "{GenericBaseInstPrefix}{t}"
		let arity = t mod GenericBaseArityShapeCount
		if arity == 0 'oneParam'
			out.append(genericBoxDecl(baseName, fetch: GenericBoxFetch.none))
			out.append("\ntypealias {instName} = {baseName} with {GenericElementAlias}\n")
		end 'oneParam' else if arity == 1 'twoParam'
			out.append("\ntype {baseName} uses A, B\n")
			out.append("\texport var first as A\n")
			out.append("\texport var second as B\n")
			out.append("\n")
			out.append("\texport static function create(a A, b B) returns Self\n")
			out.append("\t\treturn Self\{first: a, second: b\}\n")
			out.append("\tend 'create'\n")
			out.append("\n")
			out.append("\texport function firstVal() returns A\n")
			out.append("\t\treturn self.first\n")
			out.append("\tend 'firstVal'\n")
			out.append("end '{baseName}'\n")
			out.append("\ntypealias {instName} = {baseName} with ({GenericElementAlias}, {GenericElementAlias})\n")
		end 'twoParam' else 'noArityEmitter'
			panic("genericBasesSource: GenericBaseArityShapeCount is {GenericBaseArityShapeCount} but arity shape {arity} has no declaration branch — add one beside `uses T` and `uses A, B`, or lower the constant")
		end 'noArityEmitter'
	end 'eachBase'

	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(GenericBasesDriver))
	for t in 0 upto baseCount 'eachUse'
		var body = StringBuilder.create()
		let arity = t mod GenericBaseArityShapeCount
		// Both accessors hand back the value the `create` on the line above put in — an opaque-`T` field
		// read through a dictionary-passed body, and the ONE thing about a generic base that a witness can
		// state without knowing anything about layout. That is precisely what makes it worth stating.
		if arity == 0 'oneParamUse'
			body.append("\t\tlet gv{t} = {GenericBaseInstPrefix}{t}.create(a)\n")
			body.append("\t\tacc = acc + gv{t}.get(){neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue())}\n")
			body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		end 'oneParamUse' else if arity == 1 'twoParamUse'
			body.append("\t\tlet gv{t} = {GenericBaseInstPrefix}{t}.create(a, b: a + {t mod 5 + 1})\n")
			body.append("\t\tacc = acc + gv{t}.firstVal(){neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue())}\n")
			body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		end 'twoParamUse' else 'noArityUse'
			panic("genericBasesSource: GenericBaseArityShapeCount is {GenericBaseArityShapeCount} but arity shape {arity} has no USE branch — the declaration loop above and this one realize the same shape set, so both need the branch")
		end 'noArityUse'
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("gb{t}", body: body.build()))
	end 'eachUse'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(GenericBasesDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'genericBasesSource'

// The lowest upper bound a generated trivial type argument's range takes, and the mask that keeps a value
// inside it. The floor is above the mask so every instance's range — `0 to floor + i` — admits every
// masked value at every rung; the range still DIFFERS per instance, which is the only property the knob
// needs from it.
let GenericInstanceElemFloor = 1000
let GenericInstanceArgMask = 255

// The `genericInstances` knob: M INSTANTIATIONS of ONE fixed base — the per-INSTANCE cost (the layout
// descriptor, the per-instance typealias, and a managed instance's own `__destruct_<instance>`).
//
// ⚠ EVERY INSTANCE GETS ITS OWN TYPE ARGUMENT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN OF THIS KNOB. Two aliases of
// `Box with Integer` are two NAMES for one instance, so M aliases over one argument would grow M names
// and exactly one descriptor — a dimension that reads as covered and is flat by construction, which is
// this file's founding defect in miniature. So the arguments are generated alongside the aliases:
//
//   • A TRIVIAL argument: a ranged int alias whose RANGE is unique to `i`. A generic's ranged element
//     type is part of its type (`e4146cf8e`), so distinct ranges are distinct instances with distinct
//     element widths — which is also why the pushed value is masked rather than passed raw.
//   • A MANAGED argument: a struct unique to `i` that owns a `String`. There is only ONE `String` type,
//     so `Box with String` can never be more than one instance; a per-`i` holder is how the MANAGED
//     ownership path (the argument is MOVED into the box and dropped through the instance's cascade)
//     scales at all.
function genericInstancesSource(instanceCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the generic-instances knob — {instanceCount} instantiations of one fixed base, each with its OWN type argument (trivial ranged int / managed holder).\n")
	out.append("typealias {GenericInstanceHoldValueAlias} = int(i64.min to i64.max)\n")
	out.append(genericBoxDecl(GenericInstanceBaseName, fetch: GenericBoxFetch.none))

	for i in 0 upto instanceCount 'eachInstance'
		let shape = i mod GenericInstanceArgShapeCount
		if shape == 0 'trivialArg'
			out.append("\ntypealias {GenericInstanceElemPrefix}{i} = int(0 to {GenericInstanceElemFloor + i})\n")
			out.append("typealias {GenericInstancePrefix}{i} = {GenericInstanceBaseName} with {GenericInstanceElemPrefix}{i}\n")
		end 'trivialArg' else if shape == 1 'managedArg'
			out.append(managedFieldTypeDecl("{GenericInstanceHoldPrefix}{i}", managedFieldName: ManagedLabelFieldName, managedTypeName: StringTypeSpelling, valueTypeName: GenericInstanceHoldValueAlias))
			out.append("typealias {GenericInstancePrefix}{i} = {GenericInstanceBaseName} with {GenericInstanceHoldPrefix}{i}\n")
		end 'managedArg' else 'noArgEmitter'
			// The CHECK between `GenericInstanceArgShapeCount` and the branches that realize it — the same
			// fact twice, and a 2-way `if/else` keyed off it would absorb a raised count in SILENCE, leaving
			// the manifest claiming an ownership path nothing generates.
			panic("genericInstancesSource: GenericInstanceArgShapeCount is {GenericInstanceArgShapeCount} but argument shape {shape} has no declaration branch — add one beside the trivial-ranged-int and managed-holder branches, or lower the constant")
		end 'noArgEmitter'
	end 'eachInstance'

	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(GenericInstancesDriver))
	for i in 0 upto instanceCount 'eachUse'
		var body = StringBuilder.create()
		let shape = i mod GenericInstanceArgShapeCount
		if shape == 0 'trivialUse'
			// Masked, not raw: the argument's alias is narrow, so an unmasked `acc` would be a value the
			// range admits only by luck. The mask is opaque either way (the folder cannot see through
			// `acc`), and the compiler cannot prove the bound, so the range guard is emitted regardless.
			// What comes back out of the box is what went in, which is the whole witness a borrowed
			// argument can offer — and it travels through an opaque-`T` field read to get here.
			body.append("\t\tlet xv{i} = {GenericInstancePrefix}{i}.create(a and {GenericInstanceArgMask})\n")
			body.append("\t\tacc = acc + xv{i}.get(){neutralizeText(corpusSeedValue() and GenericInstanceArgMask)}\n")
			body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
		end 'trivialUse' else if shape == 1 'managedUse'
			// ⚠ THE MANAGED ARM HAS NO VALUE TO WITNESS, AND SAYS SO RATHER THAN INVENTING ONE. Its `T` is a
			// struct MOVED into the box, so reading it back through `get()` would consume a value the
			// instance now owns — a different ownership shape from the one this knob exists to price. It
			// therefore contributes nothing to `acc`, and what it still gets for free is `ownedSiteBlock`'s
			// entered-mark: a create this rung skipped is short by one and the driver says so.
			// ⚠ `_ =` rather than a binding: this arm has no value to witness, and a body `let` that
			// nothing reads is E3012 since the unused-`let` rule landed. The create this knob prices
			// is unchanged.
			body.append("\t\t_ = {GenericInstancePrefix}{i}.create({GenericInstanceHoldPrefix}{i}.create(\"generic managed instance {i} payload for the heap\", value: a))\n")
		end 'managedUse' else 'noArgUse'
			panic("genericInstancesSource: GenericInstanceArgShapeCount is {GenericInstanceArgShapeCount} but argument shape {shape} has no USE branch — the declaration loop above and this one realize the same shape set, so both need the branch")
		end 'noArgUse'
		out.append(ownedSiteBlock("xi{i}", body: body.build()))
	end 'eachUse'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(GenericInstancesDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'genericInstancesSource'

// The value an aggregate `fetch`'s `otherwise` hands back, and what the condition form's ELSE arm folds.
// They are DIFFERENT from each other and from the seed on purpose: each of the three edges a site can
// take contributes a value only that edge can contribute, so a fork that took the wrong one moves the
// witness. A shared constant would make two of the three indistinguishable.
let TryCoOwnFetchFallbackValue = 41
let TryCoOwnElseArmValue = 43
let TryCoOwnConsumerFallbackValue = 47

// Whether site `index` takes its error edge. The cycle is one full rotation of the SPELLINGS per answer,
// so every spelling is generated on both edges — keying this off `index mod 2` directly would have made
// the aggregate always succeed and the String always throw, covering half of what it claims.
//
// It is derived from `TryCoOwnSpellingCount` rather than written as its own number for this file's
// founding reason: a third spelling would otherwise leave the cycle behind at two and silently stop
// generating one of the four combinations.
function tryCoOwnTakesErrorEdge(index KnobIndex) returns bool
	return (index mod (TryCoOwnSpellingCount + TryCoOwnSpellingCount)) >= TryCoOwnSpellingCount
end 'tryCoOwnTakesErrorEdge'

// The opaque bool a site hands `fetch`/a consumer. It is written against `a` and never as `true` /
// `false`: a literal would let the folder settle the branch and delete the error edge outright, and the
// edge is what this knob exists to emit. `a` is the seed, which is positive, so `a > 0` is TAKEN and
// `a < 0` is not — and the compiler can prove neither.
function tryCoOwnLiveText(throwsHere bool) returns String
	if throwsHere 'errorEdge'
		return "a < 0"
	end 'errorEdge'

	return "a > 0"
end 'tryCoOwnLiveText'

// The per-site String payload. Long enough to leave the 56-byte inline record (so the promotion COPIES a
// heap string rather than a record the compiler could keep in registers), and unique per site so the
// literals are distinct `.rdata` records rather than one interned constant M sites share.
//
// ⚠ ITS LENGTH IS MEASURED FROM THE STRING ITSELF, never counted by hand: the generator holds the same
// bytes the emitted literal will hold — plain ASCII, no escape needed and none written — so
// `.byteLength()` here IS the `.byteLength()` the rung will compute, and a widened payload cannot leave
// a stale expectation behind.
function tryCoOwnPayloadText(index KnobIndex) returns String
	return "try co-own payload {index}: long enough to leave the inline string record"
end 'tryCoOwnPayloadText'

// The `tryCoOwn` knob: M `try` SITES over a substituted MANAGED `T` return — see `TryCoOwnSitesBase`.
//
// Each site spends the mechanism three times, and the third is a CONTROL rather than a repeat:
//
//   • the EXPRESSION form (`let x = try box.fetch(…) otherwise …`), whose promotion is lifted onto
//     `tryok`;
//   • the CONDITION form (`if let x = try box.fetch(…) 'ok' … end 'ok' else (e) 'bad' … end 'bad'`),
//     which reaches the same fork by a different road — `parseCatchingCondition`, not `desugarTry` —
//     and whose ok edge is the THEN block. This is the arm BATCH25's review found releasing the fork's
//     temporaries into the block that dominates BOTH edges;
//   • the ARGUMENT position (`try consume(box.get(), …)`), where a substituted `T` result is co-owned
//     too and the promotion must NOT move: it belongs to a value the callee is handed, on the
//     unconditional path. It prices the discrimination (`owned == target` plus the op floor) that keeps
//     the other two from over-reaching, and it is the case a widened move would break.
//
// Every one of the three is written on BOTH edges across the cycle, and each edge folds a value only it
// can produce, so the rung's exit code is a check on WHICH edge ran and not merely on whether it ran.
function tryCoOwnSource(siteCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the try-co-own knob — {siteCount} `try` sites over a substituted managed `T` return, in all three forms, on both edges.\n")
	out.append("typealias {TryCoOwnHolderValueAlias} = int(i64.min to i64.max)\n")
	out.append("\nenum {TryCoOwnErrorName} implements Error\n")
	out.append("\tbad\n")
	out.append("end '{TryCoOwnErrorName}'\n")
	out.append(managedFieldTypeDecl(TryCoOwnHolderName, managedFieldName: ManagedLabelFieldName, managedTypeName: StringTypeSpelling, valueTypeName: TryCoOwnHolderValueAlias))
	out.append(genericBoxDecl(TryCoOwnBaseName, fetch: GenericBoxFetch.throwing(TryCoOwnErrorName)))
	out.append("\ntypealias {TryCoOwnHolderBoxAlias} = {TryCoOwnBaseName} with {TryCoOwnHolderName}\n")
	out.append("typealias {TryCoOwnStringBoxAlias} = {TryCoOwnBaseName} with {StringTypeSpelling}\n")
	out.append(tryCoOwnConsumerDecl(TryCoOwnHolderConsumerName, parameterTypeName: TryCoOwnHolderName, valueText: "v.value"))
	out.append(tryCoOwnConsumerDecl(TryCoOwnStringConsumerName, parameterTypeName: StringTypeSpelling, valueText: "v.byteLength()"))

	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(TryCoOwnDriver))
	for i in 0 upto siteCount 'eachSite'
		let spelling = i mod TryCoOwnSpellingCount
		let throwsHere = tryCoOwnTakesErrorEdge(i)
		if spelling == 0 'aggregateSite'
			out.append(tryCoOwnSite(i, spelling: tryCoOwnAggregateSpelling(i), throwsHere: throwsHere))
		end 'aggregateSite' else if spelling == 1 'stringSite'
			out.append(tryCoOwnSite(i, spelling: tryCoOwnStringSpelling(i), throwsHere: throwsHere))
		end 'stringSite' else 'noSpellingEmitter'
			// The CHECK between `TryCoOwnSpellingCount` and the branches that realize it — raise the count
			// without adding an arm and the ladder stops here rather than generating fewer spellings than
			// the manifest claims.
			panic("tryCoOwnSource: TryCoOwnSpellingCount is {TryCoOwnSpellingCount} but spelling {spelling} has no emitter — add one beside the aggregate and String branches, or lower the constant")
		end 'noSpellingEmitter'
	end 'eachSite'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(TryCoOwnDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'tryCoOwnSource'

// The throwing free function the ARGUMENT-position control calls. Two of them are generated, one per
// managed spelling, from one emitter: they differ only in the parameter's type and in how a value comes
// back out of it, and a second copy would be a second place for the `throws` shape to drift.
function tryCoOwnConsumerDecl(name String, parameterTypeName String, valueText String) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\nfunction {name}(v {parameterTypeName}, live bool) returns int throws {TryCoOwnErrorName}\n")
	out.append("\tif not live 'absent'\n")
	out.append("\t\tthrow {TryCoOwnErrorName}.bad\n")
	out.append("\tend 'absent'\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("\treturn {valueText}\n")
	out.append("end '{name}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'tryCoOwnConsumerDecl'

// ⭐ **WHAT DIFFERS BETWEEN THE TWO MANAGED SPELLINGS — and it is data, not control flow.**
//
// The three `try` forms are IDENTICAL in shape for an aggregate `T` and a `String` `T`: the same fetch,
// the same catching `if`, the same argument-position control. What changes is how a value is BUILT, how
// one comes back out to something `acc` can carry, and what the ok edge is therefore worth. Two site
// emitters would state that shape twice, and the copy is exactly where a form silently stops being
// generated for one spelling — this file's founding defect, at the emitter level.
type TryCoOwnSpelling
	// The per-instance typealias the site constructs — `Box with <holder>` or `Box with String`.
	export let boxAlias as String

	// The argument text of that construction, and of the expression form's `otherwise`. They differ per
	// spelling because one builds a struct and the other is a literal.
	export let payloadText as String
	export let fallbackText as String

	// Appended to a binding to reach an int — `.value` for the aggregate, `.byteLength()` for a String.
	// It is the ONE route out of a managed `T` into the accumulator.
	export let readText as String

	// What the OK edge contributes through `readText`, and what the expression form's `otherwise`
	// contributes instead when the error edge runs. They must DIFFER, and that is the whole of what makes
	// a fold discriminate WHICH edge ran rather than merely that one did.
	export let okValue as TrackedValue
	export let fetchFallbackValue as TrackedValue

	// The throwing free function the argument-position control calls, and the binding/label stem that
	// keeps two spellings' generated names apart inside one driver.
	export let consumerName as String
	export let stem as String

	export static function create(boxAlias String, payloadText String, fallbackText String, readText String, okValue TrackedValue, fetchFallbackValue TrackedValue, consumerName String, stem String) returns TryCoOwnSpelling
		return Self{boxAlias: boxAlias, payloadText: payloadText, fallbackText: fallbackText, readText: readText, okValue: okValue, fetchFallbackValue: fetchFallbackValue, consumerName: consumerName, stem: stem}
	end 'create'
end 'TryCoOwnSpelling'

// The AGGREGATE spelling: `T` is a struct owning a String, so the caller's promotion is the single
// `__mm_retain` call the try-rewrite used to claim as its target. Its value rides in a field, so the ok
// edge is worth the seed the holder was built from.
function tryCoOwnAggregateSpelling(index KnobIndex) returns TryCoOwnSpelling
	let payload = "{TryCoOwnHolderName}.create(\"try co-own holder {index}\", value: a)"
	let fallback = "{TryCoOwnHolderName}.create(\"try co-own fallback {index}\", value: {TryCoOwnFetchFallbackValue})"
	return TryCoOwnSpelling.create(TryCoOwnHolderBoxAlias, payloadText: payload, fallbackText: fallback, readText: ".value", okValue: corpusSeedValue(), fetchFallbackValue: TryCoOwnFetchFallbackValue, consumerName: TryCoOwnHolderConsumerName, stem: "tca")
end 'tryCoOwnAggregateSpelling'

// The `String` spelling: the promotion is a COPY (`promoteToOwnedString`) whose trailing op is an
// interpolation and not a call. The rung's move is keyed on the promotion's op RANGE precisely so that
// this arm and the aggregate one need no second mechanism — so both are generated, or that claim is
// untested. Its two values are the LENGTHS of its two literals, which is what makes them differ.
function tryCoOwnStringSpelling(index KnobIndex) returns TryCoOwnSpelling
	let payload = tryCoOwnPayloadText(index)
	let fallback = "fallback {index}"
	return TryCoOwnSpelling.create(TryCoOwnStringBoxAlias, payloadText: "\"{payload}\"", fallbackText: "\"{fallback}\"", readText: ".byteLength()", okValue: payload.byteLength(), fetchFallbackValue: fallback.byteLength(), consumerName: TryCoOwnStringConsumerName, stem: "tcs")
end 'tryCoOwnStringSpelling'

// ONE SITE: the three forms, over whichever spelling and on whichever edge it was handed.
function tryCoOwnSite(index KnobIndex, spelling TryCoOwnSpelling, throwsHere bool) returns String
	let live = tryCoOwnLiveText(throwsHere)
	let stem = spelling.stem
	var body = StringBuilder.create()
	body.append("\t\tlet {stem}b{index} = {spelling.boxAlias}.create({spelling.payloadText})\n")

	// THE EXPRESSION FORM, whose promotion is lifted onto `tryok`. Its `otherwise` is worth something the
	// ok edge cannot produce, so this one fold says WHICH edge ran.
	body.append("\t\tlet {stem}e{index} = try {stem}b{index}.fetch({live}) otherwise {spelling.fallbackText}\n")
	body.append("\t\tacc = acc + {stem}e{index}{spelling.readText}{neutralizeText(tryCoOwnPick(throwsHere, taken: spelling.fetchFallbackValue, untaken: spelling.okValue))}\n")
	body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))

	// THE CONDITION FORM, whose ok edge is the THEN block — `parseCatchingCondition`'s road to the same
	// fork. Each arm folds its own value, for the same reason.
	body.append("\t\tif let {stem}c{index} = try {stem}b{index}.fetch({live}) '{stem}ok{index}'\n")
	body.append("\t\t\tacc = acc + {stem}c{index}{spelling.readText}\n")
	body.append("\t\tend '{stem}ok{index}' else ({stem}err{index}) '{stem}bad{index}'\n")
	body.append("\t\t\tacc = acc + {TryCoOwnElseArmValue}\n")
	body.append("\t\tend '{stem}bad{index}'\n")
	body.append("\t\tacc = acc{neutralizeText(tryCoOwnPick(throwsHere, taken: TryCoOwnElseArmValue, untaken: spelling.okValue))}\n")
	body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))

	// THE ARGUMENT-POSITION CONTROL — a substituted `T` result co-owned for a value the CALLEE is handed,
	// whose promotion must NOT move onto any ok edge.
	body.append("\t\tlet {stem}n{index} = try {spelling.consumerName}({stem}b{index}.get(), live: {live}) otherwise {TryCoOwnConsumerFallbackValue}\n")
	body.append("\t\tacc = acc + {stem}n{index}{neutralizeText(tryCoOwnPick(throwsHere, taken: TryCoOwnConsumerFallbackValue, untaken: spelling.okValue))}\n")
	body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
	return ownedSiteBlock("{stem}{index}", body: body.build())
end 'tryCoOwnSite'

// Which of two expectations a site owes, given the edge it takes. One function because the alternative
// is an `if` at each of the six fold sites, and an inverted one would pin `acc` to a value that is
// perfectly plausible — the OTHER edge's.
function tryCoOwnPick(throwsHere bool, taken TrackedValue, untaken TrackedValue) returns TrackedValue
	if throwsHere 'errorEdge'
		return taken
	end 'errorEdge'

	return untaken
end 'tryCoOwnPick'

// ⭐⭐ **THE NAMESPACE KNOB (N1b): M module DIRECTORIES, and one root consumer that names every one of
// them with a QUALIFIER.** See `NamespacedModulesBase` for why the corpus had none of this and what it
// meant. This is the only knob that creates directories, because a namespace IS a directory.
//
// ⭐ IT WRITES AND REGISTERS IN ONE FUNCTION, for `writeManyFunctions`' reason — but registers only ONE
// driver, the root consumer, and that asymmetry is the design rather than an omission. `main` calling
// each module's `nsHelper{m}` directly would call it BARE (`mainSource` writes `acc = {name}(acc)`),
// which is exactly the reference shape this knob exists to stop measuring. Every generated function here
// is instead reachable THROUGH a qualified reference: `main` ⇒ `scaleNamespaces` ⇒ `ns{m}.nsHelper{m}`
// and `ns{m}.inner.nsDeep{m}` ⇒ (bare, inside the module) `nsHidden{m}` / `nsShared{m}` ⇒ (qualified,
// from inside a namespaced file) `ns{m-1}.nsHelper{m-1}`. So a future DCE deletes nothing, which is the
// property `RungDriver` exists to defend.
//
// **WHAT EACH MODULE `ns{m}/` GENERATES, and every line of it is a door the flat corpus could not open:**
//
//   `ns{m}/mod.maxon` (namespace `ns{m}`)
//     • `export typealias {NsSharedAliasName}` — M-way CONTESTED, mirrored under `ns{m}.{NsSharedAliasName}`.
//       This is the whole point: the bare key is ambiguous and the qualified key is not.
//     • a FILE-PRIVATE typealias, referenced bare in this file — `RangedAliasRegistry`'s file arm, and a
//       declaration that gets NO mirror (`pushNamespaceQualifiedAlias` gates on `visibility != file`),
//       so the corpus prices the gate's false arm as well as its true one.
//     • `module function nsShared{m}` — the `Visibility.module` SUBTREE tier, which this corpus generated
//       ZERO of before this knob (`grep -c 'module ' ` read 0). Reached bare from `ns{m}/inner/`, so the
//       expensive subtree-prefix arm of `isVisibleFrom` runs at every rung.
//     • `export function nsHelper{m}` and a FILE-PRIVATE `nsHidden{m}` — and BOTH get an entry in
//       `ProgramSignatures.namespaceQualifiedFunctions`, because the function side deliberately does not
//       gate on the tier. That is the per-declaration cost this knob is really pricing.
//
//   `ns{m}/inner/deep.maxon` (namespace `ns{m}.inner` — MULTI-SEGMENT)
//     • a second M-way contested `export typealias {NsDeepAliasName}`, reachable only as
//       `ns{m}.inner.{NsDeepAliasName}`.
//     • `export function nsDeep{m}`, which reaches its ENCLOSING directory's `module` declaration BARE
//       and the PREVIOUS module's export by QUALIFIER — a qualified reference written from INSIDE a
//       namespaced file, which is not the same parser state as one written from the root.
//
// ⚠ **N1a's INTENT IS PRESERVED, NOT REPLACED.** That rung exported only `driverHeader` and left every
// other generated function file-private ON PURPOSE, so the ladder exercises `isVisibleFrom`'s expensive
// `file` arm rather than only the cheap `global` one. Every file-private declaration in the flat corpus
// is untouched, and this knob ADDS a private free function and a private alias per module — so the
// `file` arm gets MORE traffic here, not less, and the `module` arm gets its first.
function writeNamespacedModules(dir FilePath, moduleCount KnobSize, written StringArray, drivers StringArray) returns SourceByteCount throws ScaleError
	var total = 0 as SourceByteCount
	var consumer = StringBuilder.create()
	consumer.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the namespace knob's CONSUMER — one qualified reference group per module, written from the ROOT namespace.\n")
	consumer.append(witnessedDriverHeader(NamespacesDriver))

	for m in 0 upto moduleCount 'eachModule'
		let moduleDir = "{NsModuleDirPrefix}{m}"
		let innerDir = "{moduleDir}{RungRelativePathSeparator}{NsInnerDirName}"
		try createRungSubdir(dir, relative: moduleDir, written: written)
		try createRungSubdir(dir, relative: innerDir, written: written)

		total = total + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "{moduleDir}{RungRelativePathSeparator}mod", source: namespacedModuleSource(m), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threadsNothing)
		total = total + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "{innerDir}{RungRelativePathSeparator}deep", source: namespacedInnerSource(m), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threadsNothing)

		consumer.append(qualifiedReferenceGroup(m))
	end 'eachModule'

	consumer.append(accWitnessTail(NamespacesDriver))
	total = total + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "y_nsuse", source: consumer.build(), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threads(NamespacesDriver))
	return total
end 'writeNamespacedModules'

// Create one rung-relative directory and record it in `written` as a DIRECTORY — the trailing separator
// is what `pruneRungDir` reads it back by. The two facts (it exists on disk, the prune knows it is ours)
// are written here together, because a directory recorded but not created is a rung that fails to write
// and a directory created but not recorded is a rung that fails to prune.
function createRungSubdir(dir FilePath, relative String, written StringArray) throws ScaleError
	let path = dir.join(relative)
	if not Directory.create(path) 'noDir'
		throw ScaleError.reason("could not create the module directory {path.toString()}")
	end 'noDir'

	written.push("{relative}{RungRelativePathSeparator}")
end 'createRungSubdir'

// One module's own file. See `writeNamespacedModules` for what each declaration is here to price.
function namespacedModuleSource(m KnobIndex) returns String
	let privateAlias = "{NsPrivateAliasPrefix}{m}"
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: namespace `{NsModuleDirPrefix}{m}` — the namespace knob's module {m}.\n")
	out.append("public typealias {NsSharedAliasName} = int(0 to {NsAliasMask})\n")
	out.append("typealias {privateAlias} = int(0 to {NsAliasMask})\n")
	out.append("\n")
	// The `module` tier: nameable from this directory's SUBTREE and nowhere else. `ns{m}/inner/deep.maxon`
	// is what names it, which is the subtree arm rather than the same-directory one.
	out.append("module function {NsSharedPrefix}{m}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn a + {nsSharedIncrement(m)}\n")
	out.append("end '{NsSharedPrefix}{m}'\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append(driverHeader("{NsHelperPrefix}{m}"))
	// The file-private alias, named BARE from the file that declares it — the one door a file-private
	// declaration has, and the arm `pushNamespaceQualifiedAlias` mints no mirror for.
	out.append("\tlet v = (a and {NsAliasMask}) as {privateAlias}\n")
	out.append("\treturn {NsHiddenPrefix}{m}(a) + (v as int)\n")
	out.append("end '{NsHelperPrefix}{m}'\n")
	out.append("\n")
	// FILE-PRIVATE, and it still earns a `ns{m}.nsHidden{m}` entry in the namespace-qualified function set:
	// that side does not gate on the tier, so a private helper is exactly as expensive to REGISTER as an
	// exported one. Half the per-declaration cost this knob prices lives in functions nothing can call.
	out.append("function {NsHiddenPrefix}{m}(a int) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn a + {nsHiddenIncrement(m)}\n")
	out.append("end '{NsHiddenPrefix}{m}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'namespacedModuleSource'

// The two per-module increments, each written into ONE emitted `return` and read back by the tracker
// below it. Named because the consumer's witness has to know what a qualified call across a namespace
// boundary ANSWERS, and a second spelling of either would be a witness that agreed with itself and not
// with the program.
function nsSharedIncrement(m KnobIndex) returns TrackedValue
	return m mod 7 + 1
end 'nsSharedIncrement'

function nsHiddenIncrement(m KnobIndex) returns TrackedValue
	return m mod 5 + 2
end 'nsHiddenIncrement'

// What `ns{m}.nsHelper{m}(argument)` answers: its file-private helper's increment, plus the masked value
// it casts through its own file-private alias. The one-dot qualified CALL door, tracked.
function nsHelperValue(m KnobIndex, argument TrackedValue) returns TrackedValue
	return (argument + nsHiddenIncrement(m)) + (argument and NsAliasMask)
end 'nsHelperValue'

// What `ns{m}.inner.nsDeep{m}(argument)` answers: the enclosing directory's `module` function named
// BARE from one level down, and — for every module but the first — the PREVIOUS module's export named by
// qualifier from inside a namespaced file. So this witness spans two namespaces and three visibility
// tiers, and a resolution that reached the wrong module's declaration moves it.
function nsDeepValue(m KnobIndex, argument TrackedValue) returns TrackedValue
	var value = argument + nsSharedIncrement(m)
	if m > 0 'namesThePreviousModule'
		value = value + nsHelperValue(m - 1, argument: value)
	end 'namesThePreviousModule'
	return value
end 'nsDeepValue'

// The module's INNER file — namespace `ns{m}.inner`, the multi-segment half of the knob.
function namespacedInnerSource(m KnobIndex) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: namespace `{NsModuleDirPrefix}{m}{NsQualifierSeparator}{NsInnerDirName}` — the MULTI-SEGMENT half of the namespace knob.\n")
	out.append("public typealias {NsDeepAliasName} = int(0 to {NsAliasMask})\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append(driverHeader("{NsDeepFuncPrefix}{m}"))
	// The enclosing directory's `module` declaration, named BARE from a file one level DOWN — the subtree
	// prefix compare, which is `isVisibleFrom`'s most expensive arm and had no traffic at all before N1b.
	// Split for `fillerLocalsDecl`'s reason: module 0 has no predecessor, so its `acc` would be a `var`
	// nothing reassigns — E3077 — while the rest of the ladder reassigns it one line down.
	out.append("\tvar acc = 0\n")
	out.append("\tacc = {NsSharedPrefix}{m}(a)\n")

	// A qualified reference written from INSIDE a namespaced file, and pointing at a DIFFERENT namespace.
	// Module 0 has no predecessor and simply skips it — the chain is what makes these references
	// cross-module, and a module naming ITSELF by qualifier would be a shape no real program writes.
	if m > 0 'namesThePreviousModule'
		out.append("\tacc = acc + {NsModuleDirPrefix}{m - 1}{NsQualifierSeparator}{NsHelperPrefix}{m - 1}(acc)\n")
	end 'namesThePreviousModule'

	out.append("\treturn acc\n")
	out.append("end '{NsDeepFuncPrefix}{m}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'namespacedInnerSource'

// One module's worth of QUALIFIED references, as written from the ROOT namespace. Five sites, and each
// is a different door — a corpus that emitted only one of them would price a fifth of the rung.
function qualifiedReferenceGroup(m KnobIndex) returns String
	let moduleNs = "{NsModuleDirPrefix}{m}"
	let innerNs = "{moduleNs}{NsQualifierSeparator}{NsInnerDirName}"
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	// ONE-SEGMENT qualified TYPE reference: the ranged-alias mirror, resolved against the key
	// `pushNamespaceQualifiedAlias` filed. The cast is a real guarded site (the value is opaque).
	out.append("\tlet ns{m}a = (a and {NsAliasMask}) as {moduleNs}{NsQualifierSeparator}{NsSharedAliasName}\n")
	// TWO-SEGMENT qualified TYPE reference: the same mirror mechanism over a name whose qualifier is
	// itself dotted, which is the walk `parseTypeToken`'s greedy `.identifier` loop had to be widened for.
	out.append("\tlet ns{m}b = (a and {NsAliasMask}) as {innerNs}{NsQualifierSeparator}{NsDeepAliasName}\n")
	// ONE-DOT qualified CALL — `parseQualifiedCall`'s door, which asks the namespace registry with the
	// `Type.method` name it had already mangled.
	out.append("\tacc = acc + {moduleNs}{NsQualifierSeparator}{NsHelperPrefix}{m}(a) + (ns{m}a as int){neutralizeText(nsHelperValue(m, argument: corpusSeedValue()) + (corpusSeedValue() and NsAliasMask))}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	// TWO-DOT qualified CALL — `parseDottedPrimary`'s own arm, the one that WALKS the token chain and
	// ASSEMBLES the name before probing.
	out.append("\tacc = acc + {innerNs}{NsQualifierSeparator}{NsDeepFuncPrefix}{m}(a) + (ns{m}b as int){neutralizeText(nsDeepValue(m, argument: corpusSeedValue()) + (corpusSeedValue() and NsAliasMask))}\n")
	out.append(accProbe("\t"))
	// A qualified call as a STATEMENT — the third door, and the one whose absence made a namespaced
	// module's void function uncallable (`E2015: Unsupported: identifier statement`). Its result is
	// discarded here exactly as a bare call statement's is.
	out.append("\t{moduleNs}{NsQualifierSeparator}{NsHelperPrefix}{m}(a)\n")
	return out.build()
end 'qualifiedReferenceGroup'

// The `interfaceTypes` knob: N interfaces and their conforming types, spread `InterfacesPerFile` to a
// file — the per-CONFORMANCE cost.
//
// ⭐ IT WRITES AND REGISTERS IN ONE FUNCTION, for `writeManyFunctions`' and `writeCallChain`'s reason:
// this knob's per-file driver names are known only inside the loop that mints them, so each is read OFF
// THE GENERATOR rather than recomputed from the knob's size.
//
// ⚠ IT GROWS FILES ON PURPOSE. `Parser.requireInterfaceForParse` scans the CURRENT FILE's interfaces once
// per `where`-constraint site, so interfaces-per-file is a measured cost multiplier and the standing
// instruction is not to land many protocol interfaces in one artifact. Holding `InterfacesPerFile`
// constant and letting the file count double keeps that scan bounded while the conformance count climbs.
// (This corpus emits no `where` clause, so there are zero sites today. The shape is kept honest for the
// rung that adds one — and that cost is in TIME, which this instrument cannot see, so the corpus paying
// it would show nothing while slowing every run.)
function writeInterfaceTypes(dir FilePath, count KnobSize, written StringArray, drivers StringArray) returns SourceByteCount throws ScaleError
	let fileCount = bucketCount(count, perBucket: InterfacesPerFile)

	var total = 0 as SourceByteCount
	for fileIndex in 0 upto fileCount 'eachFile'
		let driverName = "{InterfaceFileDriverPrefix}{fileIndex}"
		let measureAlias = "{InterfaceMeasurePrefix}{fileIndex}"

		var out = StringBuilder.create()
		out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the interface-types knob, file {fileIndex} — up to {InterfacesPerFile} interfaces and their conforming types.\n")
		// The measure alias is per FILE, not shared: a typealias is a declaration like any other, and the
		// same name declared by every one of the rung's interface files would be a redeclaration rather
		// than a convenience.
		out.append("typealias {measureAlias} = int(i64.min to i64.max)\n")

		var body = StringBuilder.create()
		body.append("\n")
		body.append(witnessedDriverHeader(driverName))
		// This knob's per-file driver is a driver like any other: it hands back its argument once every
		// dispatch site in its file has witnessed itself, so `main` can thread it with the rest.

		for slot in 0 upto InterfacesPerFile 'eachInterface'
			let index = fileIndex * InterfacesPerFile + slot
			if index >= count 'past'
				break
			end 'past'

			out.append(interfaceConformancePair("{InterfacePrefix}{index}", typeName: "{ConformerPrefix}{index}", measureAlias: measureAlias, salt: index))
			body.append(interfaceDispatchSite(index, typeName: "{ConformerPrefix}{index}", binding: "uv{index}", label: "ic{index}", salt: index))
		end 'eachInterface'

		body.append(accWitnessTail(driverName))
		out.append(body.build())

		drivers.push(driverName)
		total = total + try writeRungFile(dir, name: "y_iface_{fileIndex}", source: out.build(), written: written, drivers: drivers, driver: RungDriver.threadsNothing)
	end 'eachFile'
	return total
end 'writeInterfaceTypes'

// One interface and the type that conforms to it. Shared by both interface knobs, so the two measure the
// SAME declaration against different dimensions: how many conformances exist, and how many sites dispatch
// over a fixed set of them.
//
// The method bodies read the field by BARE NAME (`base`, not `self.base`), which is how a method body
// inside a type resolves a field — the shape `specs-shv2/interfaces.md` documents, and a different name
// resolution path from every other function in this corpus.
//
// ⚠ NO `where T is I` — concrete dispatch only. A constrained type parameter's method call is a witness
// dispatch, whose table rides the x64-only `funcAbs64InRdata` relocation; see the knob section's header.
function interfaceConformancePair(interfaceName String, typeName String, measureAlias String, salt KnobIndex) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("\ninterface {interfaceName}\n")
	out.append("\tfunction {InterfaceProbeMethod}() returns {measureAlias}\n")
	out.append("\tfunction {InterfaceScaleMethod}(n {measureAlias}) returns {measureAlias}\n")
	out.append("end '{interfaceName}'\n")
	out.append("\ntype {typeName} implements {interfaceName}\n")
	out.append("\tlet base as {measureAlias}\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("\tfunction {InterfaceProbeMethod}() returns {measureAlias}\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn base + {salt mod 7 + 1}\n")
	out.append("\tend '{InterfaceProbeMethod}'\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("\tfunction {InterfaceScaleMethod}(n {measureAlias}) returns {measureAlias}\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn base * 2 + n\n")
	out.append("\tend '{InterfaceScaleMethod}'\n")
	out.append("\n")
	out.append("\tstatic function create(base {measureAlias}) returns Self\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn Self\{base: base\}\n")
	out.append("\tend 'create'\n")
	out.append("end '{typeName}'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'interfaceConformancePair'

// One CONCRETE interface-dispatch site: build a conforming instance and call both of its interface
// methods, one with no arguments and one with an argument. Shared by both interface knobs for the reason
// `structCreateInBlock` is shared by both struct knobs.
// ⚠ `salt` IS THE CONFORMER'S SALT, NOT THIS SITE'S INDEX, and the two are not the same number. The
// conformance pair's `probe()` returns `base + salt mod 7 + 1` for the salt IT was declared with, and
// `ifaceDispatch` rotates M sites over a FIXED set of conformances — so site 9 there calls the type
// declared with salt `9 mod IfaceDispatchTypeCount`. Reading the site index instead would have been right
// for `interfaceTypes`, where the two coincide, and quietly wrong for every rung of the other knob.
function interfaceDispatchSite(index KnobIndex, typeName String, binding String, label String, salt KnobIndex) returns String
	let scaleArgument = index mod 5 + 1
	// `base` is the accumulator because the `create` on the line above put it there, so both method
	// answers are known: `probe()` adds the conformer's salt to it, and `scaleBy(n)` doubles it and adds
	// `n`. Two CONCRETE dispatches, and a call that landed on the wrong conformance moves the first.
	let probed = corpusSeedValue() + (salt mod 7 + 1)
	let scaled = corpusSeedValue() * 2 + scaleArgument
	var body = StringBuilder.create()
	body.append("\t\tlet {binding} = {typeName}.create(a)\n")
	body.append("\t\tacc = acc + {binding}.{InterfaceProbeMethod}() + {binding}.{InterfaceScaleMethod}({scaleArgument}){neutralizeText(probed + scaled)}\n")
	body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
	return ownedSiteBlock(label, body: body.build())
end 'interfaceDispatchSite'

// The `ifaceDispatch` knob: M CONCRETE dispatch SITES over a FIXED set of conformances.
//
// The per-SITE cost is the point — the receiver's construction and the method call's lowering — so what
// doubles is the number of call sites while the number of conformances stays put. That is the whole of
// the difference from `interfaceTypes`, and the reason the two are not one knob with a bigger number:
// this is the column that moves when interface-method CALL lowering changes, and that one is the column
// that moves when `ConformanceCheck` does.
//
// It declares its OWN fixed conformances rather than reaching for the other knob's, exactly as
// `structAllocsSource` declares its own types: a knob that read another knob's declarations would move
// whenever that knob was retuned.
function ifaceDispatchSource(siteCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the interface-dispatch knob — {siteCount} concrete dispatch sites over {IfaceDispatchTypeCount} conformances.\n")
	out.append("typealias {DispatchMeasureAlias} = int(i64.min to i64.max)\n")

	for t in 0 upto IfaceDispatchTypeCount 'eachType'
		out.append(interfaceConformancePair("{DispatchInterfacePrefix}{t}", typeName: "{DispatchConformerPrefix}{t}", measureAlias: DispatchMeasureAlias, salt: t))
	end 'eachType'

	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(IfaceDispatchDriver))
	for i in 0 upto siteCount 'eachSite'
		out.append(interfaceDispatchSite(i, typeName: "{DispatchConformerPrefix}{i mod IfaceDispatchTypeCount}", binding: "dv{i}", label: "id{i}", salt: i mod IfaceDispatchTypeCount))
	end 'eachSite'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(IfaceDispatchDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'ifaceDispatchSource'

// ⭐⭐ **THE FOUR `.managed`-IN-VALUE-POSITION DOORS, ON ONE BINDING-OWNED ARRAY (BATCH9 G7).** Every one
// of them is the CO-OWNED arm of `Parser.bufferSurfaceOf` (`Parser.maxon:29149-29151`): `ba{index}` is a
// BINDING, so somebody else already owes the record a drop and the surface value must become a SECOND
// owner through `coOwnAggregateAsTemp` — a `__mm_retain`, a tracked temp and the mark. That is the
// EXPENSIVE arm of the two, and the pending-temp block below is the cheap one; a knob that reached only
// one of them would price half the mechanism.
//
// ⭐ **EVERY `.length()` HERE IS THE PROOF, NOT DECORATION.** `length` is on the BUFFER roster and NOT on
// the `Array` one, so each of these lines compiles ONLY IF the corresponding `markBufferSurface` ran —
// measured, four ways, by declaring each carrier as the plain `Array` alias instead and taking **E2015
// `Array` member 'length'** every time. So "does the ladder reach the mechanism?" is answered by the rung
// compiling at all, and cannot rot into a claim nobody re-checks.
//
// The FOURTH door is the struct FIELD, which is not a fourth spelling of the same thing: a field's declared
// surface is written by the tolerant declaration SWEEP and DERIVED at the read
// (`Parser.markDeclaredSurface`'s field carrier), which is the carrier with the documented file-ORDER
// hazard. The other two carriers — a PARAMETER (`scaleBufLength`) and a RETURN clause
// (`scaleBufferOf{index}`) — are reached by the two lines above it.
// ⭐ **EVERY `.length()` HERE ANSWERS THE NUMBER OF `push`ES THIS FUNCTION WROTE, AND THAT IS A SECOND
// PROOF ON TOP OF THE COMPILE-TIME ONE.** The block's existing evidence is that it compiles at all —
// `length` is on the BUFFER roster and not on `Array`'s, so a missing `markBufferSurface` is E2015. What
// it could not say before is whether the surface a door produced VIEWS THE SAME ELEMENTS: a co-owned arm
// that retained the wrong record, or a `return` door handing back an empty view, compiles perfectly and
// answers the wrong length. All four value doors are witnessed against the same two pushes here.
function bufferValueDoorsBlock(index KnobIndex) returns String
	let published = BufferValueDoorsPushCount
	var body = StringBuilder.create()
	body.append("\t\tvar ba{index} = {BufferSurfaceArrayAlias}.create()\n")
	body.append("\t\tba{index}.push((a and {BufferSurfaceByteMask}) as {ByteAliasName})\n")
	body.append("\t\tba{index}.push(((a + {index mod 5 + 1}) and {BufferSurfaceByteMask}) as {ByteAliasName})\n")
	body.append("\t\tlet bm{index} = ba{index}.managed\n")
	body.append("\t\tacc = acc + bm{index}.length() + {BufferSurfaceReadName}(ba{index}.managed){neutralizeText(2 * published)}\n")
	body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
	body.append("\t\tacc = acc + {BufferSurfaceReturnPrefix}{index}(ba{index}).length() + ba{index}.count(){neutralizeText(2 * published)}\n")
	body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
	body.append("\t\tlet bh{index} = {BufferSurfaceHoldName}.create(ba{index}.managed, value: a)\n")
	body.append("\t\tacc = acc + bh{index}.{BufferSurfaceHoldFieldName}.length(){neutralizeText(published)}\n")
	body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
	return ownedSiteBlock("bv{index}", body: body.build())
end 'bufferValueDoorsBlock'

// How many elements the value-doors block pushes before every door reads its length back — the number
// each of those four `.length()` calls must answer.
let BufferValueDoorsPushCount = 2

// ⭐ **THE OTHER ARM OF `bufferSurfaceOf` (`Parser.maxon:29144-29147`) — a receiver the STATEMENT already
// owns.** `"…".toByteArray()` is an unbound owned temporary, so `pendingTempsContain` answers yes and the
// mark goes on the receiver's OWN `ValueId` with no retain at all. It is a genuinely different cost from
// the block above — one `Set` insert against a retain, a tracked temp and a scope-exit drop — and it is the
// spelling every `path.toByteArray().managed` in `stdlib/` writes, so it is the one the real corpus is
// thickest in.
//
// The literal is LONG for `stringOpsSource`'s reason: a short one lives inline in the 56-byte record and
// the bytes never reach the heap the buffer surface is a view onto.
function bufferPendingTempBlock(index KnobIndex) returns String
	// The view is over the literal's own bytes, so its length is the literal's byte count — which makes
	// this the one door whose witness also checks `toByteArray()` itself.
	let payload = "scale buffer payload segment {index} long enough to reach the heap"
	var body = StringBuilder.create()
	body.append("\t\tlet bt{index} = \"{payload}\".toByteArray().managed\n")
	body.append("\t\tacc = acc + bt{index}.length(){neutralizeText(payload.byteLength())}\n")
	body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
	return ownedSiteBlock("bt{index}", body: body.build())
end 'bufferPendingTempBlock'

// The two producers that are not `.managed` at all: `__ManagedMemory.create`
// (`Parser.parseManagedMemoryStaticCall`) and a `slice` taken THROUGH the buffer surface
// (`Parser.parseArraySlice`, whose `bufferSurface` arm marks the result).
//
// ⭐ **THE SLICE'S SECOND ARGUMENT IS POSITIONAL, AND THAT IS WHAT PROVES THE SURFACE REACHED THE PARSE.**
// `endIndex:` is REQUIRED on the `Array` surface and OPTIONAL on the buffer one
// (`Parser.consumeArrayArgLabel`), so `slice(0, 2)` on an array is **E2010 Expected identifier but got
// 'integer literal'** — measured. The rung compiling is therefore evidence about the arm taken, not merely
// about the call being made.
//
// ⚠ **THE `otherwise` MUST DIVERGE, OR THE KNOB SILENTLY STOPS MEASURING.** A value `try` whose fallback
// produces an ordinary `ByteArray` merges a marked value with an unmarked one and the result is correctly
// NOT a buffer — measured, as E2015 on the very next `setLength`. `panic` is also what these three deserve:
// the capacity is requested one line above, the length is published inside it and the slice is inside that,
// so none of them can fail (`.claude/CLAUDE.md`: a `try` that should never fail uses `otherwise panic`).
function bufferStaticAndSliceBlock(index KnobIndex) returns String
	var body = StringBuilder.create()
	body.append("\t\tvar bc{index} = try {ManagedMemoryTypeSpelling}.create({BufferSurfaceCapacity}, elementSize: {BufferSurfaceElementSize}) otherwise panic(\"scale corpus: the {BufferSurfaceCapacity}-byte buffer this line just asked for\")\n")
	body.append("\t\ttry bc{index}.setLength({BufferSurfacePublishedLength}) otherwise panic(\"scale corpus: length {BufferSurfacePublishedLength} is inside the capacity allocated one line above\")\n")
	body.append("\t\tlet bq{index} = try bc{index}.slice(0, {BufferSurfaceSliceEnd}) otherwise panic(\"scale corpus: end {BufferSurfaceSliceEnd} is inside the length published one line above\")\n")
	// The three constants are ORDERED — capacity, then the length published inside it, then the slice
	// inside that — and the two `.length()` calls read the last two back. So the ordering this comment
	// asserts is now CHECKED rather than merely asserted: a `setLength` that clamped, or a `slice` that
	// returned the whole buffer, lands here.
	body.append("\t\tacc = acc + bq{index}.length() + bc{index}.length(){neutralizeText(BufferSurfaceSliceEnd + BufferSurfacePublishedLength)}\n")
	body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
	return ownedSiteBlock("bc{index}", body: body.build())
end 'bufferStaticAndSliceBlock'

// The producer that reads a DIFFERENT column to decide — `Parser.markElementReadSurface`, which marks an
// element read only when the RECEIVER's elements are buffers (`bufferSurfaceElements`, the mark
// `Array with __ManagedMemory` earns from its alias). Every array accessor funnels through one mint, so
// this one `get` prices the arm all six of them share.
//
// The pushed element is a `String`'s byte view rather than a `__ManagedMemory.create`, because the two are
// the SAME instance (`Array with Byte`) and this element only has to BE a buffer — the create path is
// already priced one block up, and paying for it twice would put two producers on one column.
function bufferElementReadBlock(index KnobIndex) returns String
	// The element read back is the one element pushed, so its length is that literal's byte count and the
	// array's count is one — which is what makes `markElementReadSurface` answering about the WRONG slot
	// visible rather than merely compiling.
	let element = "scale buffer element {index} long enough to reach the heap"
	var body = StringBuilder.create()
	body.append("\t\tvar bf{index} = {BufferSurfaceOfBuffersAlias}.create()\n")
	body.append("\t\tbf{index}.push(\"{element}\".toByteArray())\n")
	body.append("\t\tlet bg{index} = try bf{index}.get(0) otherwise panic(\"scale corpus: index 0 of an array pushed to one line above\")\n")
	body.append("\t\tacc = acc + bg{index}.length() + bf{index}.count(){neutralizeText(element.byteLength() + BufferElementPushCount)}\n")
	body.append(accProbe("\t\t"))
	return ownedSiteBlock("bf{index}", body: body.build())
end 'bufferElementReadBlock'

// How many buffers the element-read block pushes before reading one back.
let BufferElementPushCount = 1

// The `bufferSurfaces` knob: M groups, each producing the `__ManagedMemory` BUFFER SURFACE through every
// door a HOST-PORTABLE corpus can spell — see `BufferSurfaceSitesBase` for the measurement that says the
// ladder had none of them.
//
// SIX of `markBufferSurface`'s EIGHT call sites are reached, and the two that are not are named here rather
// than left to read as coverage:
//   • A FUSED WRAPPER's inline `managed` read (`Parser.emitFieldLoad`), which is what serves the corpus's
//     `String.addressableBytes()` — a `module function` no file outside `stdlib/` may call, and reachable
//     only from a type implementing one of `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`'s literal markers, which a generated rung
//     cannot declare either. No knob can close this; it is a property of the door, not of the corpus.
//     ⚠ It was `dispatchStringMethod`'s own `addressableBytes` arm until W49 wave 7 retired that member;
//     the arm's site died and this one took the mint, so the COUNT is unchanged and the NAME is not.
//   • `__ManagedDirectory.filename` / `currentPath` (`Parser.emitOwnedManagedMemoryResult`) lowers to the
//     `__md_*` runtime entries, which are x64-windows only — MEASURED as **E3104 `this construct is
//     x64-windows only at this rung: it lowers to the runtime entry '__md_current_path', which has no
//     arm64-macos implementation`**. `compileRung` spawns `build` with no `--target`, so the ladder always
//     compiles for the HOST and a rung that generated one would FAIL TO COMPILE and take the whole run down
//     as a BROKEN RUN — exactly the constraint that keeps witness tables and `Set` out of this corpus.
//
// The TWO `Array with <T>` instances this knob adds are FIXED, exactly as the array knobs' three are, and
// only the site count doubles: `{BufferSurfaceArrayAlias}` — which IS `Array with Byte`, so the `.managed`
// receivers, `toByteArray()` and `create(…, elementSize: 1)` are all one instance and not three — and
// `{BufferSurfaceOfBuffersAlias}`, the array OF buffers the element-read producer needs.
function bufferSurfacesSource(groupCount KnobSize) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: the buffer-surface knob — {groupCount} groups reaching `.managed` in VALUE position (let / argument / return / struct field) plus `{ManagedMemoryTypeSpelling}.create`, a slice through the surface, and an element read off an array of buffers.\n")
	out.append("typealias {ByteAliasName} = int(0 to u8.max)\n")
	out.append("typealias {BufferSurfaceArrayAlias} = Array with {ByteAliasName}\n")
	out.append("typealias {BufferSurfaceOfBuffersAlias} = Array with {ManagedMemoryTypeSpelling}\n")
	out.append("typealias {BufferSurfaceHoldValueAlias} = int(i64.min to i64.max)\n")
	out.append(managedFieldTypeDecl(BufferSurfaceHoldName, managedFieldName: BufferSurfaceHoldFieldName, managedTypeName: ManagedMemoryTypeSpelling, valueTypeName: BufferSurfaceHoldValueAlias))

	// The PARAMETER carrier of the declared surface, and the ARGUMENT door's callee. ONE function, because a
	// parameter's spelling is read once per DECLARATION while the axis that has to double is the CALL — which
	// the driver makes once per group.
	out.append("\nfunction {BufferSurfaceReadName}(m {ManagedMemoryTypeSpelling}) returns int\n")
	out.append("\treturn m.length()\n")
	out.append("end '{BufferSurfaceReadName}'\n")

	// The RETURN carrier, and the `return` door onto `bufferSurfaceOf`. ONE PER GROUP, because a `return` is
	// one per function by construction: a single shared returner would leave this door fixed-size while every
	// other door doubled, which is `GenericInstancesBase`'s "M names, one descriptor" failure one door over.
	// The receiver is a BORROWED parameter, which is `stdlib/File.maxon:147`'s shape and the co-owned arm's
	// second reason for existing.
	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachReturner'
		out.append("\nfunction {BufferSurfaceReturnPrefix}{i}(a {BufferSurfaceArrayAlias}) returns {ManagedMemoryTypeSpelling}\n")
		out.append("\treturn a.managed\n")
		out.append("end '{BufferSurfaceReturnPrefix}{i}'\n")
	end 'eachReturner'

	out.append("\n")
	out.append(witnessedDriverHeader(BufferSurfacesDriver))
	for i in 0 upto groupCount 'eachGroup'
		out.append(bufferValueDoorsBlock(i))
		out.append(bufferPendingTempBlock(i))
		out.append(bufferStaticAndSliceBlock(i))
		out.append(bufferElementReadBlock(i))
	end 'eachGroup'
	out.append(accWitnessTail(BufferSurfacesDriver))
	return out.build()
end 'bufferSurfacesSource'

// `main`, which threads the seed through every generated function.
//
// It calls all of them ON PURPOSE. An uncalled function is a function a future dead-code pass could
// legitimately delete, and the day that pass lands, the `manyFunctions` knob would silently
// evaporate and the ladder would go flat — reporting perfect linearity because it had stopped
// compiling anything. Threading `acc` through every call makes each one load-bearing.
//
// ⭐ **AND THE THREADED TOTAL IS NOW THE RUNG'S EXIT CODE.** Every driver hands back its argument
// UNCHANGED when its own witnesses held (`driverWitnessTail`), so `acc` is the seed at every one of
// these lines and at the last of them — and the rung exits `CorpusOkExit` if and only if it still is.
// A driver that computed the wrong thing returns `a + CorpusWitnessFailedOffset` instead, which no
// later driver can cancel, so one bad site anywhere in the rung reaches this comparison.
//
// ⚠ The expectation is a LITERAL here and an `a + K` expression in every driver, and the difference is
// deliberate: `main` has no opaque parameter to anchor to, and it does not need one — its left-hand side
// is `scaleSeed()`'s loop-carried result threaded through forty-odd calls, which nothing can fold.
//
// ⭐ EVERY name comes from `drivers`, the exact list the generators registered as they wrote each
// file — so NO enumeration is written down a second time here and none can drift from the names on
// disk. It used to be true of the `manyFunctions` loop alone, and this comment said so while the
// thirteen lines directly beneath it re-spelled every other knob's driver by hand: a comment
// contradicted by the code it sat on. Those thirteen are now `RungDriver.threads(...)` arguments at
// the site that writes each file, which is a place the compiler makes you fill in.
function mainSource(drivers StringArray) returns String
	var out = StringBuilder.create()
	out.append("// Generated by ScaleCorpus: threads the opaque seed through every generated function.\n")
	out.append("function main() returns ExitCode\n")
	out.append("\tvar acc = {SeedName}()\n")

	for name in drivers 'eachDriver'
		out.append("\tacc = {name}(acc)\n")
	end 'eachDriver'

	out.append("\tif acc == {corpusSeedValue()} 'ok'\n")
	out.append("\t\treturn {CorpusOkExit}\n")
	out.append("\tend 'ok'\n")
	out.append("\treturn {CorpusWitnessFailedExit}\n")
	out.append("end 'main'\n")
	return out.build()
end 'mainSource'

// ===========================================================================================
// The corpus self-check — THE reason anything the instrument reports can be believed
// ===========================================================================================
//
// The trap in this file's header is not hypothetical, and this is where it is caught. A corpus that
// folds away leaves the instrument measuring an empty compile at every rung; it would fit a perfect
// straight line through six near-zeroes and report flawless linearity. That is the most convincing
// possible wrong answer, and nothing downstream could catch it — every number would look lovely.
//
// So before anything is reported: did the compiler actually EMIT anything, and did it emit
// proportionally more of it as the program grew? Emitted code bytes are fitted against source bytes
// exactly as a phase's cost is, and a flat or sublinear result means the RUN IS BROKEN.
//
// This is the one condition the instrument exits NON-ZERO for, and the distinction is the whole
// design: a surprising number is a reading to explain, but a degenerate corpus did not produce a
// surprising reading — it produced no reading at all, about a compiler it never ran on a program
// that was never there.

// The floor each rung's emitted code must clear against the rung below it.
//
// THE LADDER DOUBLES, so a corpus that is really being compiled emits about TWICE the code at each
// rung — measured, x1.99 to x2.00 at every step. A corpus that folded away emits about the SAME code
// at every rung (x1.00), because what it emits is the fixed floor and nothing else. x1.50 sits in the
// wide gap between those two and is nowhere near either.
//
// It is deliberately not x2.00: real code size grows slightly SUBLINEARLY, because a rung's fixed
// overhead (the entry stub, the runtime floor) is amortized over more program as the ladder climbs.
export let MinCodeGrowthRatio = 150 as GrowthRatio

// A growth check needs two rungs. `--rungs=1` is a legal ladder — it measures one program, and its
// per-rung numbers are as exact as any other run's — but nothing about GROWTH can be said from it,
// this check included. The runner says so; it does not fail the run for a ladder it was asked for.
export let MinRungsForGrowth = 2

// EVERY rung is checked against the one below it, not just the ends of the ladder.
//
// That is strictly stronger than what it replaced (a power law fitted through all six code sizes,
// whose exponent had to clear a floor): a single rung that folded while its neighbours grew is a rung
// that stopped measuring the compiler, and a fit through the other five would absorb it and report a
// healthy exponent. A ratio per rung cannot absorb anything.
export function assertCorpusCompiled(results RungResultArray) throws ScaleError
	if results.count() < MinRungsForGrowth 'tooFewRungs'
		return
	end 'tooFewRungs'

	for i in 1 upto results.count() 'eachRung'
		let below = try results.get(i - 1) otherwise panic("assertCorpusCompiled: i >= 1 but results.get({i - 1}) failed")
		let rung = try results.get(i) otherwise panic("assertCorpusCompiled: i < count but results.get({i}) failed")

		if not grewBy(rung.codeBytes, previous: below.codeBytes, minimum: MinCodeGrowthRatio) 'folded'
			throw ScaleError.reason("rung {rung.rung} emitted {rung.codeBytes} bytes of code against rung {below.rung}'s {below.codeBytes} — a growth of {growthText(growthOf(rung.codeBytes, previous: below.codeBytes))} where the ladder DOUBLES the program and the floor is {growthText(GrowthReading.measured(MinCodeGrowthRatio))}. The corpus FOLDED AWAY rather than compiling, so every number this run produced is a number about nothing. The generated programs must be fold-resistant (see this file's header): seed every value from a loop-carried accumulator or a call result.")
		end 'folded'
	end 'eachRung'
end 'assertCorpusCompiled'

// The corpus's own growth, rung over rung — PRINTED, and in `--result-json`, because a reader looking
// at a set of memory numbers is entitled to see the evidence that they are numbers about a program
// that was actually compiled rather than one that folded away.
export function codeGrowthAt(results RungResultArray, rung LadderIndex) returns GrowthReading
	if rung == 0 'bottomRung'
		return GrowthReading.noBaseline
	end 'bottomRung'

	let below = try results.get(rung - 1) otherwise panic("codeGrowthAt: rung >= 1 but results.get({rung - 1}) failed")
	let here = try results.get(rung) otherwise panic("codeGrowthAt: results.get({rung}) failed")
	return growthOf(here.codeBytes, previous: below.codeBytes)
end 'codeGrowthAt'

// ===========================================================================================
// THE INSTRUMENT DECLARES WHAT IT CAN SEE — AND WHAT IT STRUCTURALLY CANNOT
// ===========================================================================================
//
// PRINTED ON EVERY RUN. An instrument that cannot state its own blind spots produces confident zeros:
// this corpus was blind in four measured dimensions at once — it grew no locals, nested no loops,
// shifted by no runtime value, and shifted no ranged operand — and every one of those blind spots was
// SILENT. Two independent reviews found five compiler defects the corpus could see NONE of. The
// through-line was always the same: the corpus finds only bugs someone already thought to generate. So
// the corpus now SAYS what it generates and, more importantly, what it does not and what it cannot —
// because the gaps are the part a reader cannot otherwise know, and a gap nobody is told about reads as
// coverage.
//
// ⚠ AND A MANIFEST CAN LIE BY OMISSION, WHICH IS WORSE THAN THE SILENCE IT REPLACED. This list read
// `let / var` under CONSTRUCTS GENERATED and meant the BODY-LOCAL kind — the only kind the corpus had.
// Top-level `let` and `var` were named under NEITHER heading, so two landed rungs and 36 spec tests
// read as covered by a reader doing exactly what this manifest asks of them. `structs` sat under NOT
// GENERATED for a rung after P1.0r implemented them, and integer `/` and `mod` sat there for three
// after P1.0d.3 built the entire idiv path.
//
// ⚠⚠ AND IT CAN LIE A THIRD WAY, WHICH IS THE WORST OF THE THREE: BY NAMING A GAP WITH A REASON THAT
// HAS STOPPED BEING TRUE. The struct entry said field WRITES were "P1.1a wave 2 and NOT IMPLEMENTED"
// and a struct crossing a CALL was "P1.4, deferred on the borrow-vs-consume ruling". Both had shipped
// — `specs-shv2/challenge-struct-field-assign.md`'s `struct-field-reassignment` and
// `struct-managed-field.md`'s `param-consumed-into-field` were live, non-disabled tests — and the file
// went ten rungs without either. An omission stops a reader from KNOWING; a stale reason stops them
// from LOOKING, because the reason is what they use to decide whether the gap is theirs to close.
//
// ⇒ SO WHEN A FAMILY MOVES TO CONSTRUCTS GENERATED, ITS OLD REASON GOES WITH IT. And a reason that
// remains must be one this file can still defend today: the three x64-only families below are named
// with the mechanism (`funcAbs64InRdata`) and the consequence (a BROKEN RUN, not a skewed number),
// because that is checkable, and "not implemented" was not.
//
// ⇒ THE RULE THIS LIST IS NOW HELD TO: every construct in the subset appears under EXACTLY ONE
// heading, and a construct that is PARTLY generated (structs — reads yes, writes no) says which part
// under GENERATED rather than being filed whole under either. A heading is a claim; an omission is a
// claim too, and it is the one nobody audits.
//
// THREE of these are STRUCTURAL — no knob can close them, so they are stated as blind spots rather
// than filed as TODOs:
//   • NO WALL TIME — and this one is HALF-CURED, so it is stated at its new width rather than at its
//     old one. scale-test used to measure allocations and bytes and nothing else, which made a
//     time-only superlinearity invisible by construction; it now also measures per-phase CPU TICKS,
//     the compiling thread's own time, and that column sees exactly that cost. The loop-nest knob's
//     scan remains the worked example — of a cost ONE column can see and another cannot. What is
//     still structurally absent is WALL time, which counts every other process on the box and
//     therefore cannot be trended at all. And the CPU column is not exact: it carries a few percent
//     of noise, so a movement inside that band is not a datapoint.
//   • THE SUBSET. shv2 compiles a fragment of Maxon, so whole language features generate nothing at
//     all, and a codegen change to any of them moves no number here.
//   • ONLY WHAT THE LADDER TRIGGERS — the blind spot the CPU column does NOT touch, in ANY column.
//     A construct the generator never emits, or a knob set too low to reach the cost it exists to
//     measure, reads a flat zero in allocations, in bytes AND in CPU ticks alike. `FloatsLivePerSpillLoop`
//     was 4, which stopped overflowing the callee-saved XMM half the day that half existed, so the
//     knob whose whole job is forcing a float spill forced none and a genuinely quadratic path read
//     FLAT. A delta of zero from an instrument that cannot express the feature is not evidence of
//     anything, and no amount of measuring can distinguish it from a delta of zero that means the
//     compiler did not change.
export function printCorpusManifest()
	print("\n")
	print("scale-test: CORPUS MANIFEST — what this ladder exercises, and what it structurally cannot see.\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  CONSTRUCTS GENERATED (a codegen change to one of these moves the memory numbers):\n")
	print("    int arithmetic (+ - *), comparisons, BODY-LOCAL let / var, return\n")
	print("    integer / and mod — the idiv path, over an OPAQUE divisor, with the dividend live across\n")
	print("      the RAX/RDX clobber (a constant divisor folds and measures nothing). BOTH shapes, on\n")
	print("      two knobs: the divisor cast into a NON-ZERO range (the bare idiv) and the divisor left\n")
	print("      possibly-zero (the CHECKED divide — cmp/setcc/or plus a three-block `try` fork, A1)\n")
	print("    TOP-LEVEL let — the constant arena and its initializer DFS, as a 2-PARENT DAG so an\n")
	print("      unmemoized DFS would go exponential rather than merely slow. It is FOLDED into every\n")
	print("      use and emits almost no code: that is correct, and it is the front end being measured\n")
	print("    TOP-LEVEL var — .data slots, GlobalDataTable.layOut, globalAddr/loadIndirect/storeIndirect\n")
	print("    STRUCTS (the heap): `type` declarations, an explicit `static create`, Self\{\} construction,\n")
	print("      field READS *and* field WRITES (`p.y = ...`, and `o.inner.x = ...` through a loaded box),\n")
	print("      a struct crossing a CALL BOUNDARY as a parameter, and the __mm_alloc site each create\n")
	print("      emits. ⚠ THIS ENTRY USED TO CLAIM THE OPPOSITE — that writes were 'P1.1a wave 2 and NOT\n")
	print("      IMPLEMENTED' and a struct across a call 'P1.4, deferred on the borrow-vs-consume ruling'.\n")
	print("      Both had shipped, with live spec tests, for ten rungs\n")
	print("    the COLORER's exhaustion repair — straight-line groups holding the whole GPR pool live and\n")
	print("      then a callee-saved-confined set on top, so `chooseRegister` runs out of registers at a\n")
	print("      point whose Hall verdict is FEASIBLE and `SplitDriver.repairAtExhaustion` splits it. It\n")
	print("      is DISTINCT from the pressured-loops knob, which makes the SPLITTER work instead; before\n")
	print("      it, that path ran ZERO times at every rung and every column read a confident Delta-0\n")
	print("    if, if/else, while, and NESTED while (to a doubling depth); `break` out of a loop\n")
	print("    functions (<=6 params), cross-file merge, chained calls holding values live across them\n")
	print("    short-circuit `and` and `or` (control flow, not operators), `xor` and `not` over bools,\n")
	print("      bitwise `and` / `or` / `xor` / `not` over ints\n")
	print("    shl / shr with: a CONSTANT count (folded) AND a DYNAMIC count (runtime-guarded), over a\n")
	print("      SIGNED operand (arithmetic/sign fill) AND a ranged int(0 to u64.max) operand (zero fill)\n")
	print("    ranged typealiases at BOTH doors that emit a RUNTIME RANGE GUARD over an OPAQUE value —\n")
	print("      a cast or a `return` of a LITERAL is settled at compile time and emits nothing, so both\n")
	print("      are driven from the opaque seed. `grep -c 'Range check failed' <rung binary>` is the\n")
	print("      audit, and it reads 41 at rung 0 and 1,281 at rung 5:\n")
	print("        an ALIAS-NAMED `as` cast — the divisor cast into `{ScaleDivisorAlias}`, one per\n")
	print("          int-divide group so it DOUBLES, plus ONE fixed `{CoverageNarrowAlias}` cast. This\n")
	print("          door carries `guardSiteAt`, the guard CHAIN and `splitChainEnd`\n")
	print("        a narrowed `return` TYPE — its own doubling SITE count, all in ONE function and all\n")
	print("          returning ONE parameter. This door carries `retBlockOf` and `splitBlockInPlace`,\n")
	print("          and NOTHING generated one until A2a: every alias reaching a `return` was\n")
	print("          full-range, which `rangeIsFull` discards, so a per-site scan measured at x3.20 CPU\n")
	print("          per doubling on a hand ladder read Δ0 here in every column\n")
	print("    `print` — the formatted-output path\n")
	print("    FLOAT (the SSE band): + - * / , compares in BOTH forms (feeding a jcc, and materialized\n")
	print("      as a bool), float PHIs (branch-merged and loop-carried), int->float promotion and\n")
	print("      trunc (the conversion band's two directions), and floats live ACROSS A CALL — whose\n")
	print("      allowed set is EXACTLY empty, so every one is a forced spill\n")
	print("    ENUM declarations (bare and int-backed) and EXHAUSTIVE `match` in BOTH forms — statement\n")
	print("      (`then`, arms that assign, so the continuation phis are minted) and expression (`gives`) —\n")
	print("      over an OPAQUE scrutinee, and rotating over ALL THREE dispatch strategies: the linear\n")
	print("      compare chain (<4 intervals), the balanced binary search (>=4, span too wide for a table)\n")
	print("      and the JUMP TABLE (>=4 and dense: an .rdata slot table plus an indirect jump)\n")
	print("    STRING (the heap): literals long enough to leave the 56-byte record, INTERPOLATION of the\n")
	print("      opaque accumulator (an int->String conversion that allocates), `.append` (which detaches\n")
	print("      onto a private buffer), `==` through __str_eq, `.byteLength()` as the way back out to int,\n")
	print("      and a struct owning a String field dropped through its synthesized __destruct_<T> cascade\n")
	print("    STRING, THE REST OF THE MEMBER SURFACE — four knobs, one per `dispatchStringMethod` family,\n")
	print("      because the retirement of these members onto `stdlib/String.maxon`'s bodies moves ONE\n")
	print("      family at a time and each wave's cost has to be readable on its own:\n")
	print("        BYTE/ASCII (slice C): startsWith / endsWith / contains — each in BOTH directions, since a\n")
	print("          predicate stuck at `true` is witnessed identically by its true arm alone — toLower /\n")
	print("          toUpper (pinned by a needle that exists only AFTER the conversion, because an ASCII case\n")
	print("          map does not change a length), replace / replaceFirst (whose replacement is deliberately\n")
	print("          WIDER than the needle) and split (count AND element 0, so a split into empty pieces fails)\n")
	print("        STRINGINDEX (slice B2): startIndex / endIndex / charIndex / bytePos, the two THROWING\n")
	print("          searches findFirst / findLast, the third throwing member indexAfter, and BOTH `slice`\n")
	print("          overloads — which are told apart by an argument LABEL rather than by a type. ⚠ EVERY\n")
	print("          throwing member is generated on BOTH EDGES: a needle no payload contains throws at every\n")
	print("          site, so the `otherwise` fork is emitted AND taken\n")
	print("        TRIMS (slice D): trim / trimStart / trimEnd, in BOTH argument forms — the default\n")
	print("          CharacterSet and an explicit one — over a core that opens and closes on a MULTI-BYTE\n")
	print("          cluster, which is the only cluster either end has to classify rather than skip.\n")
	print("          ⚠ ONE SITE PER BLOCK here, alone in this corpus, and its knob base is an EIGHTH of its\n")
	print("          siblings': a trim against `CharacterSet.whitespacesAndNewlines()` materializes that set\n")
	print("          INLINE and costs 194 x64 ops in the calling block against 50 for `decimalDigits()` and 12\n")
	print("          for a `toUpper`. Three of them in one block took `slotCount` to ~4.2M and exhausted\n")
	print("          committed memory in `PressureDeltaColumn` — rung 4 would not compile. See StringTrimOpsBase\n")
	print("        MATERIALIZERS (slices B and E): count() (the UAX #29 grapheme walk), bytes(),\n")
	print("          toByteArray(), codepoints(), utf16(), isEmpty() and clone(). ⚠ THIS IS THE CORPUS'S ONLY\n")
	print("          NON-ASCII PAYLOAD, and it has to be: over ASCII all four of those counts are THE SAME\n")
	print("          NUMBER, so a materializer answering with its neighbour's walk would witness perfectly.\n")
	print("          Its `é` + emoji + ZWJ-family segment reads 24 bytes / 3 graphemes / 7 codepoints / 11\n")
	print("          UTF-16 units — four distinct numbers, and the ZWJ sequence is what parts the first two.\n")
	print("          `clone()` is witnessed by INDEPENDENCE rather than by length: the copy is grown and both\n")
	print("          are then measured, so a clone that aliased its source reads a difference of zero\n")
	print("    ⚠ BEFORE THOSE FOUR KNOBS THE STRING KNOB GENERATED A LITERAL, AN INTERPOLATION, `.append`,\n")
	print("      `.byteLength()` AND `==`, AND NOTHING ELSE — not one predicate, case conversion, trim,\n")
	print("      StringIndex producer or materializer, at rung 0 or rung 5. Every one of them was in the\n")
	print("      subset with live spec cases, so every retirement wave would have read a flat delta of ZERO\n")
	print("      in allocations, in bytes and in CPU alike. That is this file's founding defect for the\n")
	print("      EIGHTH time; see `StringSearchOpsBase`\n")
	print("    CHARACTER (the OTHER byte record, BATCH23): character LITERALS at both widths (one ASCII byte\n")
	print("      and two, sharing a lead byte), a `let`-bound Character, `==` through __str_eq (where a\n")
	print("      one-byte literal used to be an immediate integer compare), `>` through __char_cmp plus the\n")
	print("      Ordering verdict — the ONE operation a Character has and a String does not — a literal in an\n")
	print("      INTEGER position (which REWRITES the emitted stringLiteral op into an integer literal in\n")
	print("      place, and is the only thing that fills Parser.charLiteralOps), a `match` with a character\n")
	print("      INTERVAL arm (the linear compare chain a Character scrutinee keeps), and character RANGE\n")
	print("      `for` loops in BOTH element forms — one USING the per-trip Character and one DISCARDING it\n")
	print("    ⚠ THE CORPUS EMITTED NOT ONE CHARACTER LITERAL AT ANY RUNG until that knob, so the whole model\n")
	print("      above read a flat delta of ZERO in allocations, in bytes and in CPU alike on the rung that\n")
	print("      introduced it. That is this file's founding defect for the sixth time; see `CharOpsBase`\n")
	print("    ARRAY, trivial-element: create / reserve / push / get / count, plus the O(1) COW trio\n")
	print("      slice / clone / append — which refcount the element BUFFER rather than the record\n")
	print("    ARRAY, CONCRETE-managed-element: `Array with String` and `Array with <struct owning a\n")
	print("      String>` — the element-drop function threaded through the array record, and the RETAIN a\n")
	print("      `get` of a managed element owes.\n")
	print("      ⚠ EVERY ARRAY KNOB IS PER-SITE: the `Array with <T>` INSTANCES are FIXED, and they are\n")
	print("      these FIVE and no others — `{IntArrayAlias}` (a ranged int), `{StringArrayAlias}`,\n")
	print("      `{ManagedRowArrayAlias}` (a struct owning a String), plus the buffer knob's\n")
	print("      `{BufferSurfaceArrayAlias}` (`Array with {ByteAliasName}`, which is what\n")
	print("      `{ManagedMemoryTypeSpelling}` denotes) and `{BufferSurfaceOfBuffersAlias}` (an array OF\n")
	print("      buffers). Only the OP-SITE count doubles.\n")
	print("      (Named from the generator's own constants, so `grep 'Array with' rung0/*.maxon` on a dumped\n")
	print("      corpus is the audit, and a rename cannot make this line stale.)\n")
	print("      A per-INSTANCE Array dimension — many distinct element types, so many distinct\n")
	print("      layout descriptors — is NOT generated; the generic-instances knob is the only place that\n")
	print("      cost is measured, and it measures it for a user base, not for `Array`\n")
	print("    THE `{ManagedMemoryTypeSpelling}` BUFFER SURFACE — the SECOND member roster over the same\n")
	print("      `Array` record, reached at SIX of its eight producer call sites: `.managed` in VALUE\n")
	print("      position through BOTH ownership arms (a BINDING's array, which must become a second owner\n")
	print("      via __mm_retain, and an unbound `\"…\".toByteArray()` TEMPORARY, which must not), at all four\n")
	print("      value doors (a `let`, a call ARGUMENT, a `return` out of a borrowed parameter, and a struct\n")
	print("      FIELD); `{ManagedMemoryTypeSpelling}.create(count, elementSize:)`; a `slice` taken THROUGH\n")
	print("      the surface (its second argument POSITIONAL, which is the spelling only the buffer admits);\n")
	print("      an element read off an array of buffers; and all THREE declared-surface carriers — a\n")
	print("      PARAMETER, a RETURN clause and a struct FIELD. Every buffer member here is `length()`, which\n")
	print("      the `Array` roster does NOT have, so each of these lines compiles ONLY IF its mark was made\n")
	print("      — the rung compiling at all is the evidence, and it cannot go stale\n")
	print("      (it also brings `otherwise panic(...)` on a `try` that cannot fail — the corpus's only one)\n")
	print("    GENERICS, UNCONSTRAINED: `type ... uses T` and `uses A, B` bases (dictionary-passing, so ONE\n")
	print("      shared body per base), `typealias X = Base with Arg` instantiations each with their OWN\n")
	print("      type argument, and both ownership paths for that argument — a TRIVIAL ranged int (borrowed\n")
	print("      into the box) and a MANAGED struct owning a String (moved in, dropped through the\n")
	print("      instance's own cascade)\n")
	print("    A `try` OVER A SUBSTITUTED MANAGED `T` RETURN (BATCH25): a shared generic body cannot classify\n")
	print("      its `T` return, so the CALLER takes the `+1` after the call and a `try` must carry it onto\n")
	print("      the OK EDGE alone. All three forms per site — the EXPRESSION form (lifted onto `tryok`), the\n")
	print("      CONDITION form (`if let x = try …`, whose ok edge is the THEN block and which reaches the\n")
	print("      fork through `parseCatchingCondition` rather than `desugarTry`), and the ARGUMENT position\n")
	print("      (`try consume(box.get(), …)`), where the co-own must NOT move — over BOTH managed spellings,\n")
	print("      an aggregate (whose promotion is one `__mm_retain` call) and a `String` (whose promotion is a\n")
	print("      COPY ending in an interpolation). ⚠ EVERY SITE IS GENERATED ON BOTH EDGES: the throwing\n")
	print("      condition is an opaque bool, so the error edge is emitted AND TAKEN, and each of the three\n")
	print("      edges folds a value only it can produce — the rung's exit code says WHICH edge ran\n")
	print("    ⚠ NOT ONE `try` OVER A SUBSTITUTED RETURN EXISTED AT ANY RUNG until that knob, so the rung that\n")
	print("      BUILT the lift read a flat delta of ZERO in allocations, in bytes and in CPU alike. That is\n")
	print("      this file's founding defect for the seventh time; see `TryCoOwnSitesBase`\n")
	print("    INTERFACES: `interface` declarations, `type X implements I` conformance, and CONCRETE\n")
	print("      dispatch (`x.method()`, with and without arguments) — a direct call to a known body\n")
	print("    NAMESPACES (N1b): a rung is no longer ONE FLAT DIRECTORY. M module SUBDIRECTORIES, each with a\n")
	print("      nested `{NsInnerDirName}/` of its own, so `deriveModuleDirectory` answers something for the first\n")
	print("      time — and every reference to them is written with a QUALIFIER, at all three doors and both\n")
	print("      segment counts: a one-segment and a two-segment qualified TYPE name (`ns3.{NsSharedAliasName}`,\n")
	print("      `ns3.{NsInnerDirName}.{NsDeepAliasName}` — the ranged-alias MIRROR), a ONE-DOT qualified call\n")
	print("      (`parseQualifiedCall`'s door), a TWO-DOT one (`parseDottedPrimary`'s chain WALK), and a\n")
	print("      qualified call as a STATEMENT. Plus the three VISIBILITY tiers on one declaration set: an\n")
	print("      `export`ed alias CONTESTED by every module (so the bare key is ambiguous and the qualified\n")
	print("      key is not), a file-private alias and a file-private free function (which still earns a\n")
	print("      qualified entry — that side does not gate on the tier), and `module function` — the SUBTREE\n")
	print("      tier, named BARE from one directory down, of which this corpus generated exactly ZERO\n")
	print("      before this knob existed.\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  COST DIMENSIONS ON THE DOUBLING LADDER (each DOUBLES per rung; the rung-over-rung RATIO is\n")
	print("  the growth — x2 linear, x4 quadratic — read straight off the counts):\n")
	print("    function count, file count, sequential-if ops, block/CFG count, call-chain depth,\n")
	print("    pressured-loop COUNT, loop-nest DEPTH, locals-in-scope per function (the V dimension),\n")
	print("    float-op groups, float-spill loop COUNT, top-level `let` COUNT, top-level `var` COUNT,\n")
	print("    `type` declaration COUNT (a per-TYPE cost), struct `create` SITE count (a per-SITE cost,\n")
	print("    over a FIXED set of types, so the two move independently), int-divide groups AND\n")
	print("    CHECKED-divide groups (two knobs, because the second costs three BLOCKS per group where the\n")
	print("    first costs none — it was named under CONSTRUCTS and missing from this list until A2a),\n")
	print("    guarded ranged-`return` SITE count — all in ONE function and all returning ONE parameter, so\n")
	print("    the column carries the sites x blocks-in-the-function product a per-site scan would cost,\n")
	print("    `enum` declaration COUNT (per-TYPE) and `match` SITE count (per-SITE, over a fixed pair of\n")
	print("    enums, so a change to tag layout and a change to dispatch strategy move different columns),\n")
	print("    String op-group count, plus FOUR more String knobs — the BYTE/ASCII, StringIndex, TRIM and\n")
	print("    MATERIALIZER families, all per-SITE over a FIXED payload shape, and deliberately NOT all at\n")
	print("    one base: the index family is halved because its four `try` forks per group buy BLOCKS where\n")
	print("    its neighbours buy ops, and the trim family is an EIGHTH because one whitespace-set trim site\n")
	print("    costs 16x a `toUpper`. Both are BUDGETS, not blind spots — every member is generated at every\n")
	print("    rung and every count still doubles,\n")
	print("    CHARACTER op-group count (per-SITE, over a FIXED roster of four\n")
	print("    characters, so it grows how many character sites a program holds and not how many distinct\n")
	print("    characters exist), managed-struct TYPE count (per synthesized __destruct_<T> body),\n")
	print("    trivial-element Array group count and MANAGED-element Array group count (the element-drop\n")
	print("    threading is what separates them), generic BASE count (per shared body) and generic\n")
	print("    INSTANTIATION count (per layout descriptor, over a FIXED base), interface CONFORMANCE count\n")
	print("    — which also doubles the FILE count, deliberately, to keep interfaces-per-file constant —\n")
	print("    and interface DISPATCH SITE count over a FIXED set of conformances,\n")
	print("    BUFFER-SURFACE group count (per-SITE, over the fixed aliases above: it grows how many places a\n")
	print("    buffer is PRODUCED and how many marks one file's mark set holds, against the array-method\n")
	print("    dispatches in that same file that ASK about them — the product both halves of a value-keyed\n")
	print("    regression would cost, which is why they are one file),\n")
	print("    TRY-CO-OWN SITE count (per-SITE, over FIXED instances — one aggregate and one String — so it\n")
	print("    grows how many `try` sites carry a substituted managed result and NOT how many instances\n")
	print("    exist, which is the generic-instances column beside it),\n")
	print("    NAMESPACED-MODULE count (per-NAMESPACE, and the third knob that grows FILES — two per module,\n")
	print("    one of them a level deeper — because a namespace IS a directory and cannot be simulated inside\n")
	print("    one. It doubles the qualified-reference count with it, deliberately: a reference cannot exist\n")
	print("    without a namespace to name, so two knobs would double in lockstep and be one knob twice)\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  NOT GENERATED — a codegen change to any of these is INVISIBLE here (structural blind spot):\n")
	print("    `for`-in over anything but a CHARACTER RANGE — an array, a String's clusters and a bare\n")
	print("    integer range are all still absent, and the character-ops knob is the only `for`-in here.\n")
	print("    Closures and async. Float ARGUMENTS and float RETURNS are absent because they do not\n")
	print("    exist yet (Wave 2 — the xmm0 ABI slot); every float here enters by promotion and leaves\n")
	print("    through trunc, which is the whole of what Wave 1 can express.\n")
	print("    ⚠ AND THREE FAMILIES ARE ABSENT FOR A REASON THAT IS NEITHER 'unimplemented' NOR 'forgotten',\n")
	print("    so it is stated in full: WITNESS-TABLE DISPATCH (a `where T is I` constraint), `Set`, and\n")
	print("    OPAQUE-`T` MANAGED ARRAY ELEMENTS all ride the `funcAbs64InRdata` relocation, which is\n")
	print("    x64-only — and this ladder always compiles for the HOST, because `compileRung` spawns\n")
	print("    `build` with no --target. On arm64/wasm a witness call is a panic(), not a diagnostic, so a\n")
	print("    corpus that generated one would FAIL TO COMPILE and take the whole run down as a BROKEN RUN\n")
	print("    rather than merely skewing a number. Generating them needs host-awareness in the runner\n")
	print("    first, which is a decision, not an oversight.\n")
	print("    The per-type deep CLONER cascade (__clone_<T>, and `noteClonerUsage`'s needs-fixpoint) is\n")
	print("    absent too: nothing here deep-clones a MANAGED-element array, so `neededCloners` stays\n")
	print("    empty and that fixpoint early-outs at every rung.\n")
	print("    ⚠ AND `String.byteAt` IS UNREACHABLE FROM A GENERATED RUNG, for the SAME reason as\n")
	print("    `addressableBytes` below and not for any reason a knob could fix: it is one of the five\n")
	print("    STDLIB-ONLY String members (`module function` in the corpus). Since W49 wave 7 the refusal is\n")
	print("    the corpus declaration's own — E3088 from `SemanticCheck.calleeVisibleFrom` — rather than a\n")
	print("    parser gate on the calling file's location, which changes the diagnostic and not the reach:\n")
	print("    a rung directory is outside `stdlib/` either way. It is named here rather than left out of\n")
	print("    the roster silently, because a member missing from a list of what is covered is exactly the\n")
	print("    defect the four String knobs above exist to end. Its stdlib-only siblings `byteAtOrPanic`,\n")
	print("    `hasSingleByteGraphemes` and `setByte` are absent for the identical reason. User code reaches\n")
	print("    those bytes through `toByteArray()`, which IS generated.\n")
	print("    ⚠ AND TWO OF THE BUFFER SURFACE'S EIGHT PRODUCERS ARE UNREACHABLE FROM A GENERATED RUNG, each\n")
	print("    for a reason that is a property of the DOOR and not of this generator, so no knob can close\n")
	print("    either: a fused wrapper's inline `managed` read (which serves the corpus's own\n")
	print("    `String.addressableBytes()`, and which no rung can declare a fused wrapper to reach), and\n")
	print("    `__ManagedDirectory.filename`/\n")
	print("    `currentPath` lower to the `__md_*` runtime entries, which are x64-windows only — E3104, so a\n")
	print("    rung that generated one would FAIL TO COMPILE on any other host and take the whole run down as\n")
	print("    a BROKEN RUN, exactly as a witness table would.\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  !! THE MEMORY COLUMNS CANNOT SEE A COST THAT ALLOCATES NOTHING. THE CPU COLUMN CAN.\n")
	print("     Allocations and bytes are exact and blind in the same breath: a pass that re-walks tokens or\n")
	print("     chases pointers without allocating reads FLAT in them however quadratic it is in time. That\n")
	print("     is why there is now a THIRD per-phase table — CPU TICKS, the compiling THREAD's own time, so\n")
	print("     preemption and every other process are invisible to it and it can be trended where wall time\n")
	print("     never could. The worked example is IN this corpus: the loop-nest knob drives the loop-phi\n")
	print("     token pre-scan (Parser.blockStatementEndIndex + assignedBindingsIn) to O(depth x tokens) while\n")
	print("     allocating ~one small list per loop, so it reads ~x2 in allocations and is the CPU column's\n")
	print("     to show. It is no longer an example of what this instrument cannot see.\n")
	print("     ⚠ THE CPU COLUMN IS NOT EXACT, AND IS NOT READ LIKE THE OTHER TWO. It moves a few percent\n")
	print("     with turbo, thermal throttling and cache pressure from other cores, so a movement inside that\n")
	print("     band is NOT a datapoint — against the x2-vs-x4 question the ladder asks that band has a 100%\n")
	print("     margin, but against a claimed few-percent constant-factor win it is worth nothing. Its unit is\n")
	print("     platform-defined (TSC ticks on Windows, nanoseconds on POSIX) with no honest conversion, so\n")
	print("     compare RATIOS between rungs, and absolutes only within one platform. Each rung is compiled\n")
	print("     --repeat times and the CPU reading is the MINIMUM of them, since noise can only ADD.\n")
	print("     WALL time is still never collected: it counts every other process on the box and cannot be\n")
	print("     trended at all. For the wall-clock view of ONE compile use `--log=compiler:debug` or\n")
	print("     `--metrics=<path>` — this instrument will not show it.\n")
	print("\n")
	print("  !! AND THE CPU COLUMN CURES ONLY HALF THE BLINDNESS. THE OTHER HALF IS THIS CORPUS, AND NO\n")
	print("     COLUMN CAN CURE IT: every one of the three can only see a cost the generated programs\n")
	print("     actually TRIGGER. A construct this generator never emits, or a knob set too low to reach the\n")
	print("     cost it exists to measure, reads a flat delta of ZERO in allocations, in bytes AND in CPU\n")
	print("     ticks — indistinguishable from a compiler that did not change.\n")
	print("     THIS HAS HAPPENED, TWICE, WHICH IS WHY IT IS PRINTED RATHER THAN FILED: FloatsLivePerSpillLoop\n")
	print("     was 4, which stopped overflowing the callee-saved XMM half the day that half existed, so the\n")
	print("     knob that exists to FORCE a float spill forced none and a genuinely quadratic path read flat;\n")
	print("     and ten consecutive rungs of match / String / Array / generics / interfaces were in the\n")
	print("     subset while this ladder emitted NOT ONE of them, with every rung's log entry recording a\n")
	print("     delta of zero as though it meant something. A zero from an instrument that cannot express\n")
	print("     the feature is not evidence. Check the list above before believing one.\n")
	print("\n")
end 'printCorpusManifest'
